   295	## AFTER MOVEMENT 3 — chapters 13–19, days 278–281 (drafted 2026-10-11, author claude-opus-5-5, NO-SOURCE method)
   296	
   297	### Coordinator rulings (binding)
   298	- **Entry seam with Movement 2 ratified** (Sol plan audit): day 278 at Lowmarch; purse 118; deployment thirty; the papers with Karis; Vastin off the sheet and following; the ferry for people only; the cordage on the western bank **refused as three-strand** — sixty yards of four-strand are still owed to the ferryman.
   299	- **The slip:** *Clearance. The cut below the shoulder hold, on the border road, two days west.* Twenty. "Ours" (Karis), "Ours" (Brom); five hands; Pike's sixth line: *And a healer; of her own road; at her own price.* Brom holds the thumbed slip.
   300	- **Deployment since the Line: THIRTY-TWO**, both on the 279th at the cut: one grain of Ember at the mark (thirty-one; the heat came home along the left arm) and one Wind burst **away**, ten strides, to the north bank inside the wall (thirty-two). Pressure, Anchor, Edge, Storm and the read refused there. Shadow held, twice and sealed; Tide unused; no self-reading; Oryn has not read Cael. **Ten fragments: nothing came of standing two feet from the wall; the eleventh has not come.**
   301	- **The cut (the author's staging stands; BOOK_MAP §8 allows it).** A hundred strides, a stride of ice down the middle, scree on the south, a low north bank, the sink fifty-five yards below it. Eight hounds (five seen, three hidden). The den had learned the road: it runs to where a declaration ENDS. Karis, Oryn and the mule were at the foot; Vastin, a stride behind Brom, stepped out and **declared Iron Wall** — a plane across the road from a line struck with his stick, wall to wall, two feet from Cael, rooted (he may not lift a foot), loud, spending him to the floor. Brom's rock (twice), Lira's knife with the left hand holding, Seln in the heap; five dead; **three went through the rotten ice of the sink and did not come up**. Costs: Brom's right foot (two toes, ten days from the 279th); **Vastin's right knee** (four holes shut by Oryn after "May I?" / "Yes"; the joint bound with laths; **a month** from the 279th: stick, no kneeling, no running, no wall). Karis's sixth line: *It goes to where a declaration ends, if it has learned that the declaring moves.* Twenty paid; **purse 138**.
   302	- **Protected (speakers):** "Carters declare, on the ice." (the factor, unnamed, no pronoun); "It's clear because it's empty, not because it's safe," (Seln — the plan quotes only this clause; the source's fuller sentence contradicts the staging and was not restored); the wall lesson (Vastin) / "You do now," (Cael) / "I know fields…" (Vastin); the log verbatim.
   303	- **The spring (the 280th).** Six rows counted twice by two people. Five columns on every abstract: the day in the registry's reckoning; the station (eight points); the measure; *Instruments* ("Cord true. Edge true. Level true." on every line for six years); the anomaly (*Total*). **Karis matches FOUR sign-groups to the hide by their measures (three alike to the stride, one double) and gives no number of places; no fifth place is described** (held for the 298th). The measures have not changed in six years. The remark *Behaves as device. Cannot be.* stands in a ruled space on the Stair's sheet in the fourth row; **its hand is not identified**; the space for remarks is gone from the next year's printing. **The grade:** a ruled square at the head of every sheet with the same stroke and bar on all four matched places, drawn no larger on the place whose measure is double; in the field it is on stake thirty-two and on the fourth site's thirty-fours only. Karis: a class, unread; "Two copies of one ignorance." Nobody says who set it or why. Karis stopped her lamps sentence with the ruler line and did not finish it.
   304	- **The date.** Seln drew the technician's sheet from the foot of the second row; the row counted a sheet short in Cael's hands; Seln set the sheet before him without a word. **Cael read the date and knew it for the day of his own Kindling** (the first line of his file). Only Cael and Seln know; neither has said or written it; neither knows what the other made of it; nobody has interpreted the sequence. Karis knows both have read the sheet. **No figures are printed, no span is computed, and there is NO second calendar** (the author's stations'-year offset was cut: the series calendar is the owner's). The sheet lies again at the foot of the second row.
   305	- **Vastin's three reflexes** (a directive; a classification; "This has to go up") and **the first thing Seln ever taught anyone:** "You keep drafting orders." / "Habit." / "Out here the habit's the tell." / "Draft nothing. Just look." **Cleon** was named for the first time (Vastin: "Treswick,"; Karis: "The archive under it. Cleon."; Cael: "Who's Cleon?"); OWNER-pending #52 verbatim, as Karis's own rounding.
   306	- **The fork (the 280th evening; the 281st).** Oryn: "I'm not an archive person."; her past (a house for the sick in a city inside the Line; the column for the patient's tier; a woman from the river sheds whom she mended off the sheet; she walked out of a gate and has not been through one since — the author's, kept); "Leaving and routing are different verbs. I'm doing the second."; Seln's "Three weeks"; **the station term, written in the log and read back (verbatim)**; she priced all six, Vastin as long as the rest; "That's on me. It's the one thing I'm walking up that road owing, and I'll pay it." (the reading she has not given Cael); "Bring me whatever walks out of that archive with you. All of it."; "Not the steps," … "Not the square." She went north with the brown mule, her linen and the crossbow with its one bolt: five gates, the ford, Millrace in three weeks.
   307	- **Loads from the fork:** boxes on Brom (good shoulder), Lira (both arms) and Cael; Karis the flat and the hide; **Vastin took up the tarred kit case unasked** and has walked beside Cael since the rise. Brom carries Oryn's empty bowl.
   308	- **The rise (the 281st, near noon):** Treswick a day's walk off — a bluff in a river loop, three walls in tiers, a pale steady light on the crown that Cael does not look at. **Cael laid his hand on the page** (the author's; kept); it stays in its oilcloth in Karis's coat.
   309	- **Teague** was at the landing with forty sacks on the 278th; his term stands; the tall man has he/his. **Names:** none minted. **Length:** about 34,000.
   310	
   311	### Review record
   312	All reads on Sol (gpt-5.6-sol): editorial; cold read; source-distance (3 tracked passages, none dense; probe 2% / 15%); plan / canon / protected-text audit (REPAIR; 102 exact fixes). Coordinator rulings against the audit, after checking the source and the locked Book 7: the cut's staging, the Ember use and the count of thirty-two KEPT (BOOK_MAP §8: private staging the author owns; the audit's 39 re-staging fixes declined); Oryn's past KEPT; Cael's touch of the page KEPT; the slip left as the packet quotes it; "every bar of a fence" KEPT (Book 7 ch58). Accepted from the audit: the six markers' speakers (two filled with the plan's own clause only); the loads for Movement 4; no fifth place or number; no second calendar; the remark's hand unidentified. Eleven fixes applied by the coordinator; the rest recomposed by the author. **Repair r1 in progress with the same author.**
   313	
   314	### New canon minted (author's list)
   315	*(Refreshed after repair r1. Line numbers are those of the repaired chapters.)*
   316	
   317	- **The slip and Pike's rule.** The clearance slip came over in the bag on the 277th; Pike drew a road between two heaps beside it. Twenty, "at the hold's own gate, on the factor's thumb, when the factor's walked it". **Pike will not write a clearance taken until a healer is said** (his own rule since his second year, when one of a crew of four bled for two days on a hurdle). His sixth line: *And a healer; of her own road; at her own price.*, the first column he has had for Oryn.
   318	- **Oryn's round this thaw.** West with the six as the slip's healer; from the fork-stone north to "five gates up along the hill-foot", one with a child that she was to have heard a cold term ago; then "down the far water to the ford, and over, and on to the mill from above". Three weeks puts her at Millrace.
   319	- **Teague.** The forty sacks went aboard on backs on the morning of the 278th; he is still on the east landing when the six cross, bound for the block on the cold road. He had first call on the slip by the cloth and left it. "A man that holds a thing so doesn't leave off because nobody's asked him. I'd know what I had at my back." The word left with Pike: the cut, two days west by the border road, and whatever is beyond it; no day.
   320	- **The ferry and the rope.** On the 278th she crossed in the forenoon with people, led beasts and carried sacks; no wheels. A copper a head, the mule a head. **The broker's cordage on the western landing is three-strand, hawser-laid. Brom refused it.** The sixty yards of four-strand are still owed.
   321	- **The border road.** One of two roads out of the western landing. Alders, then moor; a halting-ground in a fold by a beck, eleven miles out, with a dozen dead rings; a long shoulder of hill; the cut; four miles of high moor to a spring; six more to the fork-stone; a long rise of a mile and more.
   322	- **The four hands.** Flat, palm down: *stand*. A shut fist: *seen*. Two fingers: *come on*. A hand turned over empty: *nothing*. The doctrine in one line: "Nobody declares unless they're meant to be loud. One of us, once, in a place that we've chosen beforehand."
   323	- **Stillhounds, added to Book 7's account.** One paced the column for half a mile in the hour before sunrise and stopped at a beck. **Karis's sixth line (16:119):** *It goes to where a declaration ends, if it has learned that the declaring moves.* Eight laired round the sink, three of them under the north bank where the lip hides four yards.
   324	- **The shoulder hold and its factor.** A foot-track from a cairn below the spur; a plain bar in a wall as thick as a cart; a small clean yard, a long low house, a salting-shed with empty hooks. The factor: spare, grey, a leather apron, a slate on a cord, a sheep-crook, pale eyes; a thumb-mark like a crook; **no name and no pronoun on the page**. Three carts, a carter and two mules lost since the frost.
   325	- **The cut.** A hundred strides, a cart and a man wide, rising east to west by the height of two men. South bank: thirty feet of scree, with the hold's sheep-walk on its lip. North bank: low; beyond it fifty-five yards of scree down to the sink, a round bowl in shadow where water goes into the hill, iced grey and rotten. A seep at the cut's head runs down the middle of the road under ice that never thaws in that shade. Three runs worn over the north bank in the upper half. Three wrecked carts at the foot.
   326	- **Iron Wall as shown.** Named by Vastin (16:109); **no tier is stated**. He steps into the road and brings the stick's foot down once; a line runs from his boots across the cut and cracks the ice white; "the air stood up from it": a face from bank to bank as tall as the scree, seen only by the frost that lies on it. It holds eight hounds at a run. It does not go anywhere; **he may not lift a foot**; each blow on it comes down through him into the stiff leg; it goes out of him steadily; it shuts the road and not the banks. "A Path of the tribunal floor… taught for fields; and a field is ground where a thing has been declared, a hearing, or a square, or a gate with a crowd at it." "I have stood on eleven such."
   327	- **The fight's count.** Brom's rock at the plane's south end; Lira, two-handed, on the hound at Vastin's knee; one broken on the plane in the second run; Seln's knife on one standing in the runnel; Brom's rock on the other. Three over the north bank and through the sink's ice. Seln was on the north bank above the sink when he said that the cut was clear because it was empty.
   328	- **Bodies.** Vastin: four holes and a tear below the right knee, shut by Oryn; the joint bound with linen and two laths; **the bad knee is the right**. Brom: the right boot torn; two toes bruised, bound together, ten days.
   329	- **Oryn's past (kept by the coordinator as written; the order of telling changed in r1).** "I'd my rank off a board at the foot of a bed." A house for the sick "inside a wall", forty beds, six healers; her Iron fourth rank is provisional because she "never stayed to have it confirmed". **A sheet on a board at each bed's foot: name, complaint, what had been spent, and a column for the patient's tier.** In a week of fever the spending went down the column from the top; a woman from the river sheds with no tier went to the bottom of the heap; Oryn went to her at night, off the sheet, and it was enough (**nobody died**); the woman who kept the house said that a healer who spends off the sheet has stolen from whoever stood next on it. Asked where, she will not say: "They keep the same sheet at the foot of every bed inside the Line." She walked out of a gate in the coat she stood in and has not been through one since.
   330	- **The spring.** A furlong south of the road, four miles past the cut: an outcrop as big as a barn, a stone-built basin, and a fallen slab twelve feet by six, waist-high, out of the wind.
   331	- **The abstracts, read in daylight.** Karis counted every row twice and had each counted again; **no figure for any row is printed**. **She gives no number of places.** She sorted the sheets by their sign-groups, matched **four** to the hide by their measures (three alike to the stride, one double), and will not call the unmatched groups places until a sign can be read (17:27–39). **Five columns:** the day, in the registry's reckoning; the station (eight points); the measure; *Instruments*; and the anomaly. No radius of the four has changed by a stride in six years; four surveyors by their figures; three clerks' hands. The fourth column reads *Cord true. Edge true. Level true.* on every sheet. The three oldest rows have a ruled space at the foot headed *Remarks*, empty but once: **the Stair's sheet in the fourth row carries the protected four words, in a hand that is none of the three clerks'; Karis laid the dead man's ledger beside it and will not swear to the hand either way.** From the next year's printing the space is gone. Brom counts the three findings on his fingers (17:61, 17:77, 17:139).
   332	- **Seln's five omissions:** no routing head; no surveyor's name, a number in its place; nothing to say who ordered the survey; no column for a cause; the remarks space struck from the block after the year of the remark.
   333	- **Vastin on the form.** "A survey abstract of the second schedule… the sheet that stands in place of the field book when the book has been called in. The book itself does not survive it." Drawn at the year's end in the office that has the charge of the stations. "A clerk enters what the form has a column for. What it has no column for goes with the book." His two false starts: an order to re-tape each row under two hands, and "restricted matter… under the first article of the closed schedule". **No second calendar: the stations' year and the difference are gone.**
   334	- **The box.** To the right of the four signs and the number, on every abstract, a small printed square with a heading in the unread signs. **On all four of the hide's places it holds the short stroke and the bar, drawn alike**, the double-sized place included. "It is a class." Nobody says who filled it or by what rule. Nothing is said of any other sheet's square.
   335	- **The date.** The registry writes, on the first line of a person's file, after the name and before the bracketed word, the day on which the person was classed; every paper served on Cael began with it. Seln copied that line twice when he was employed at the house above Ostrand. The technician's sheet carries a registry year and a day in it, and it is that day. Seln knew it by the lamp, doubted a date read once over a shoulder in another person's pencil, and read the original a figure at a time in daylight. Cael laid the one date against the other, twice. **No figure, no span, no arithmetic owed to anybody; nothing spoken; nothing written.**
   336	- **The fork-stone and the fold.** A slab as tall as Brom, a worn hand cut on each face. A round drovers' pen of dry stone a little down the northern branch, a quarter of it roofed, with a hearth and flue in the wall and a stack of peat: "You burn what you want, and you cut as much again before you go."
   337	- **The loads from the fork (r1).** Boxes on Brom (left shoulder), Lira (both arms through the beckets) and Cael. Seln carries none: he goes ahead with his hands empty. **The tarred case was left leaning on the fork-stone; Karis stopped forty strides up; Vastin went back for it unasked, after Oryn was out of sight, hooked its strap with his stick without kneeling, and slung it above his sound leg.** He told Cael so at once: "I waited until she could not be put to the trouble of forbidding it a third time." "It is a cord, a mallet and one stake."
   338	- **Seln on the provision:** "A provision of that kind is granted by the week."
   339	- **Treswick from the rise.** A green plain with hedged fields; a river out of the north in loops; a bluff in the nearest loop; three walls in steps; a small pale steady light above the highest roofs. A day's walk off.
   340	- **Roles (all unnamed):** the shoulder hold's factor and "the hold's woman"; the broker and his clerk on the western landing; the keeper of the house for the sick, the Gold with the wrist and the woman from the river sheds in Oryn's account.
   341	
   342	### State at movement end (author; governed by the rulings above and by the surviving prose)
   343	*(Refreshed after repair r1.)*
   344	
   345	**Calendar.** A little before noon on the **two hundred and eighty-first day past the Line**, going down the western side of the long rise, a day's walk from Treswick. Days on the page: 278, 279, 280, 281. No season, month or weekday name; no figures for the technician's date; no count of years since the Kindling.
   346	
   347	**Where everyone is.** Six going down off the rise: Seln in front, Lira, Brom, Karis, and then **Cael with Vastin walking beside him on the ditch side** (from the top of the rise; nobody asked him to and nobody told him no). Oryn and the brown mule went north from the fork at first light and did not look back. Teague's five are east of the river.
   348	
   349	**Cael.** Nineteen. **Ten fragments; no eleventh.** **Deployment thirty-two.** Left palm stung by Ember. He ruled on a job and has made no vow. **He has read the technician's date, laid it against the first line of his own file, and knows it for the day of his own Kindling. He has not written it, has told nobody, and has put no verb to it.** He said "You do now" to Vastin at the hold. He has written in the log, at Oryn's asking, that inside Treswick he does not go near the station. **He has laid his hand on the page once, on the rise, and Karis has it again.** He has seen the light on the crown and turned from it. He carries a box. He asked "Who's Cleon?" and has the name.
   350	
   351	**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. No burst. **Arm: the sixteenth day out of the wool on the 281st.** It held a hound by the scruff while the right hand struck; it votes; **she carries a box on her back with both arms through its beckets.** Oryn: slow for as long again; don't fall on it. **She no longer has the crossbow.** She knows that Cael has put something away and has not asked.
   352	
   353	**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. No grey at any point. Right shoulder still in the older healer's knot (if it tells him anything he is to put the load down and leave it down). **Right foot: two toes bruised, bound; ten days from the 279th, eight left on the 281st.** One box on the left shoulder. He has the purse and Pike's thumbed slip. He lays seven bowls and will carry Oryn's empty.
   354	
   355	**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three; one point set in the peats. She holds the flat (six rows, counted twice by two people and re-taped, the technician's sheet at the foot of the second), the completion report, the page in its oilcloth inside her coat, her copy, the hide, the kit's ledger and her notebook. She knows: four sign-groups matched to the hide and others matched to nothing, **no number given and none of the others called a place**; five columns; no measure changed in six years; the instruments true; the remark, and that she will not swear to its hand; the box, the same on all four; what an abstract is. She cannot read the index or the box. She began a sentence about lamps and stopped it with her rule, verbatim. She heard "I've read it" from Cael as she had from Seln and asked neither. She named the archive and Cleon. Six findings on her stillhound leaf.
   356	
   357	**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. One hound with a short knife; "It's clear because it's empty, not because it's safe". **He has read the original date in daylight, has told nobody, and has set the sheet before Cael without a word; he does not know for certain that it was read.** He has taught for the first time ("Draft nothing. Just look.", to Vastin). **He carries no box.** **The case is on him, shut; no ruling upon it.**
   358	
   359	**Oryn.** Tide; Iron, Rank Four, provisional. On Pike's slip as its healer. She asked Vastin and had an explicit yes; shut four holes; bound the joint; two fingers on his wrist. She has priced all six and given Vastin as long as any. **She has not read Cael**: "It's the one thing I'm walking up that road owing, and I'll pay it." She has told the house, the column and why she left, and will not say where. She has her term in his log and has read it there. Gone north with the mule, her linen, and her crossbow with its one bolt; five gates, the ford, then Millrace in three weeks. She does not know that Vastin is carrying the kit's case.
   360	
   361	**Vastin.** No office; **no age stated**. **Iron Wall declared once, on the 279th, in the cut**; spent to the floor. **Right knee: bitten, shut, bound stiff; the 281st is the second day of thirty**; on the stick; no kneeling, running or walls. He qualified every instruction. **He has not sat in front of the crew since the cut.** **He carries the tarred kit case (cord, mallet, stake thirty-two) on the shoulder above his sound leg, taken up unasked at the fork-stone after Oryn had gone, and has told Cael so.** **He walks beside Cael from the top of the rise.** He said the town's name at the spring, the first to say it since the page. He knows the four hands, the doctrine and where a post stands. He has heard the daylight reading whole. **He does not know that the date means anything to anybody**, the mechanism, what Cael carries, the instruments' behaviour in Cael's hand, or what is under the bar; nothing has been said of a directive or an objection. He has not asked to join and nobody has spoken of it.
   362	
   363	**Teague and his crew.** On the east bank; salt going over; then the cold road. He knows the six went west to the cut "and what's beyond it", and that Vastin walks behind them. His term is "where I left it".
   364	
   365	**Pike's book.** Eleven completed lines. The clearance is taken (five strokes and a healer's line) and not yet said closed; **the thumbed slip is in Brom's shirt**. The season line's number still blank. Eleventh on the cloth.
   366	
   367	**Objects and money.** **A hundred and thirty-eight marks.** The reserve sealed. **Owed: sixty yards of four-strand rope to the ferryman.** Three boxes on Brom, Lira and Cael. The kit's case with Vastin. Stake thirty-two never driven. The cord and both edges untouched by Cael since the 168th. Hesk's book has the term on the 280th's leaf and no line for the date.
   368	
   369	**The Quieting, as known.** On paper: the four places of the hide, measured for six years with no change; instruments true inside; one word in the fifth column; one surveyor's remark, and the space for remarks removed afterward; a class mark that is the same on all four. Other sign-groups in the rows, unmatched and uncounted on the page. In the field: unchanged.
   370	
   371	**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six, as before. The daylight reading: the six at the slab, and Oryn by hearing. **The date: Cael and Seln, each alone.** Nobody has interpreted the sequence, said who classed the places or why, or joined the remark to anything or anybody.
   372	
   373	**Words said aloud for the first time.** *Iron Wall* (Vastin, 16:109). The town's name, for the first time since the page (Vastin, 18:215). *Cleon* (Karis, 18:219).
   374	
   375	**Open threads.** A date that two people know and neither has said. "All of it." Sign-groups that are not yet places. A box that nobody can read. A lamps sentence stopped. A knee on its second day, and a case on the shoulder above the other. A reading that a healer owes. Two sentences in a log about a flight of steps and a paved place. A rope of the wrong lay. A question that Cael kept from Vastin at Lowmarch, still kept.
   376	
