   685	### Coordinator rulings (binding)
   686	- **Entry seam with Movement 6 ratified** (Sol plan audit): twelve fragments; deployment thirty-two; purse 130; the loft and handcart to the 295th; the leaf's back unread; Vastin's knee day fourteen on the 293rd; Karis's stitched palm; Brom's arms; Lira's days out of wool; sixty yards of rope owed.
   687	- **The 294th: the list of parts.** Cleon turned the skin between boards; its back is paler, scored and not inked, **nine short lines, a thing and its number to each**. Karis can spell all nine and turn four (the four things the face says were made); the other five are spelled and unturned, and **she has said none of the numbers aloud**. **The fourth line is the mark's:** the word for the mark is what bears; two runs hang from it LEVEL (those who are kindled; and a six-sign word in the scraped-thin place), and after both the number *three*. Cleon made a second copy, drawn and not read, and gave it to Karis ("Your hand's better than mine on the fourth line."). The fifty-two sheets were counted into three boxes under two hands, K. and V. The clerk wrote *Withdrawn by its bearer* and *four* and ruled the account off. Protected, Cleon's, at the iron door: "Work. Not a finding." / "Mind the stair. It leans." **He is not above ground and did not look at Cael.** This edition has no noun "bearer" for a sign (signs bear, are carried, or are nothing).
   688	- **The road east (the 295th–297th).** The rope: **sixty yards of four-strand, two marks (purse 128)**, bought at the walk under Treswick's wall and paid to the Lowmarch ferryman, who waived the fare; the debt is closed. Out by the outer arch: six out as six in, none written. **The bridge: five piers counted; the crown not looked at** ("the promise held to the gate and is done; the not looking was mine"). The spring, with the wind in their faces. The fork-stone passed.
   689	- **How Vastin travelled (ruled against the audit).** After the alley his knee would not carry him a day's road: **he rode the rope-walk dray's tail-board for three days at a copper a day of his own, got down for every hill, and walked the whole cut**; on the 298th Teague put him on a salt cart ("It's owed a seat. For him. I'll not walk ten at the pace of one."). **From the ford on the 299th he walks in the file.** The packet's "walked again on a bad knee" is met by the hills and the cut; closed Movement 6 (a rope down a ladder, a stall, a handcart, a stair on his seat) governs the rest.
   690	- **The shoulder hold.** The factor offered twenty at each first frost to the five; Brom declined ("We're walking east"). Protected: "He said you'd say that." — **"he" is Teague**, who took the salt up himself, asked the factor twice how long the wall stood and whether its maker moved, and built his term on the answer. (The draft's offer of a seat at the hold's fire for Vastin was CUT: where he belongs is not this book's to open.)
   691	- **The cut revisited (the 296th).** Protected, **Vastin's**: "Here."; he stood on the bound leg once with the stick off the ground; Brom, low: "Held,"; "Two words," … "Both of them ours." (Vastin's, one speech — the two words are "Here" and "Held"); Cael: "Noted" — the first time he has given that word to this man. **A horseman kept a mile behind from the cut to the dark; a riderless horse stood in the alders on the western bank on the 297th.** No face, no name; Seln "can put it no higher". Nothing was seen east of the river.
   692	- **Lowmarch (the 297th).** Pike's twelfth line is the clearance (*Eight. Twenty; paid.*); the thirteenth, protected: *Treswick. Survey read. Bearing.*, with price and outcome left empty and said to be empty. **Cael read it the right way up, from Pike's side of the bench.** Pike put a DAY against the season line (nothing printed; "Eleventh. And dated."). **Teague's term, taken as a contract** (protected: "Ten of us on a wall's count. Your six and my four." … "Contract. Not more than that. I'll not have it called more." / "The wall's still the wall." — all Teague's): **ten on the count — Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis, Seln, Vastin; Teague, the tall man with the spear, the bow, the young one; NEITHER healer**; shares and costs by the count; **the field is Teague's** (Cael: "It's yours. I'd have asked you to take it."); the reading is Karis's ("You'll be asked."). The wall has a month on it and is not stood before the month is out. No hand was offered; the cloth still reads first and eleventh.
   693	- **Teague (cutaway).** He is going "to be at the front of the thing" and says so. He knows that a factor saw an Iron Wall and that its maker "had been somebody … and had left off being it"; **nothing of the mechanism, the fragments, the leaf, the list, the date, or who Vastin was.** The bow (a broad man with a bad hip), the tall man and the older healer (a lean old woman with a staff and a board, "not counted … not told where to stand") have not spoken to the six. The young one "does what he's told a little too soon".
   694	- **Force SEEN, not acquired (the 298th, first light).** Teague lined heel, knee, hip, shoulder and palm from an ash root to a sunk salt cart and put it a body's length; the recoil came back down the same line to the wrist (a click heard at twenty strides); the young one's cloth; the old woman's board. Protected, Teague's: "Don't put it in front of anything that needs doing twice." **Nothing came. Twelve. Thirty-two.** The two crews walked the meadow in file to four hand-signs (*stand*, *seen*, *come on*, *nothing*); no drill under a wall.
   695	- **The march order (Teague's):** Seln ahead; the tall man; Teague, **with Cael at his left shoulder a stride back**; Lira; the young one (watching her feet); Karis, the healers and the mule in the middle; Brom; Vastin a stride behind him; the bow last.
   696	- **THE GRADE (the 298th).** By laying Cleon's drawn copy on her own, Karis has four of the six signs: the word is ***set***. The fourth line: the mark, over those who are kindled and what is set; three. **Three marks:** a stroke alone (a box ruled and EMPTY — no sheet carries it; no first-tier site appears); a stroke with one bar (stake thirty-two, the dead surveyor's book, every sheet of the four places); a stroke with two bars (six sheets of a FIFTH place). Protected, Karis's: "A mark is a grade. The one they give a boy at fourteen says what he's *for*." … "The one on a sheet says how *old* the thing is."; "Somebody laid them down in order"; "It's observation. I'm telling you what the paper says. Not what it means." **Ages, each counted from the office's first sheet for the place: the Fallow sixty-one, the Stair ninety-four, the Hall a hundred and thirty-eight, the place behind the iron two hundred and six**; the fifth place has two unread signs where its figure should be ("older than two hundred and six", no figure). The paper says how long ago a first sheet was drawn and nothing of who walked there since. **Vastin's first "Noted."** Protected, Teague's: "Oldest last." … "Then that's where we're going." Hesk's dividers were **opened by Karis on Cael's loan** ("They're lent. I'll not open them."): the office's run from the fourth place to the fifth is "three days past the iron, a little less"; its compass point and its figure in lengths are unsaid. Stake thirty-two is back in the tarred case, never driven.
   697	- **The log of the 298th** (protected, whole, once; the grandmother is Cael's, Hesk's late wife): "Karis cracked the grade … Turns out the line was already drawn four hundred years ago. We're just the first to follow it to the end." **"Four hundred" stands nowhere else in the movement**; the centuries and the figure are Cael's own inference, put on his own page.
   698	- **Millrace (the 298th evening).** "Eleven off the ridge." (the woman with the net, as in Book 7) / "I know." (Oryn). Oryn to Vastin: "Stand there."; he stood an hour without a clause. Protected: "Nineteen days," (Vastin) / "Then it's eleven more." (Oryn). Oryn's terms: three holds owed yet on her round; "I'm a stop on my own route."; on nobody's count. "Sixth chair." (Lira) / "On my terms. Which are the only kind." (Oryn); she sat. **Readings, each asked first:** Brom's LEFT shoulder mended (a quarter-hour; she was grey), the right re-tied shorter by the older healer ("That'll do"); Lira's arm ("You listened when it told you the second time." / "Once." / "Once is once more than I priced." — "It's an arm."); Karis's palm ("Who sewed this." — the seven stitches drawn; "Write. Don't lean."); Vastin's knee — he told her of the alley wall himself; "It held in a cut," (Oryn); new linen and two laths; **the month stands as counted**; "Noted," (Vastin) / "That's new." (Oryn) / "It's a useful word." (Vastin) / "It's his." (Oryn). **Purse 127** (meal and a flitch, one mark).
   699	- **What Oryn was told before she read him:** the two self-readings of the 281st and why; **the eleventh** (Vastin's, in the stair; he does not know); **the twelfth** (from "a man under the registry"; made things only; "Not a man."). Her ruling: nothing is turned "on my plank"; the road is his own.
   700	- **THE DEEP READING.** "May I?" / "Yes." Forty minutes, to her own floor; she found the eleventh ("stands in the stream like a post") and the twelfth ("a little flat thing behind your eyes") unprompted; her current went round the still place from every side. **At about the three hundredth breath the twelfth turned inward, unbidden: *Still.* Then *Set.*** He kept his jaw and hands loose and did not let the next word come. Oryn felt only "Something turned over … out at the edge of you. One of yours, in its sleep," and dismissed it. **Her finding, protected, whole:** "There's a place in you I can't read because it isn't *flow*. I've spent my life reading flow. Whatever that is, it's not the kind of thing my Path was built for." **The two findings do not agree, and the two words are told to NO ONE** — not Oryn, not Lira. Lira offered the leaf; protected: "Is it a finding?" / "It's a leaf" … "Then it's not mine." … "Tell me on the road, if there's road. Not at my table." … "Lie still when I say. That's still the term." **Oryn has not heard the leaf.** The eyes paid in the dark. Lira knows that something unasked came and has said that she will wait; the log gives four reasons for writing nothing ("Four is not mine to write") and the ruling *hold*. **Cleon's warning "has begun to lean".**
   701	- **Dawn, the 299th.** "Twelve," … "And a mule." (Seln). The mule carries the three boxes, the flat, a sack of meal, Oryn's pannier and canvas. Karis pinned a clean sheet to the hide's eastern edge, pricked the divider's point on it, and ruled the pencil line from the fourth ring through the point and a hand's breadth past ("I am not going to draw a stop that the paper does not give me").
   702	- **Bodies at dawn on the 299th.** Vastin: **day twenty of thirty**, Oryn's linen and two laths, stick, walking. Brom: both arms up to the shoulder. Lira: the arm released. Karis: stitches out, linen on, not to lean. Teague: the right wrist in linen under the leather. Cael: sound.
   703	- **Money:** 130 → 128 → **127 marks**; the reserve sealed in the third box. **Names:** none minted. **Length:** about 32,000 against 32,000.
