All of this is mine and is flagged for ruling.

- **The calendar of the leg, laid against Book 7 (47–49).** The 299th: the Stair's brow a little after noon; camp at a beck a mile short of the halting-ring. The 300th: the road's swing at noon; the track's end above the hall's marsh at dusk. The 301st: the east road; two living holds; the dead hold at dusk. The 302nd: the heap of chock-stones two hours after sunrise; the ridge by noon; the seep in the afternoon; a hollow on the ridge's back. The 303rd: the ridge; the brow at dusk. Stair to hall in a day and a half of hard walking against Book 7's two days and a morning at a survey's pace (said on the page at 47:253).
- **The cairn (47:197–201).** At the bend where the carters' road swings south to the hold, a built cairn as high as a man's hip marks a mule-path that goes north over a saddle to "three gates"; it is not Book 7's heap of chock-stones, which is passed on the 302nd (48:109–117).
- **Oryn's reason and her word north (47:237–249).** Brom's shoulder, shut on the 298th, wants a week of her eye (the 305th); the knee on her linen; an edge twice the size with ten going at it. She sent "a week, or more" up the water from Millrace with the miller's man before the eleven came down the notch. She is counted from the cairn in each log (one day on the 301st … five on the 305th).
- **The hold south of the cairn (47:205–233).** Four marks at its gate for two new water-skins, meal, a cheese and corn for the mule: **123**. Teague's four buy their own (a side of bacon). The keeper had skins filled and priced since the day before: a man on a bay rode through two days earlier, bought nothing, gave no name, and said that a crew would want them.
- **The Stair's bowl on the 299th (47:35–49; 47:53–81; 47:131).** Karis's grey stone is gone from between the two irons and a square of turf has been laid in its place with a spade, since the thaw; Seln: "There is one to a year." She sets another stone. Her number: the dead surveyor's *North station to floor*, nine lengths and a piece from the old iron to the grey's lip, which is also on the Stair's sheet in each of six years; laid with the kit's own cord from Vastin's case, by Karis, Lira and Seln, in an hour and ten minutes; "I am not checking the place; I am checking the cord." Cael does not touch it (a hundred and thirty-one days). Teague's three hold the rim unasked, the tall man on the spot where he stood in Book 7.
- **Teague's two lessons.** The brow (47:83–127): "A piece of ground's a sum … you put down what a man needs, and it comes out as a place"; the bow goes on the short walk and the legs go far; the captain stands where a voice carries to the other three. The dead hold (48:65–97): he sits down beside Cael a second time; "Tell me what I've seen as well"; **three things worth a fist** — a thing out of its place; a thing stopped that ought to be moving; a thing moving that ought to be stopped. Cael gives him "Noted" there (48:93).
- **The boy and Lira (47:153–181).** He has had a sigil since he was fourteen and has never been without it; carters say that in such a place "it's like being a child again"; her account: the last step of a stair in the dark; it has never come back short; "Don't find out on a morning when somebody's shooting at you." On the first dry night he wrings the cloth for Teague's wrist out of his own cup of water and goes to his blanket dry (told by Lira, 49:15–19).
- **Vastin on the leg.** He finds for himself how a stick keeps a file (47:11–13). At the hall's rise: "I have put my name to a good many papers which said that such a thing could not be … I am glad to have been spared the sight until I had left off signing" (47:265–271). His stick and the older healer's staff fall into one beat (48:23). At noon on the 303rd he says before he is asked that he will not stand a wall on day twenty-four, and that he does not know what he is for without it (49:33–49); Lira: "You can stand."
- **The country east of the marsh (48:5; 48:9–39).** Every beck goes into the ground within a mile. Both living holds on the east road have their gates barred at noon and no head on the walk; the second lets down one bucket for the mule on a rope, for a copper. The dead hold's well is ten feet of steining on dry rubble: "That's what kills them, out here."
- **The seep (48:131–143; 48:147–209; 48:213–285; 48:289–311).** A fold in the ridge's southern flank; a band of dark shale across it; a weeping ledge and a trodden pan under the band's western end; nineteen shale-backs, four of them calves. Teague: they lick the wet at first light and at dusk and mow along the band between; a Force shifted a block under a ledge herd on the cold road in Pike's time and "they found two mules" (this is Book 7's tale, with Teague now saying that he has stood where it was done). His rule for the fold: in by the west along the band's foot, never on the fall of the hill under a beast, two at a time, three steps and a stand, nobody steps quick near a calf. A skin takes more than six minutes at the drip. **The tall man stops the boy with a flat hand on the breastbone (48:197–209).** The read finds a bucket of water behind a loose leaf of shale; Pressure is wanted and refused; Brom draws the leaf with both arms; the tall man lifts his free hand from his knee and brings it down once. The old cow stands over them for the last skins. The mule drinks four skins from Oryn's canvas; the tall man and then the boy fetch four more at dusk; the bow takes one skin out of the boy's hand. Seln finds a keg's hoop and five made heels at the pan, the newest a week old: "a posting sends for its water."
- **The name (49:77–83).** Teague has had it from three carters on a salt road two days north that looks down into the place from far off: a hole where the country's water goes, and a span over it, sunk in the dish. "The Sunken Span." He calls the hole "the sink".
- **The fourth place's ground (49:85–97; 49:127–145; 50:3–9; 50:155; 50:231; 51:203–207; 51:261–267).** A dry ravine comes down from the north; for a hundred yards it runs along the outside of the fence, and the level strip between its lip and the irons is the shelf (a cart wide; the drop three men deep on the left hand going east; the irons on the right; the line a stride past them). Two squared timbers on trestles at the shelf's narrowest, forty yards in from its mouth; behind them five strides of width, a hut of turf and stone with a blanket for a door, a fire, a keg, a hide weighted with stones; at the east end the ravine bends inward and its lip comes to within a stride and a half of the last bar; beyond that gap a trodden path a pace wide goes on round the fence. The ravine cuts the fence there (the bars stop on one lip and begin on the other, twelve yards off) and runs to the middle. **The Span:** two piers of the grey rising out of the ravine's rock walls, with no course and no join, and an arch in one piece; on its back the grey comes up out of the turf a cart wide, with no kerb and no step, rises to a crown and goes down into the grass again; no wheel has marked it. Twenty yards beyond the arch the ravine's bed ends in a round hole as wide as a cart is long, with no bottom to be seen.
- **The watch (49:95–111; 49:223–255; 50:9–27; 50:131–135; 50:219–225; 50:293–299).** Seven, hooded, faces tied even at night among themselves: two crossbows, four shod staves, one empty-handed practitioner whose Path is not named; he makes a point of white light the size of a fist and hangs it over the shelf, and calls, "This ground is posted. Stand where you are, and declare." He keeps the ravine's side of the shelf always and never comes within three yards of the bars. One of the seven has a wet cough; one walks the path two hundred paces east and back. Leaving, they take the broken bow and leave the sound one on the baulk, with a stave and their fire.
- **Seln's reading of the iron (49:187–219).** Three ages in the fence: most bars scaled like the Stair's old stake; here and there a newer one driven into an old hole; and every thirty or forty paces a thicker bar under a heavier mallet's head, whose scale "had scaled again". All three kinds carry thirty-four with the stroke and bar. The path is a groove a hand deep in the shale. He thinks of the sheets that a posting of seven needs, and of "that house's hands"; the case is on his breastbone, shut; nothing about it is altered. "That made eight, with himself, for fifteen years."
- **The fight's choreography inside the plan's four exchanges (50).** Positions: Seln in the grass above the path sixty yards east of the gap; the healers and the mule at the old ruts; nine at the shelf's mouth with Brom in front. *One:* two bolts on Iron under a buttoned coat — the breastbone and the left forearm; the neck and hands go "the colour of a slate that is drying after rain"; no sound. *Two:* Teague walks, since the Force wants three breaths of stillness; Cael keeps his left with the shortened watchman's stave from the alley (Seln's, handed over behind the brow) and takes the third stave across the left collar-bone without giving the stride; the tall man keeps the right with the ash crosswise and the steel high; both timbers and their trestles go a body's length back in one piece; five hoods go down; a crossbow's stock breaks under the lower baulk; nobody is under a timber; the wrist clicks and then the elbow. *Three:* the watch reforms behind the timbers where they lie, one bow spanned, the light relit; Teague cannot shut his right hand; he sees the width past the iron and asks Karis whether it is ground. Cael over first ("Grass"); Lira a stride in and a stride back ("Gone." / "Back. All of it." — "It's the same"); Karis; the boy when Teague looks at him; Vastin ("I am forbidden a wall. I am not, I think, forbidden to stand."). Sixty strides, two paces inside the iron; the watch's bow cannot turn without giving its side to Brom, the spear and the broad man's crossbow; the empty-handed man hauls back by the collar the one stave who starts for the iron. *The pinch:* eleven counts; at four the watch's bowman looses at the broad man, the bolt takes his crossbow in the stock, his hip goes and a yard of rotten lip goes with it; Teague checks for half a stride and does not turn; the boy looks at Lira's boots and stays; at eleven the first stave is dropped and seven go through the gap singly. Seln counts seven through the gap and again over the second rise, a mile on (51:11).
- **How the thirteenth comes (50:117–129).** Up through his own heels, out of the same stone, to the right hand, where it finds nothing to be laid against; "It lay along him from the heel to the palm, as a prop lies under a beam before the wedges are driven. Then it listened." It does not ask. It attends to whatever braced thing stands in front of him (the timbers; the ravine's lip; the grey of the Span's crown; a slab on the shale), and the old three ribs, low on the left, ache where its road would leave him.
- **Costs carried forward.** The broad man's right shin (the good leg), broken in the ravine's bed; drawn straight on the older healer's board, knit by Oryn in a quarter of an hour, bound to the board in four turns of webbing; he walks on a trestle's leg from the 305th. Teague's right arm, wrist and elbow, in linen to the shoulder on a slat; he cannot feel the fingers. Brom: a round bruise the size of a saucer on the breastbone and a ridged one along the left forearm; Oryn will not spend on them ("You'll have a week of colours"). Cael: a welt across the left collar-bone, "a bruise on a bone", ten days. A crossbow with a bolt standing in its split stock.
- **After the fall (50:307–367; 51:3–75).** The boy runs when Teague lifts two fingers and holds the broad man's head off the stones. Oryn asks; a nod; "I don't go in on a nod. I'll have it in a word."; the word is given and does not carry up the rock. The two healers nod, once each, across the leg. Vastin, coming out over the iron: "It is all there … To the grain." Brom carries the broad man's pack as the contract's second head ("I can carry what was on his back") and leaves three coppers of his own on the watch's keg for a skin of water. Cael to Lira: "Thirteenth. His. A line." / "It listens. I'd have liked it better if it asked." The tier in the fragment's lines is the thing that he once declined to read off that wrist at a marsh's edge.
- **Teague's window (51:79–175).** He counts three by breathing and the spear by a shadow at the gap. The elbow has gone once before, "a long while ago, on a ledge". The bow to him: "And you never turned your head." / "If you'd turned, I'd have had something to say to you from the bottom of that hole"; "It's the good one"; of Oryn, "She asked me first. With my leg lying two ways." The boy asleep sitting, the dry cloth in his fist. Teague's reckoning: the boy "would go wherever he was looked at, a half-beat before he had seen what was there", and held at the grass by a girl's boots; "He would have to think about whose boots a boy watched." Of Cael: a left side kept with a stick and nothing else; "Teague could put that in a column." **Under the hide:** a sack and a half of meal, a bundle of bolts with no thread on their feathers, a mallet, a tarred cord knotted every few feet, and a dozen new bars, never driven, each stamped with two figures and the stroke and bar. He does not read it further; "It was not his to read."
- **Noon at the Span (51:179–241).** Oryn at the irons, two fingers on Brom's wrist, asked first: "Four. The same." She will not be told how many "across a row of iron". Six go down: Karis, Lira, Brom, Seln, Vastin, Cael. The twelfth leans at the whole of the thing and is refused by counting bootlaces. On the crown: nothing; the thirteenth listens to the grey; the ribs.
- **The notch (51:245–259).** Lira sees it from the crown: a gap low in the second ridge, square; "The brass goes through it. I'd lay my knife." Seln looks at it longer than either.
- **The piers' inner faces (51:261–327).** Reached from the ravine's bed by a rib of rock on the western side; five courses to the height of a man, in the floors' hand, unworn; Karis draws nine sheets in two hours on a board of Brom's, with a tick where each run ends. **The lowest course of the west pier has fifty-two signs; Cael counts fifty-one, twice, and fifty-two with Lira's finger beside his** (the Book 11 plant; ruling *hold*, 51:351). The twelfth takes that course for a breath: *Goes*, eastward, out of the face toward the other pier. Karis can turn one group in the second course (marker 8). She keeps one of the new bars from under the hide, by Teague's leave (51:333).
- **The 304th's log (51:335–353).** After the fixed text Cael sees that he has written *tomorrow* for three days and lets it stand: "It was not a true count; it was the truth about how near the thing had come."
- **The 305th's road (52:3–37).** The tall man cuts the broad man's stick out of the watch's trestle. The file goes round the outside of the iron by the trodden path; from the far side the road on the Span's back lies end-on and the same line runs out to the gap. A captain's pace "that a child could have kept"; two sticks at the back that stop within a breath of one another and never look round. Stepped shale with a thin red moss and no water. Camp in the middle of the afternoon under a step a man's head high, with forty yards of flat before it and the gap four or five miles off. Seln goes on alone.
- **The blanket (52:41–101).** Brom's blanket, twenty yards upwind of the fire. In the middle the hide, turned until its pencil line runs at the gap, the Ternhall brass upon it; one sheet from the Fallow, three from the Stair, one from the hall and the nine of the piers, each turned to lie as its wall stood (she had noted on every sheet which wall and which way it faced); the flat at the head with its six rows; her notebook at the list, Cleon's roll and the sheet of three boxes at the foot; the fair copy from Cael's coat at one corner, folded, under a pebble, not opened. **The lean:** kneeling to hold a corner, Cael has the twelfth take all fourteen sheets in a breath: *Goes*, every run, one way, along the brass, east. He tells Karis, Lira and Brom (Vastin is at the fire). Karis enters it as his, as one unchecked glance, and will not ask for a second: "whoever cut them cut each one to go on to the next … I have only his word for it."
- **The corner (52:105–157).** The two signs in the corner of the two-bar sheets are two of the twenty-three letters: the first letters of a year's number-words, as the old town's clerks headed a page when short of room; an office's clerk has copied them forward "as he would copy a blot". They are the same two letters as the year at the head of the skin; "the same hundred"; the figure is to be said once, at night, when the reduction is done. **The thumbnail ring** goes round the prick; "I shall ink that when I have had a cord round it." Vastin reads the oldest row standing ("I have never read a paper standing that I did not afterward wish I had read sitting").
- **The lamps finding's ground (52:161–221).** A ruled table kept since the Fallow: who crossed, what each carries, of what rank, and the stride; three ranks of Iron, a Copper, a Bronze, and since the 304th the boy and "a man who signed for other people's for forty years"; she has asked six people beside herself. The same stride for every one, whole, no dimming, back whole. After Vastin's stopped sentence and her rule she tears the ruled leaf out and carries it the length of the blanket to the corner farthest from the folded paper: "Two pages … Whoever wants a line from the one to the other will have to find something to rule it with." **Cael's third page** (52:213–221): the technician's sheet lies at the foot of the second row; he does not know to which corner it would lie the nearer, and keeps it off the wool. Nothing is said or written.
- **The two slips (52:225–285).** Folded small and pinned under the knot of the oldest row's tape; a wafer of red wax on the directive and a broader, nearly black one on the objection, with different impressions. Vastin knows both seals at a glance and will not name them ("I should have drafted nine. I can feel all of them."). Karis pins them back where they were and enters the day, the place, and "read aloud and understood in part".
- **One fire (53:3–71).** Wood for one, carried by the boy from the watch's stack; seven bowls and five. Vastin moves the foot of his stick a hand to the left: "I am informed that the ground falls away on that side." The two sticks stand in one crack. **The healers' third exchange:** the older woman holds two turns of her own webbing apart and Oryn's hands go in under them. Teague to Cael: "You kept my left … I never had the place behind my shoulder stay so full."
- **Force, dry (53:75–151).** Behind the step's corner: the right heel on a rib of rock, the right palm on a fallen slab, a body's length apart; the line found by moving the hip a finger's breadth; the thing "standing, as a prop stands when the weight has come onto it"; the ribs give "one clear line of warning … such as a cracked board gives". Three reasons for not letting it go: never fired, and a camp forty yards off; the ribs unread for this; its owner round the corner, not told. Lira: "You looked like him." Brom has the read and the thirteenth said at last: "you'll pay it when it can be paid, and not a day sooner."
- **Karis's night (53:155–229).** The reduction leaves eleven years in one hundred, of which the skin's year is the fourth; "*not shown to be the same year; shown to be one of eleven, of which that year is another.*" Being believed is heavier than not being believed. The ruled leaf and her copy of the skin lie on the lid with a hand's breadth of bare wood between; she knows that a sentence exists and that it is six words long, as she once knew the length of a word under a glove; she covers the wood with her bound hand, does not say it, and writes: "*Second page: nothing entered. Not for want of a pencil.*"
- **Seln on the gap (53:233–261).** A gap in a ridge, square because both walls are rock; a dry bed going in; the only way through for five miles on either hand; he lay a mile off until dark and saw no fire, no person and no heel-mark. "A watch that I can count is a watch that I can plan for." Teague will not move a stick until he has looked again from nearer at first light.
- **Roles (all unnamed):** the keeper of the hold south of the cairn; the man on a bay; the people of the two barred holds; the seven of the watch (the empty-handed man; the two bowmen; the four staves, one with a cough); Teague's tall man, bow (the broad man), boy (the young one) and old woman; the miller's man (spoken of).

## State at movement end (for the ledger)

**Calendar.** The night of the **three hundred and fifth day past the Line**, on a shelf of shale under a step, four or five miles short of a square gap in the hills to the east, a day's walk past the fourth place. Days on the page: 299 (the ridge; the Stair's brow; the beck), 300 (the cairn at noon; the track; the hall's marsh at dusk), 301 (the east road; the dead hold), 302 (the chock-stones; the ridge; the seep; the hollow), 303 (the ridge; the brow at dusk; the fold; Seln's night), 304 (the shelf at first light; the ravine's bed; the hut; the Span at noon; the watch's fire at dusk), 305 (round the fence; the shale; the blanket; one fire; the step; Karis's lamp; Seln's return). No season, month or weekday name; no figure for the technician's date; no count of years since the Kindling.

**Where everyone is.** Twelve and a mule in one camp under the step, at one fire in two halves: Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis, Seln, Vastin; Teague, the tall man, the broad man, the young one, the older healer; Oryn and the brown mule. Seln is back from the gap and is to look again at first light; nobody moves until he has. Cleon is under Treswick. Pike is at Lowmarch. The watch's seven went east along the perimeter path on the morning of the 304th and have not been seen since.

**Cael.** Nineteen. **Thirteen fragments. Deployment since the Line thirty-three** (thirty-two, and the read at the seep on the 302nd). The thirteenth (Force-adjacent, from Teague, at the timbers, at a stride) is unused; he has lined it dry once and taken his palm away; he knows that its cost would come out at the old three ribs, low on the left, and has that to tell Oryn "when she'll hear it". **Teague is not told**; the debt is entered beside the first (Vastin's). He has told Lira; Brom has it from her. The twelfth has taken two unbidden looks (the west pier's lowest course; the blanket) and been refused twice; never inward; never on a person. **The two words are still told to no one.** He stood on the Span's crown at noon on the 304th: nothing. He kept a captain's left for three breaths with a stick and opened nothing; he stood inside the line at the pinch with the eleventh asking and the thirteenth listening and told both no. He drew the shelf for Teague and asked one term of his field. He counted a course of fifty-two as fifty-one, twice (ruling *hold*). He has not put the date on any page; he has not written his own third page. He has heard Karis fix the point and say the figure (marker 10). He has not touched the cord (a hundred and thirty-seven days on the 305th) or opened the dividers. Body: a stave's bruise on the left collar-bone, ten days from the 304th; the ribs talk when the thirteenth has ground; eyes paid on the night of the 304th and to be paid for the blanket.

**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. No burst. The arm is an arm. She read the herd beside Teague, walked Cael in and out under it, went a stride over the fourth line and back ("It's the same"), stood in the row of five, and did not move her feet when the broad man fell. She coaches the boy and is watched by him. She knows the thirteenth and that Teague is not told; she saw the dry stance. She does not know the two words.

**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. **He went grey at the shelf on the 304th and took two bolts** (breastbone; left forearm): two bruises, a week of colours, not mended. Both arms to the shoulder; he drew a leaf of shale with them at the seep and broke a rafter at the dead hold. He carries the broad man's pack on the mended shoulder. **The purse: a hundred and twenty-three.** He laid three coppers of his own on the watch's keg. His week under Oryn's eye ended on the 305th.

**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three; no point struck. She has: the cord's figure at the Stair confirmed; nine sheets of the piers' inner faces; one word turned on the west pier (marker 8); the lean of fourteen sheets, entered as Cael's and unchecked; the two corner signs read as an old-style year, lying among eleven years of one hundred of which the skin's year is one; the point ringed with a thumbnail; **the lamps finding**, said aloud and filed on a leaf torn from her notebook, which is now in the back of that notebook with the line "Second page: nothing entered. Not for want of a pencil."; the two slips entered as present, under two unlike seals, unnamed. She has said the figure once (marker 10). She holds one new bar numbered thirty-four with the stroke and bar. She knows that a six-word sentence exists and has not said or written it. She still has not said the office's compass point or its figure in lengths aloud. Left palm in linen; she writes; she carried a box one-handed.

**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. He read three ages of iron in the fence and the watch's habits from the grass; counted seven out; looked at the gap from a mile and saw nothing, and does not like it. He has seen a keg's hoop and five made heels at the seep. He gave the watchman's stave to Cael. The case is on his breastbone, shut; nothing about it is altered; not mentioned aloud.

**Vastin.** No office; no age stated. **Right knee: day twenty-six of thirty on the 305th**; Oryn's linen and two laths; stick; he walked the whole leg in the file, a stride behind Brom. He said on the 303rd that he would not stand a wall and did not. **He stood inside the fourth line on the 304th, fifth of five; the sigil went out for the first time in his life and came back "to the grain".** He has said "Noted" a third time (to Karis's two pages). He began the sentence about the apparatus and the sigils and was stopped. He read the oldest row through, found the two slips, read both aloud, knows both seals and has named neither; he drafted nothing. He made room for the broad man's stick. He does not know the mechanism, the eleventh (his own), the twelfth, the thirteenth, the two words, the date, or that the lean of the rows was seen. He has not asked to join and nobody has spoken of it.

**Oryn.** Tide; Iron, Rank Four, provisional. **Five days past the cairn on the 305th** (she looked up her road once at noon on the 300th and walked on east; her reasons were a shoulder's week, a knee, and an untested edge). She knit the broad man's shin on the 304th, asked first and answered in a word, and was grey for a quarter of an hour; she read it again on the 305th inside the older healer's binding. She counted Brom over the fourth line: "Four. The same." She would not spend on Brom's bruises or be told the count of fragments across the iron: **she has not been told of the thirteenth.** She has not heard the leaf; she was at the fire, not the blanket. Nobody has died under her hands. Three holds are still owed, with word sent.

**Teague and his crew.** His right arm is in linen to the shoulder on a slat; the wrist and the elbow went at the timbers; he has been warned about a third time (marker 7). He said "Noted" for the first time on the 303rd. He did not turn when the broad man fell, and has told the broad man why. He thanked Cael for his left side. **He does not know that anything came to Cael at the timbers.** He knows what lies under the watch's hide and has left the reading of it to Karis. The broad man: right shin knit and bound to a board; a trestle's leg for a stick; his crossbow's stock split; he has said "Aye" to Vastin and to Oryn and nothing else to any of the six. The tall man has said nothing to any of them; he stopped the boy with a hand at the seep, kept Teague's right at the timbers, cut the stick. The young one: stepped over stake thirty-four fourth ("Four"), stood at the pinch, held when the broad man fell, ran when he might; he walked the seep at dusk. The older healer has spoken to none of the six; she set the shin and nodded once to Oryn. **Teague's four have seen:** five people stand inside the line and come out whole; a boy with a stick. **They have not been told:** the mechanism, any fragment, the leaf, the list, the lamps finding, the two slips, the date, who Vastin was, or the doctrine "behind it". No wall has been drilled or stood.

**The watch.** Seven, hooded, faceless, unnamed, one with a Path that throws light. It saw: a big man take two bolts on Iron; a Force put a barricade a body's length; five people cross the iron, walk sixty strides inside it, and stand; an old man on a stick among them. **It saw nothing work inside the line, and no light, crack or named thing from either crew.** Nobody was killed or taken. It went east.

**Objects and money.** **127 → 123** (the 300th: four marks at a hold's gate for two water-skins, meal, a cheese and corn). No other spending from the purse. Coppers, kept apart from it as the closed text keeps them: one for the mule's bucket on the 301st; three of Brom's own on the watch's keg. The reserve is sealed in the third box. On the mule: three boxes (the fifty-two sheets, with fourteen of them and the nine new ones handled on the 305th and put back), the flat, meal, Oryn's pannier and canvas. With Karis: the hide with its pinned sheet (the prick now ringed with a thumbnail, uninked), the Ternhall brass, her notebook (the torn lamps leaf in its back), Cleon's roll, the sheet of three boxes, Vastin's copied page, nine sheets of the piers, one new stake thirty-four. With Cael: Hesk's book, the dividers shut in it, the fair copy of the leaf (laid on the blanket folded and returned). With Vastin: the tarred kit case (cord, mallet, stake thirty-two, never driven; the cord was used at the Stair by Karis and put back). The two slips are pinned again under the knot of the oldest row's tape, in the flat. Left at the watch's post: its hut, fire, keg, meal, bolts, mallet, cord, eleven new bars, a sound crossbow on the baulk, a stave. Ten water-skins between the crews (two bought on the 300th).

**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six, as before; Cleon knows how one fragment came. **The thirteenth: Cael, Lira, Brom** (Karis and Seln by Lira or the log; not staged); not Oryn, Teague or Vastin. The two words: Cael alone. The lean of the rows: Cael, Karis, Lira, Brom. The corner's year and the ringed point: the five at the blanket and Vastin; the figure itself, Karis and Cael (marker 10). The lamps finding: Karis, Cael, Lira, Brom, Vastin; nobody has laid it on the leaf, aloud or silently. The two slips: the same five; the seals' owners: Vastin alone. The date: Cael and Seln, each alone, unchanged. The short row: Cael, Karis, Lira.

**Open threads.** Ten markers. A gap in the hills where seven went and nothing shows. A thing that listens and has never been let go, whose owner is forty yards off and not told. Two debts of one kind. A healer who has not been told a count, and has ribs to hear about. A sentence six words long that is in nobody's book. A third page that was kept off a blanket. Two seals that one man can name. A ring in a sheet of paper with no ink in it, two days off. A boy who goes where he is looked at. A captain with one arm. A week that ended today, and a healer who is still here.

## Owner flags

1. **The cairn against Book 7 (47:197–201; 48:109–117).** The plan puts Oryn's north-turning cairn at noon on the 300th, before the hall's marsh; the edition's Book 7 has one north-going fork on this line, the heap of chock-stones, a day PAST the marsh. I kept the ruling and minted a second place (a built cairn and a mule-path at the bend where the carters' road swings south to a hold), and she passes the chock-stones on the 302nd without looking up. Is a second north road acceptable, or should the cairn BE the chock-stones (which would move it to the 302nd and change her count)?
2. **The leg's intervals (47:253).** Stair to hall in a day and a half, against Book 7's two days and a morning; the page gives the reason (no surveying; a quiet man who knows where the track comes out). Hall to brow is three days, as in Book 7. Does that hold?
3. **Ten markers** (table above). Four carry choices that change a scene's sense: 49:169 (whose term "Nobody declares…" is; the scene and the 303rd's log are staged as Cael's); 50:245 (who answers "We've been in three times"); 51:21 (who warns Teague about the arm; staged as Oryn from a distance, since the older healer may not speak before the six); 53:291 (the close's alternation).
4. **What "nobody declares" is on the page (50:19–45; 50:99–105).** I have read it by Seln's Book 7 definition ("a crack, a light"): nothing is called by its name, nothing is thrown at a person, nothing answers the watch's light. Brom's Iron (silent, under a coat) and Teague's Force (silent, on a timber) are both used outside the line. Is that the sense the plan means, or should even those be withheld?
5. **Deployment thirty-three (48:247–251; 48:327).** I added one use in earnest, the read at the seep, on the precedent of the calf at the fence (deployment twenty-eight). If the count must stay at thirty-two through this movement, the seam of water can be found by Brom's hands alone and four log lines change (48:327, 49:271, 51:349, 53:273).
6. **The purse reaches 123 on the 300th, not the 304th (47:227–229).** Four marks at a hold's gate for skins and victual; nothing is bought at the Span. The plan's calendar shows 123 against the 304th. Is the earlier day acceptable?
7. **The S19 log's "Tomorrow" (51:339; 51:343).** The fixed text says "Tomorrow I find out what standing in the middle of the first one feels like", written on the 304th; this edition's office distance is three days. I printed it whole and had Cael notice the word and let it stand as "the truth about how near the thing had come". Is that patch wanted, or should the two sentences after the log go?
8. **Whose the watch is (49:95–111; 49:213–217; 51:339).** Cael's fixed log says that the Compact fenced the place "and left men on it". On the page nobody but that log says whose the seven are; Seln thinks of the sheets such a posting needs and of "that house's hands". Too much, or too little, for a faction that must stay unnamed and unlocated?
9. **A rider on a bay east of the river (47:231).** Movement 7's ruling has nothing seen east of the river as of the 299th. The hold's keeper reports, on the 300th, a man on a bay who went through two days earlier and foretold a crew; and two holds are barred on the 301st. No face, no name. Does a report at second hand breach that ruling?
10. **The Span's ground (49:85–97; 51:203–207; 51:261–267).** The ravine ("the sink"), the shelf, the pinch, the arch with a road on its back, the hole twenty yards beyond it, and the name from Teague's carters are mine, built to §8's left-and-right. The Book 7 valley is "open toward the south"; I have put the ravine on the north. Anything here that the pass or the court needs otherwise?
11. **The watch's man (50:15–27).** His Path is unnamed and is shown only as a hung point of white light; his call ("This ground is posted. Stand where you are, and declare.") is mine. Is a spoken demand to declare right for this edition?
12. **Who is inside, and for how long (50:181–211; 50:235–299).** Five, for about three minutes; nothing opened; four of them without what they carry for the whole of it. **Vastin's sigil goes out for the first time** (50:209; 50:315–319: "It is all there … To the grain."). Does that spend something that the court's line (his fixed speech on the 307th) needs?
13. **The young one (47:153–181; 48:197–209; 49:15–27; 50:195–203; 50:271–277; 51:123–141).** His water given to a cloth; the hand at the seep; "Four"; holding at the grass by Lira's boots; asleep with the cloth; and Teague's "He would have to think about whose boots a boy watched." Is the last line, or "he would go wherever he was looked at", too plain a finger at the pass?
14. **The broad man's leg and his words (50:261; 50:325; 50:337–343; 51:91–119; 53:33; 53:47).** It is the right shin, the good leg. His consent to Oryn is "in a word" that does not carry to Cael; in Teague's window he speaks to Teague at some length. His first word to the six, "Aye", is said to Vastin's remark about the ground, and a second "Aye" to Oryn's "May I?". Does the unheard word on the 304th sit inside the lock?
15. **Teague is thanked and not told; Oryn is not told either (51:55–75; 51:195; 53:63–71; 53:139–147).** Lira and Brom know the thirteenth. Oryn refuses the count "across a row of iron" and has not been given it by the movement's end, though she is owed the ribs. Movement 9 has her knowing thirteen; should she be told on the 305th?
16. **The tier in the fragment (51:71–75).** Cael marks that the "Bronze" in his own cataloguing is the thing he once declined to read off Teague's wrist (Book 7 chapter 56). Is that connection wanted?
17. **The row that counts short (51:279–309; 51:351).** Fifty-two signs counted as fifty-one, twice, and right with Lira beside him; Karis remembers the inn yard and says nothing. Deniable enough?
18. **"Through" as a word on a pier, and *Goes* (51:311–327).** I have staged the plan's quoted word as the one group that Karis can turn on the west pier, and given the twelfth a breath of that course (*Goes*, eastward) just before. If "Through" is somebody's remark about the line, the lead-in at 51:317–321 changes.
19. **How "the rows lean along the bearing" is known (52:71–101).** By the twelfth's one unbidden glance over fourteen sheets laid as their walls stood; told to Karis, Lira and Brom; entered by Karis as Cael's and unchecked; not counted as a deployment. Karis's sentence "whoever cut them cut each one to go on to the next" is the furthest that anybody goes. Too far?
20. **The corner signs and the figure (52:117–145; 53:159–173).** I have made the two unread signs the first letters of a year in the old town's count (a clerk's short form), the same two letters as the year at the skin's head, with eleven candidate years in one hundred. That is what earns "documented four hundred years back … or near it" in the close. It lays the fifth place's series beside the leaf's date (not beside the lamps). Is that join the plan's, and is the mechanism acceptable?
21. **The lamps finding's ground (52:165–181).** A table of crossings kept since the Fallow; seven people; "a man who signed for other people's for forty years". Karis says "sir" to Vastin. No tier is given for him.
22. **The two slips (52:225–249).** Pinned under the knot of the oldest row's tape, with a red wafer and a broader dark one. Vastin had carried the order "in my own hand the whole time, under a knot". The rows were counted and re-taped at the spring in Movement 3 without anybody remarking them; the page has Vastin say that he took the folded slips for a clerk's tag. Does finding them inside the flat agree with what Movements 2 and 3 say was looked for and not found?
23. **Karis's night (53:189–229).** Her interior says that a sentence exists, that it is six words long, and that she will not say or write it; nothing in it states the sentence or lays one page on the other. Her entry, "Second page: nothing entered. Not for want of a pencil.", is in a notebook that others may read. Inside the limit?
24. **Seln's three ages of iron (49:195–205) and the dozen new bars under the hide (51:159–169; 51:333).** The oldest bars are older than he can put a figure to; new ones are stocked at the post; Karis keeps one. Does a visibly maintained fence say more about a present keeper than §11 allows ("every site is old and stable")?
25. **The stone at the Stair (47:35–49).** Somebody has removed Karis's marker and laid a turf since the thaw, "to make it agree with his sheet". A yearly visit by the office is implied. Acceptable?
26. **Shale-backs at the seep (48:151–183).** Teague's account of their day (licking at first light and dusk; mowing between) and his rule of approach are mine, laid on Book 7's two-part carters' rule and Karis's derived leaf. The mule drinks four skins.
27. **Teague's teaching and his thanks (47:83–127; 48:65–97; 53:63–67).** "A piece of ground's a sum"; the three things worth a fist; "I never had the place behind my shoulder stay so full." Cael says "Noted" to him on the 301st (48:93), two days before Teague's own first.
28. **Vastin's refusal entered beforehand (49:33–49) and his step over the iron (50:205–209).** He says on the 303rd that he will not stand a wall; on the 304th he crosses on his own motion. Oryn's terms forbid kneeling, running and a wall, and say nothing of standing in a file.
29. **Force, dry (53:75–121).** Right heel and right palm, as Teague sets it; the ribs are low on the LEFT (Book 7 chapter 55), and the page says that the road goes "through the cage" and not up one side. Movement 9's "the recoil stops in the ribs" should be checked against that.
30. **Seln on the gap (53:233–261).** He sees nothing at all, and that is what he dislikes; the rig, the shelf and the count are left wholly to the 306th.
31. **One fire on the 305th (53:3–7).** Two fires on every other night of the leg, by the contract; one here, for want of wood, in two halves.
32. **Length.** 36,996 words of mine, at the top of the brief's allowance; the restorations will take it a little past 37,000.
# EVENT LIST — Book 8, Movement 8 "The Sunken Span" (chapters 47–53, days 299–305 past the Line)

Written before any prose, from: the compiled prompt read whole (seat profile, formula, packet `packets/MOVEMENT-008.md` with all its coordinator notes, chapter 46 as compiled, `EDITION_BRIEF.md`, `BOOK_MAP.md` §1–§16, `STATE_LEDGER.md` ENTRY and AFTER MOVEMENT 1–7, `CANON_RULES.md`, the owner voice); the launch prompt; the coordinator's mid-run note that Movement 7's repair had landed (five items); and the edition's own closed text (Book 7 chapters 47, 48, 50–53, 55, 57 and 58 in full, 54 and 56 by range; Book 8 chapters 44 and 45 in full, 46 as compiled and its repaired close from disk, 2 by range; the rest by search). No file under `books/` or `v3-runs/` was opened.

## Standing facts carried in

- Dawn of the 299th at the notch's head above Millrace. Twelve and a mule. File (Teague's): Seln ahead; the tall man; Teague, Cael at his left shoulder a stride back; Lira; the young one (watching her feet); Karis, the two healers and the mule in the middle; Brom; Vastin a stride behind him; the bow (the broad man) last. Four hands: flat *stand*; fist *seen*; two fingers *come on*; hand turned over empty *nothing*. On a road nobody speaks; talk is for halts and camps.
- **Twelve fragments; deployment since the Line thirty-two; purse 127.** The eleventh (*This far?*) unused, not told to Vastin. The twelfth leans outward at made things unbidden; never turned inward by will; *still* / *set* are Cael's alone. Cleon's warning has begun to lean.
- Bodies: Vastin day twenty of thirty on the 299th (21, 22, 23, 24, 25 on the 304th, 26 on the 305th), Oryn's linen and two laths, stick, no kneeling, running or Wall. Brom both arms to the shoulder. Lira's arm "an arm". Karis's left palm in linen ("Write. Don't lean."). Teague's right wrist in linen under the leather (one push, on the 298th).
- Contract: ten on the count, neither healer; shares and costs by the count; the field Teague's; the reading Karis's ("You'll be asked."). Teague's four know: a survey read, a bearing, the four ages, "three days past the iron". Not the mechanism, any fragment, the leaf, the list, the date, or who Vastin was. The tall man, the broad man and the older healer have said nothing to the six. Teague has not said "Noted".
- Oryn: on nobody's count; three holds owed, lying the way they walk "for a day and some; and after that my road turns". Has not heard the leaf. Mended Brom's left shoulder on the 298th.
- Country, from the edition's Book 7 (chapters 47–48, 52–53, 57–58): the fork above Millrace; half a day south along the bare ridge to the Stair's bowl (the brow; the live ruts going round; the old road straight down; the grey stone and two irons numbered thirty-two, forty paces below the brow; the floor and its square hole). Beyond it the carters' road runs south and east under the ridge's foot for a carter's day, with a halting-ring; then the road swings south to a hold whose smoke shows, and an old track (two ruts, a tree in one) keeps the line for a carter's day more, goes under the turf, and comes to the rise above the Hall's marsh (reeds that stop where the line stops; stake thirty-three; the grey path; the hall in the water). A road lies a mile east of that water; two holds and a dead one on it; a fork at a heap of chock-stones (the left hand to a notch, north and west); then the road bends south and the line climbs a bare whale's back of a ridge, a mile broad, for two days, to a brow. Under the brow: a great shallow head of pale ground open to the south; old ruts high round its head; a furlong below them (a hundred and ninety paces) a fence of the registry's iron, one bar to a pace, each stamped thirty-four with the stroke and the bar; four hundred and forty yards and two strides from that line to a grey upright thing in the middle, "not one thing… there's sky in it". A carter's "day" is about half a walking crew's (chapter 47: a loaded cart takes the whole of a short day to the mill; a crew on foot is at the fork before noon).

## Decisions made before drafting

1. **The calendar of the leg, laid against Book 7.** 299: dawn at the notch; the Stair's bowl a little after noon; Karis's hour; four hours on down the carters' road; camp at a beck short of the halting-ring. 300: the place where the road swings south and the old track keeps the line, at noon — **the cairn is there** (decision 2); the track through the afternoon; the rise above the Hall's marsh at dusk; camp at the track's end outside the grey stone. 301: the road a mile east of the marsh; two holds; the dead hold at dusk (dry camp one). 302: the heap of chock-stones two hours after sunrise; the road bends south before noon and the file climbs the ridge; the seep in a fold of its southern flank in the afternoon; dry camp two in a hollow on the ridge's back. 303: the ridge; the brow at dusk. That gives Hall to brow in three days, as Book 7 walked it (121st–123rd), and Stair to Hall in a day and a half of hard walking against Book 7's two and a morning at a survey's pace.
2. **The cairn (flag: the plan and the closed text pull apart here).** The plan puts the cairn where Oryn's road turns north at noon on the 300th, BEFORE the Hall's marsh. The edition's Book 7 has only one north-going fork on this line, the heap of chock-stones, and it lies a day PAST the marsh. I have kept the coordinator's ruling (noon on the 300th) and minted a second place: at the bend where the carters' road swings south to the hold, a built cairn as high as a man's hip marks a mule-path that leaves northward over the hill; it is the way to the three holds that she owes. The chock-stones are passed on the morning of the 302nd, by which time she is two days past her road; she does not look up the notch. Her days are counted from the cairn (301: one … 305: five).
3. **Oryn's honest reason for walking on east** (her own rule, Book 7 chapter 53): she shut a deep wrench in Brom's left shoulder on the 298th, and "a mend so deep wants a week of somebody's eye on it" — that is the 305th; there is a knee on her linen at day twenty-one; and there is an edge at the end of the brass twice the size of any that she has stood at, which nobody has tested, and she said in a loft where somebody ought to stand when a crew crosses one. She sent "a week, or more" up the water from Millrace with the miller's carter. At the cairn she looks up the path once, straight, and walks on; she gives the sum afterwards in three lines, as a sum and not a kindness. Nobody asks her (Cael wrote that he would not).
4. **Money.** 127 → **123** on the 300th: four marks at the gate of the hold south of the cairn (two water-skins, meal, a cheese, corn for the mule; the six's own victual for the dry leg and the way back; Teague's four buy their own). No other spending. The watch's stores are not touched except its water, for which Brom leaves three coppers of his own on the keg (coppers do not move the purse).
5. **Deployment.** One use in earnest: the read (Iron-adjacent), on the wet ledge at the seep on the 302nd, for the length of six breaths, paid in near sight for an hour. 32 + 1 = **33**. Nothing else is opened in the movement: not at the crest, not on the shelf, not inside the line. The twelfth's outward leans are unbidden and uncounted (the old iron at the Hall's stone; the fence; the arch; the piers; the blanket). The dry stance on the shale is a trial, not counted. **Fragments: thirteen** from the 304th.
6. **"Known" under each date** (the five dated entries of the leg): 299, Karis's grey stone is gone from between the two irons and a square turf has been set where it stood; 300, the hold's keeper had skins filled and priced before Brom spoke, because a rider went through two days before and said that a crew would want them; 301, both holds on the east road have their gates barred at noon, a month after the thaw; 302, nailed boots have been at the seep's pan within the week, and a hoop of a keg lies there; 303, the watch is standing to at its barricade when they top the brow.
7. **The Stair's bowl: the number that she already has.** The dead surveyor's "station to floor", which is also on every yearly sheet: nine lengths and a piece from the old iron at the stone to the floor's lip. She lays the kit's own cord (from Vastin's case) down the old road with Lira on its far end and Seln cutting turfs: she is checking the ruler, not the place, before she stands in front of a fence where the measure is double. It is what it was. Cael does not touch the cord (his ruling of the 168th). Teague's four stay on the brow; the tall man stands where he stood on the southern rim. Teague gives Cael a first lesson in reading ground (where two men lay on that brow for a day).
8. **The seep (how it is solved; mine).** A fold in the ridge's southern flank; a band of dark shale across it; under the band's western end a ledge weeps into a trodden pan no bigger than a wash-tub; a herd of shale-backs stands on the slope and along the band. The carters' rule is "Never be below them", and the only water is below them. Teague's reading: they lick the weep at first light and at dusk and mow away from it between; the old cow at the east end is the one that the rest take their feet from; she has turned, and will be back over the pan in about an hour and a half; nobody stands on the fall-line under a beast; nobody steps quick near a calf; in by the western end along the foot of the band, out the same way; two at a time; the mule stays on the ridge and is watered from Oryn's canvas. The pan gives a skin in the time that it takes to say a slip over forty times, and there are ten skins and a mule. The tall man goes first. **The young one volunteers and is already moving; the tall man stops him with a flat hand on the breastbone, wordlessly.** Cael and Lira take the third turn; he lies on the ledge and opens the read on the rock: water stands in a seam behind a leaf of shale as thick as a hand, which is held only at its foot. He wants Pressure for it and goes without; two fingers bring Brom, who draws the leaf off with both arms. The pan fills three times as fast. The old cow comes back along the band and stands over them while the last skins fill; they go out sideways, off her fall-line, at her own pace, Cael with no near sight and Lira tying his skins. Nobody declares; no beast lifts its feet in a hurry. The mule drinks four skins.
9. **The watch and its post (private staging).** North of the bowl a dry ravine comes down out of the hills ("the sink": the country's water goes underground). For a hundred yards it runs along the outside of the fence, and the level ground between its lip and the irons is a shelf a cart wide; then it bends inward, cuts the line, and runs to the middle, where the arch stands over it; a little beyond the arch it ends in a hole. Walking the shelf eastward: the sink's drop (three men) on the left hand, the irons on the right, the line a stride past the irons. Thirty yards in, at the shelf's narrowest, **a barricade of two timbers** on trestles, chest-high. Behind it the shelf widens to five strides: **a hut** of turf and shale, **a fire**, **kit under a hide**. At the east end the sink's lip comes to within a stride and a half of the irons (**the pinch**); beyond it a trodden **perimeter path a pace wide** goes on round the fence. **Seven hoods**, faces tied: **two crossbows**, four with shod staves, and **one low practitioner, empty-handed**, whose work on that posting is to find the line with his foot and to throw light.
10. **What "nobody declares" is on the page.** Seln's definition (Book 7 chapter 50): "a crack, a light"; a thing that a pair of eyes could carry to a boat. Nothing is thrown at a person and nothing is called by its name. Brom's skin under his coat and Teague's line on a timber make neither sound nor light; both are known kinds; neither is aimed at a body. Inside the line nothing is opened by anybody.
11. **The fight, exchange by exchange.**
    - *Positions at first light (304th).* Seln went round the head of the bowl in the dark and lies in the grass above the perimeter path, sixty yards east of the pinch, outside the line: he is to count them out and to say if a second team comes. Oryn, the older healer and the mule are at the old ruts, a furlong up, out of bowshot. Nine at the shelf's western mouth: Brom in front; Teague behind his right shoulder, with Cael at Teague's left a stride back (carrying the shortened watchman's stave from the alley, on Teague's order: "Three breaths. Keep my left"); the tall man at Teague's right; Lira; the young one; Karis; Vastin; the broad man last with his crossbow.
    - *One (the watch's).* At forty yards the practitioner declares: a white light thrown up and hung over the shelf, and a call to stand and declare. Nobody answers. Brom walks on. Two bolts: one on the breastbone, one on the left forearm, both on Iron under the coat, both turned, both paid for in bruises. The bowmen bend to span (twenty counts and more).
    - *Two (Teague's).* Inside those counts Teague goes up the middle past Brom. Cael is at his left a stride back the whole way. At the timbers Teague sets his right heel against a rib of the shelf's rock and his palm on the upper baulk, and takes three breaths to line it. A stave comes over the top at his head on the first breath; Cael takes it on his own stave (the yard's *out* and *home*). A second catches Cael across the left collar-bone on the third breath; he keeps the place. The tall man keeps the right with the ash crosswise, steel high. Then the push: no light, no sound but the timber. Both baulks and their trestles go a body's length back, all of a piece, and take five people off their feet; one crossbow is under a baulk and its stock is broken; nobody is under it. The recoil: the wrist (the click), then the elbow. **[[TIER-B: "Through."]]** **The thirteenth arrives** at a stride: it comes up out of the ground through his own heels and stops at the right hand; it does not pour, set or turn; it stands to, like a prop under a beam, and then it listens. **Fragment thirteen, verbatim, in italics, as his own plain words, once.**
    - *Three (width).* The watch reforms behind the timbers where they lie: one bow spanned, four staves, the light relit. Teague's arm is dead to the elbow and the Force cannot be put in front of that twice. He reads the ground a stride past the irons: as flat as a floor, and nobody's. He asks Karis (the reading is hers). **[[TIER-B: "Nothing was gained inside"]]** stands before the act. **[[TIER-B: "By count."]]** Five go over stake thirty-four, one at a time: Cael (grass); Lira, a stride in and a stride back ("Gone." / "Back." — the same as the others; the test that she asked for at this fence in Book 7); Karis; the young one, when Teague looks at him ("Four."); Vastin, fifth, on the stick ("I am forbidden a wall. I am not forbidden to stand."). Four stay on the shelf: Teague, the tall man, Brom, the broad man. The five walk east, two strides inside the irons, past the timbers and the fire, at a road's pace. The watch's bow cannot turn to them without giving its side to Brom, the spear and the broad man's bow. No hood crosses the irons.
    - *The pinch; eleven seconds.* The five stop two strides inside the line, abreast of the pinch, and turn their faces outward. Cael counts. One–two: **[[TIER-B: "You'll die in there." / "We've been in three times."]]** Inside him the eleventh asks *This far?* at a gap a stride and a half wide, and the thirteenth listens at the rock of the lip; he tells both no. **Four: the watch's bowman turns and looses at the one bow that threatens him; the bolt takes the broad man's crossbow at the stock; he twists; the bad hip goes and the shale lip goes with it, and he is over the edge. Teague does not turn.** The young one, facing the shelf from inside the line, sees it; there is no hand near him; Lira's feet do not move, and he stands. Five–ten: the watch looks at an emptied bow, a grey man, a spear, a captain walking up the shelf with his left hand on his axe, and five people standing where nobody stands. Eleven: the first hood goes through the pinch, a stride from Vastin's stick; then the rest, one at a time; the practitioner last, with his light let go. East along the path. Seln counts seven and does not stir. Nobody follows.
    - **Win sentence, verbatim, once.**
    - *Costs carried forward.* The broad man's right shin, broken in the sink's bed (his good leg). Teague's right arm, wrist and elbow. Brom's breastbone and forearm ("a week of colours"). Cael's left collar-bone, a stave's bruise. A crossbow's stock, split.
12. **Inside the line (who, how long).** The five are inside from the crossing to the last hood: by Cael's reckoning about three minutes — sixty-odd strides at a walk, eleven seconds standing at the pinch, and the time that seven take to go through a gap singly. Nothing is opened. The four with Paths are without them for the whole of it; Vastin's sigil is out for the first time in his life. They come out over the irons at the pinch.
13. **After.** The leg: a fall of rock at the shelf's west end lets the healers down into the bed. Oryn asks and has her answer in a word that does not carry to Cael; the older healer draws the leg straight on her board; Oryn knits it and is grey for a quarter of an hour; **the two healers nod.** Teague's arm: **[[TIER-B: "A third time on that arm and I'll set it in a splint. That's not a healer's opinion. That's a fact about a line." / "Noted," / "Everybody says that now,"]]** (the third line is Oryn's by §13; the first two are not pinned). Brom's bill. Cael tells Lira first ("Thirteenth. His. A line."), then the other four of the six, and Oryn at the irons. **Teague is not told**: a second debt of the same kind as the first.
14. **Teague's cutaway (the hut; two scenes).** He counts four by their breathing. The bow's leg on the old woman's board. The boy asleep sitting up with the cloth in his fist. What the watch left under the hide: a bundle of new irons stamped thirty-four with the stroke and bar, a mallet, a cord knotted at the fives, bolts with no fletcher's thread, a keg of water, meal. Why he did not turn. What he saw the boy with the book do at his left for three breaths (a stave, and nothing else). **Nothing of the mechanism and no suspicion of it; the boy is "the boy".**
15. **The Span at noon.** Down the bowl inside the line: Cael, Karis, Lira, Brom, Seln and Vastin; Oryn at the irons with two fingers on Brom's wrist as he crosses ("Four. The same."). An arch on two piers over the sink, all of the grey, no join. No steps: the grey comes up out of the turf on either hand as a road rises, a cart wide, with no kerb, to a crown and down again. **Nothing happens on the crown.** The thirteenth listens at the grey underfoot (nothing goes into it) and the old three ribs ache where the line would come out. The view east: hills, and a notch, low and square. The piers' inner faces, reached from the sink's bed by a rib of rock, are cut in courses; Karis copies nine sheets. **The row counts one short for Cael, twice** (the Book 11 plant), and whole when Lira counts beside him; entered, ruling *hold*. Karis reads the one word that she can turn on the west pier: **[[TIER-B: the bearing runs "Through"]]**. The name: Teague has heard carters on a salt road two days north call the thing that they see from far off "the Sunken Span" (flag).
16. **The 304th's log:** my head line (day, place, purse 123, Vastin twenty-five); **the S19 log, verbatim, in italics, once** (it holds the rate line; its "Tomorrow" is the source's and is flagged — the office's run is three days, a little less); then my count line with the arithmetic (thirty-three; thirteen).
17. **The 305th.** East along the bearing from the fence's far side; two men on sticks; shale country, stepped; camp by the middle of the afternoon on a shelf of shale under a step as high as a man, with the notch four or five miles off. Seln goes on alone to look at it.
18. **The blanket (order of work; mine).** Brom's blanket, on the flat of the shelf, twenty yards from the fire and upwind; Karis, Cael, Lira, Brom and Vastin; Seln when he returns. In the middle the hide with its pinned sheet, the brass on the pencil line; round it, each turned to lie as its stone lay, north to north: one sheet from each of the three floors and the nine of the piers; at the head the flat with its six rows; at the foot her notebook open at the list, Cleon's roll, the sheet of three boxes; under a pebble at one corner the fair copy from Cael's coat, folded, not opened. **The rows lean along the bearing:** a sign that bears heads every run, so each run has a head and a tail; laid as they lay, every run on that blanket has its head to the west and its tail to the east, along the brass. (The twelfth sees the same thing, unbidden, and Cael looks at the brass instead.) **The point:** the two signs in the fifth place's corner are a year in the old town's count, the count in which the head of a certain skin is dated; she lays the one against the other without saying the figure until the close. **The thumbnail circle** goes round the divider's prick.
19. **The lamps finding (Karis's; Tier A 8, verbatim, once).** Its ground on the page: at the Stair and the Fallow and the hall, and yesterday at the irons, every sigil went at the same stride, whole, and came back whole — a Wind of the first rank, an Ember of the third, a boy, and a man who was at the top of the ladder for forty years; no dimming, no order by strength. Vastin: "If a man designed the apparatus, then the sigils —" (pinned; placed). Karis: the ruler rule (Tier B, verbatim). Two pages; the second has nothing on it. **Vastin's third "Noted."** Cael sees the foot of the second row, where a technician's sheet lies, and does not add a third page; Seln's eyes rest there once; neither speaks.
20. **The directive and the objection (Tier A 7).** Vastin, reading the sixth and oldest row through for the first time, finds two slips pinned inside its tape under two seals: both texts read out by him, verbatim; then his three sentences and "That's a crack." (pinned; placed). **[[TIER-B: "The Compact … Yes. Part of it."]]** He does not name the seals; nobody presses him; it is not pried.
21. **One fire.** The broad man is brought to it on the old woman's staff and a cut stick; Vastin, on his, moves his own stick's foot a hand to the left to make room for the other, and says a thing about the ground; **the broad man: "Aye."** (his first word to any of the six). **The healers' third exchange:** Oryn's hands inside the older healer's binding on the shin while the older woman holds the turns open; no word.
22. **Force, dry.** Behind the step, at dusk, out of the fire's sight: heel on a rib of shale, palm on a slab; the line set joint by joint as he saw it set twice. The thirteenth listens and finds both ends. The ribs warn: the line would come out there, short of the hand. He takes his palm away. Trial; not counted. Lira saw. Reasons: a thing that has never been fired is not tried first beside a camp; and its owner is forty yards off with his arm on a board, and has not been told.
23. **Karis's cutaway (two scenes), the night after.** The refusal held in her own head. She knows that a sentence exists; she knows how long it is (six words), as she once knew the length of a word under a glove; she does not let it be said, and she does not write it. Nothing in her head lays the one page on the other.
24. **Seln on the pass:** "I looked at it." (pinned; placed). A notch, low and square; he did not like it; he will look again from nearer. **The close:** **[[TIER-B: the S20 close]]**, then the fixed last line of narration, verbatim.

