   101	- **Costs carried forward.** The broad man's right shin (the good leg), broken in the ravine's bed; drawn straight on the older healer's board, knit by Oryn in a quarter of an hour, bound to the board in four turns of webbing; he walks on a trestle's leg from the 305th. Teague's right arm, wrist and elbow, in linen to the shoulder on a slat; he cannot feel the fingers. Brom: a round bruise the size of a saucer on the breastbone and a ridged one along the left forearm; Oryn will not spend on them ("You'll have a week of colours"). Cael: a welt across the left collar-bone, "a bruise on a bone", ten days. A crossbow with a bolt standing in its split stock.
   102	- **After the fall (50:307–367; 51:3–75).** The boy runs when Teague lifts two fingers and holds the broad man's head off the stones. Oryn asks; a nod; "I don't go in on a nod. I'll have it in a word."; the word is given and does not carry up the rock. The two healers nod, once each, across the leg. Vastin, coming out over the iron: "It is all there … To the grain." Brom carries the broad man's pack as the contract's second head ("I can carry what was on his back") and leaves three coppers of his own on the watch's keg for a skin of water. Cael to Lira: "Thirteenth. His. A line." / "It listens. I'd have liked it better if it asked." The tier in the fragment's lines is the thing that he once declined to read off that wrist at a marsh's edge.
   103	- **Teague's window (51:79–175).** He counts three by breathing and the spear by a shadow at the gap. The elbow has gone once before, "a long while ago, on a ledge". The bow to him: "And you never turned your head." / "If you'd turned, I'd have had something to say to you from the bottom of that hole"; "It's the good one"; of Oryn, "She asked me first. With my leg lying two ways." The boy asleep sitting, the dry cloth in his fist. Teague's reckoning: the boy "would go wherever he was looked at, a half-beat before he had seen what was there", and held at the grass by a girl's boots; "He would have to think about whose boots a boy watched." Of Cael: a left side kept with a stick and nothing else; "Teague could put that in a column." **Under the hide:** a sack and a half of meal, a bundle of bolts with no thread on their feathers, a mallet, a tarred cord knotted every few feet, and a dozen new bars, never driven, each stamped with two figures and the stroke and bar. He does not read it further; "It was not his to read."
   104	- **Noon at the Span (51:179–241).** Oryn at the irons, two fingers on Brom's wrist, asked first: "Four. The same." She will not be told how many "across a row of iron". Six go down: Karis, Lira, Brom, Seln, Vastin, Cael. The twelfth leans at the whole of the thing and is refused by counting bootlaces. On the crown: nothing; the thirteenth listens to the grey; the ribs.
   105	- **The notch (51:245–259).** Lira sees it from the crown: a gap low in the second ridge, square; "The brass goes through it. I'd lay my knife." Seln looks at it longer than either.
   106	- **The piers' inner faces (51:261–327).** Reached from the ravine's bed by a rib of rock on the western side; five courses to the height of a man, in the floors' hand, unworn; Karis draws nine sheets in two hours on a board of Brom's, with a tick where each run ends. **The lowest course of the west pier has fifty-two signs; Cael counts fifty-one, twice, and fifty-two with Lira's finger beside his** (the Book 11 plant; ruling *hold*, 51:351). The twelfth takes that course for a breath: *Goes*, eastward, out of the face toward the other pier. Karis can turn one group in the second course (marker 8). She keeps one of the new bars from under the hide, by Teague's leave (51:333).
   107	- **The 304th's log (51:335–353).** After the fixed text Cael sees that he has written *tomorrow* for three days and lets it stand: "It was not a true count; it was the truth about how near the thing had come."
   108	- **The 305th's road (52:3–37).** The tall man cuts the broad man's stick out of the watch's trestle. The file goes round the outside of the iron by the trodden path; from the far side the road on the Span's back lies end-on and the same line runs out to the gap. A captain's pace "that a child could have kept"; two sticks at the back that stop within a breath of one another and never look round. Stepped shale with a thin red moss and no water. Camp in the middle of the afternoon under a step a man's head high, with forty yards of flat before it and the gap four or five miles off. Seln goes on alone.
   109	- **The blanket (52:41–101).** Brom's blanket, twenty yards upwind of the fire. In the middle the hide, turned until its pencil line runs at the gap, the Ternhall brass upon it; one sheet from the Fallow, three from the Stair, one from the hall and the nine of the piers, each turned to lie as its wall stood (she had noted on every sheet which wall and which way it faced); the flat at the head with its six rows; her notebook at the list, Cleon's roll and the sheet of three boxes at the foot; the fair copy from Cael's coat at one corner, folded, under a pebble, not opened. **The lean:** kneeling to hold a corner, Cael has the twelfth take all fourteen sheets in a breath: *Goes*, every run, one way, along the brass, east. He tells Karis, Lira and Brom (Vastin is at the fire). Karis enters it as his, as one unchecked glance, and will not ask for a second: "whoever cut them cut each one to go on to the next … I have only his word for it."
   110	- **The corner (52:105–157).** The two signs in the corner of the two-bar sheets are two of the twenty-three letters: the first letters of a year's number-words, as the old town's clerks headed a page when short of room; an office's clerk has copied them forward "as he would copy a blot". They are the same two letters as the year at the head of the skin; "the same hundred"; the figure is to be said once, at night, when the reduction is done. **The thumbnail ring** goes round the prick; "I shall ink that when I have had a cord round it." Vastin reads the oldest row standing ("I have never read a paper standing that I did not afterward wish I had read sitting").
   111	- **The lamps finding's ground (52:161–221).** A ruled table kept since the Fallow: who crossed, what each carries, of what rank, and the stride; three ranks of Iron, a Copper, a Bronze, and since the 304th the boy and "a man who signed for other people's for forty years"; she has asked six people beside herself. The same stride for every one, whole, no dimming, back whole. After Vastin's stopped sentence and her rule she tears the ruled leaf out and carries it the length of the blanket to the corner farthest from the folded paper: "Two pages … Whoever wants a line from the one to the other will have to find something to rule it with." **Cael's third page** (52:213–221): the technician's sheet lies at the foot of the second row; he does not know to which corner it would lie the nearer, and keeps it off the wool. Nothing is said or written.
   112	- **The two slips (52:225–285).** Folded small and pinned under the knot of the oldest row's tape; a wafer of red wax on the directive and a broader, nearly black one on the objection, with different impressions. Vastin knows both seals at a glance and will not name them ("I should have drafted nine. I can feel all of them."). Karis pins them back where they were and enters the day, the place, and "read aloud and understood in part".
   113	- **One fire (53:3–71).** Wood for one, carried by the boy from the watch's stack; seven bowls and five. Vastin moves the foot of his stick a hand to the left: "I am informed that the ground falls away on that side." The two sticks stand in one crack. **The healers' third exchange:** the older woman holds two turns of her own webbing apart and Oryn's hands go in under them. Teague to Cael: "You kept my left … I never had the place behind my shoulder stay so full."
   114	- **Force, dry (53:75–151).** Behind the step's corner: the right heel on a rib of rock, the right palm on a fallen slab, a body's length apart; the line found by moving the hip a finger's breadth; the thing "standing, as a prop stands when the weight has come onto it"; the ribs give "one clear line of warning … such as a cracked board gives". Three reasons for not letting it go: never fired, and a camp forty yards off; the ribs unread for this; its owner round the corner, not told. Lira: "You looked like him." Brom has the read and the thirteenth said at last: "you'll pay it when it can be paid, and not a day sooner."
   115	- **Karis's night (53:155–229).** The reduction leaves eleven years in one hundred, of which the skin's year is the fourth; "*not shown to be the same year; shown to be one of eleven, of which that year is another.*" Being believed is heavier than not being believed. The ruled leaf and her copy of the skin lie on the lid with a hand's breadth of bare wood between; she knows that a sentence exists and that it is six words long, as she once knew the length of a word under a glove; she covers the wood with her bound hand, does not say it, and writes: "*Second page: nothing entered. Not for want of a pencil.*"
   116	- **Seln on the gap (53:233–261).** A gap in a ridge, square because both walls are rock; a dry bed going in; the only way through for five miles on either hand; he lay a mile off until dark and saw no fire, no person and no heel-mark. "A watch that I can count is a watch that I can plan for." Teague will not move a stick until he has looked again from nearer at first light.
   117	- **Roles (all unnamed):** the keeper of the hold south of the cairn; the man on a bay; the people of the two barred holds; the seven of the watch (the empty-handed man; the two bowmen; the four staves, one with a cough); Teague's tall man, bow (the broad man), boy (the young one) and old woman; the miller's man (spoken of).
   118	
   119	## State at movement end (for the ledger)
   120	
   121	**Calendar.** The night of the **three hundred and fifth day past the Line**, on a shelf of shale under a step, four or five miles short of a square gap in the hills to the east, a day's walk past the fourth place. Days on the page: 299 (the ridge; the Stair's brow; the beck), 300 (the cairn at noon; the track; the hall's marsh at dusk), 301 (the east road; the dead hold), 302 (the chock-stones; the ridge; the seep; the hollow), 303 (the ridge; the brow at dusk; the fold; Seln's night), 304 (the shelf at first light; the ravine's bed; the hut; the Span at noon; the watch's fire at dusk), 305 (round the fence; the shale; the blanket; one fire; the step; Karis's lamp; Seln's return). No season, month or weekday name; no figure for the technician's date; no count of years since the Kindling.
   122	
   123	**Where everyone is.** Twelve and a mule in one camp under the step, at one fire in two halves: Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis, Seln, Vastin; Teague, the tall man, the broad man, the young one, the older healer; Oryn and the brown mule. Seln is back from the gap and is to look again at first light; nobody moves until he has. Cleon is under Treswick. Pike is at Lowmarch. The watch's seven went east along the perimeter path on the morning of the 304th and have not been seen since.
   124	
   125	**Cael.** Nineteen. **Thirteen fragments. Deployment since the Line thirty-three** (thirty-two, and the read at the seep on the 302nd). The thirteenth (Force-adjacent, from Teague, at the timbers, at a stride) is unused; he has lined it dry once and taken his palm away; he knows that its cost would come out at the old three ribs, low on the left, and has that to tell Oryn "when she'll hear it". **Teague is not told**; the debt is entered beside the first (Vastin's). He has told Lira; Brom has it from her. The twelfth has taken two unbidden looks (the west pier's lowest course; the blanket) and been refused twice; never inward; never on a person. **The two words are still told to no one.** He stood on the Span's crown at noon on the 304th: nothing. He kept a captain's left for three breaths with a stick and opened nothing; he stood inside the line at the pinch with the eleventh asking and the thirteenth listening and told both no. He drew the shelf for Teague and asked one term of his field. He counted a course of fifty-two as fifty-one, twice (ruling *hold*). He has not put the date on any page; he has not written his own third page. He has heard Karis fix the point and say the figure (marker 10). He has not touched the cord (a hundred and thirty-seven days on the 305th) or opened the dividers. Body: a stave's bruise on the left collar-bone, ten days from the 304th; the ribs talk when the thirteenth has ground; eyes paid on the night of the 304th and to be paid for the blanket.
   126	
   127	**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. No burst. The arm is an arm. She read the herd beside Teague, walked Cael in and out under it, went a stride over the fourth line and back ("It's the same"), stood in the row of five, and did not move her feet when the broad man fell. She coaches the boy and is watched by him. She knows the thirteenth and that Teague is not told; she saw the dry stance. She does not know the two words.
   128	
   129	**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. **He went grey at the shelf on the 304th and took two bolts** (breastbone; left forearm): two bruises, a week of colours, not mended. Both arms to the shoulder; he drew a leaf of shale with them at the seep and broke a rafter at the dead hold. He carries the broad man's pack on the mended shoulder. **The purse: a hundred and twenty-three.** He laid three coppers of his own on the watch's keg. His week under Oryn's eye ended on the 305th.
   130	
   131	**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three; no point struck. She has: the cord's figure at the Stair confirmed; nine sheets of the piers' inner faces; one word turned on the west pier (marker 8); the lean of fourteen sheets, entered as Cael's and unchecked; the two corner signs read as an old-style year, lying among eleven years of one hundred of which the skin's year is one; the point ringed with a thumbnail; **the lamps finding**, said aloud and filed on a leaf torn from her notebook, which is now in the back of that notebook with the line "Second page: nothing entered. Not for want of a pencil."; the two slips entered as present, under two unlike seals, unnamed. She has said the figure once (marker 10). She holds one new bar numbered thirty-four with the stroke and bar. She knows that a six-word sentence exists and has not said or written it. She still has not said the office's compass point or its figure in lengths aloud. Left palm in linen; she writes; she carried a box one-handed.
   132	
   133	**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. He read three ages of iron in the fence and the watch's habits from the grass; counted seven out; looked at the gap from a mile and saw nothing, and does not like it. He has seen a keg's hoop and five made heels at the seep. He gave the watchman's stave to Cael. The case is on his breastbone, shut; nothing about it is altered; not mentioned aloud.
   134	
   135	**Vastin.** No office; no age stated. **Right knee: day twenty-six of thirty on the 305th**; Oryn's linen and two laths; stick; he walked the whole leg in the file, a stride behind Brom. He said on the 303rd that he would not stand a wall and did not. **He stood inside the fourth line on the 304th, fifth of five; the sigil went out for the first time in his life and came back "to the grain".** He has said "Noted" a third time (to Karis's two pages). He began the sentence about the apparatus and the sigils and was stopped. He read the oldest row through, found the two slips, read both aloud, knows both seals and has named neither; he drafted nothing. He made room for the broad man's stick. He does not know the mechanism, the eleventh (his own), the twelfth, the thirteenth, the two words, the date, or that the lean of the rows was seen. He has not asked to join and nobody has spoken of it.
   136	
   137	**Oryn.** Tide; Iron, Rank Four, provisional. **Five days past the cairn on the 305th** (she looked up her road once at noon on the 300th and walked on east; her reasons were a shoulder's week, a knee, and an untested edge). She knit the broad man's shin on the 304th, asked first and answered in a word, and was grey for a quarter of an hour; she read it again on the 305th inside the older healer's binding. She counted Brom over the fourth line: "Four. The same." She would not spend on Brom's bruises or be told the count of fragments across the iron: **she has not been told of the thirteenth.** She has not heard the leaf; she was at the fire, not the blanket. Nobody has died under her hands. Three holds are still owed, with word sent.
   138	
   139	**Teague and his crew.** His right arm is in linen to the shoulder on a slat; the wrist and the elbow went at the timbers; he has been warned about a third time (marker 7). He said "Noted" for the first time on the 303rd. He did not turn when the broad man fell, and has told the broad man why. He thanked Cael for his left side. **He does not know that anything came to Cael at the timbers.** He knows what lies under the watch's hide and has left the reading of it to Karis. The broad man: right shin knit and bound to a board; a trestle's leg for a stick; his crossbow's stock split; he has said "Aye" to Vastin and to Oryn and nothing else to any of the six. The tall man has said nothing to any of them; he stopped the boy with a hand at the seep, kept Teague's right at the timbers, cut the stick. The young one: stepped over stake thirty-four fourth ("Four"), stood at the pinch, held when the broad man fell, ran when he might; he walked the seep at dusk. The older healer has spoken to none of the six; she set the shin and nodded once to Oryn. **Teague's four have seen:** five people stand inside the line and come out whole; a boy with a stick. **They have not been told:** the mechanism, any fragment, the leaf, the list, the lamps finding, the two slips, the date, who Vastin was, or the doctrine "behind it". No wall has been drilled or stood.
   140	
   141	**The watch.** Seven, hooded, faceless, unnamed, one with a Path that throws light. It saw: a big man take two bolts on Iron; a Force put a barricade a body's length; five people cross the iron, walk sixty strides inside it, and stand; an old man on a stick among them. **It saw nothing work inside the line, and no light, crack or named thing from either crew.** Nobody was killed or taken. It went east.
   142	
   143	**Objects and money.** **127 → 123** (the 300th: four marks at a hold's gate for two water-skins, meal, a cheese and corn). No other spending from the purse. Coppers, kept apart from it as the closed text keeps them: one for the mule's bucket on the 301st; three of Brom's own on the watch's keg. The reserve is sealed in the third box. On the mule: three boxes (the fifty-two sheets, with fourteen of them and the nine new ones handled on the 305th and put back), the flat, meal, Oryn's pannier and canvas. With Karis: the hide with its pinned sheet (the prick now ringed with a thumbnail, uninked), the Ternhall brass, her notebook (the torn lamps leaf in its back), Cleon's roll, the sheet of three boxes, Vastin's copied page, nine sheets of the piers, one new stake thirty-four. With Cael: Hesk's book, the dividers shut in it, the fair copy of the leaf (laid on the blanket folded and returned). With Vastin: the tarred kit case (cord, mallet, stake thirty-two, never driven; the cord was used at the Stair by Karis and put back). The two slips are pinned again under the knot of the oldest row's tape, in the flat. Left at the watch's post: its hut, fire, keg, meal, bolts, mallet, cord, eleven new bars, a sound crossbow on the baulk, a stave. Ten water-skins between the crews (two bought on the 300th).
   144	
   145	**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six, as before; Cleon knows how one fragment came. **The thirteenth: Cael, Lira, Brom** (Karis and Seln by Lira or the log; not staged); not Oryn, Teague or Vastin. The two words: Cael alone. The lean of the rows: Cael, Karis, Lira, Brom. The corner's year and the ringed point: the five at the blanket and Vastin; the figure itself, Karis and Cael (marker 10). The lamps finding: Karis, Cael, Lira, Brom, Vastin; nobody has laid it on the leaf, aloud or silently. The two slips: the same five; the seals' owners: Vastin alone. The date: Cael and Seln, each alone, unchanged. The short row: Cael, Karis, Lira.
   146	
   147	**Open threads.** Ten markers. A gap in the hills where seven went and nothing shows. A thing that listens and has never been let go, whose owner is forty yards off and not told. Two debts of one kind. A healer who has not been told a count, and has ribs to hear about. A sentence six words long that is in nobody's book. A third page that was kept off a blanket. Two seals that one man can name. A ring in a sheet of paper with no ink in it, two days off. A boy who goes where he is looked at. A captain with one arm. A week that ended today, and a healer who is still here.
