     5	## Standing facts carried in
     6	
     7	- Dawn of the 299th at the notch's head above Millrace. Twelve and a mule. File (Teague's): Seln ahead; the tall man; Teague, Cael at his left shoulder a stride back; Lira; the young one (watching her feet); Karis, the two healers and the mule in the middle; Brom; Vastin a stride behind him; the bow (the broad man) last. Four hands: flat *stand*; fist *seen*; two fingers *come on*; hand turned over empty *nothing*. On a road nobody speaks; talk is for halts and camps.
     8	- **Twelve fragments; deployment since the Line thirty-two; purse 127.** The eleventh (*This far?*) unused, not told to Vastin. The twelfth leans outward at made things unbidden; never turned inward by will; *still* / *set* are Cael's alone. Cleon's warning has begun to lean.
     9	- Bodies: Vastin day twenty of thirty on the 299th (21, 22, 23, 24, 25 on the 304th, 26 on the 305th), Oryn's linen and two laths, stick, no kneeling, running or Wall. Brom both arms to the shoulder. Lira's arm "an arm". Karis's left palm in linen ("Write. Don't lean."). Teague's right wrist in linen under the leather (one push, on the 298th).
    10	- Contract: ten on the count, neither healer; shares and costs by the count; the field Teague's; the reading Karis's ("You'll be asked."). Teague's four know: a survey read, a bearing, the four ages, "three days past the iron". Not the mechanism, any fragment, the leaf, the list, the date, or who Vastin was. The tall man, the broad man and the older healer have said nothing to the six. Teague has not said "Noted".
    11	- Oryn: on nobody's count; three holds owed, lying the way they walk "for a day and some; and after that my road turns". Has not heard the leaf. Mended Brom's left shoulder on the 298th.
    12	- Country, from the edition's Book 7 (chapters 47–48, 52–53, 57–58): the fork above Millrace; half a day south along the bare ridge to the Stair's bowl (the brow; the live ruts going round; the old road straight down; the grey stone and two irons numbered thirty-two, forty paces below the brow; the floor and its square hole). Beyond it the carters' road runs south and east under the ridge's foot for a carter's day, with a halting-ring; then the road swings south to a hold whose smoke shows, and an old track (two ruts, a tree in one) keeps the line for a carter's day more, goes under the turf, and comes to the rise above the Hall's marsh (reeds that stop where the line stops; stake thirty-three; the grey path; the hall in the water). A road lies a mile east of that water; two holds and a dead one on it; a fork at a heap of chock-stones (the left hand to a notch, north and west); then the road bends south and the line climbs a bare whale's back of a ridge, a mile broad, for two days, to a brow. Under the brow: a great shallow head of pale ground open to the south; old ruts high round its head; a furlong below them (a hundred and ninety paces) a fence of the registry's iron, one bar to a pace, each stamped thirty-four with the stroke and the bar; four hundred and forty yards and two strides from that line to a grey upright thing in the middle, "not one thing… there's sky in it". A carter's "day" is about half a walking crew's (chapter 47: a loaded cart takes the whole of a short day to the mill; a crew on foot is at the fork before noon).
    13	
    14	## Decisions made before drafting
    15	
    16	1. **The calendar of the leg, laid against Book 7.** 299: dawn at the notch; the Stair's bowl a little after noon; Karis's hour; four hours on down the carters' road; camp at a beck short of the halting-ring. 300: the place where the road swings south and the old track keeps the line, at noon — **the cairn is there** (decision 2); the track through the afternoon; the rise above the Hall's marsh at dusk; camp at the track's end outside the grey stone. 301: the road a mile east of the marsh; two holds; the dead hold at dusk (dry camp one). 302: the heap of chock-stones two hours after sunrise; the road bends south before noon and the file climbs the ridge; the seep in a fold of its southern flank in the afternoon; dry camp two in a hollow on the ridge's back. 303: the ridge; the brow at dusk. That gives Hall to brow in three days, as Book 7 walked it (121st–123rd), and Stair to Hall in a day and a half of hard walking against Book 7's two and a morning at a survey's pace.
    17	2. **The cairn (flag: the plan and the closed text pull apart here).** The plan puts the cairn where Oryn's road turns north at noon on the 300th, BEFORE the Hall's marsh. The edition's Book 7 has only one north-going fork on this line, the heap of chock-stones, and it lies a day PAST the marsh. I have kept the coordinator's ruling (noon on the 300th) and minted a second place: at the bend where the carters' road swings south to the hold, a built cairn as high as a man's hip marks a mule-path that leaves northward over the hill; it is the way to the three holds that she owes. The chock-stones are passed on the morning of the 302nd, by which time she is two days past her road; she does not look up the notch. Her days are counted from the cairn (301: one … 305: five).
    18	3. **Oryn's honest reason for walking on east** (her own rule, Book 7 chapter 53): she shut a deep wrench in Brom's left shoulder on the 298th, and "a mend so deep wants a week of somebody's eye on it" — that is the 305th; there is a knee on her linen at day twenty-one; and there is an edge at the end of the brass twice the size of any that she has stood at, which nobody has tested, and she said in a loft where somebody ought to stand when a crew crosses one. She sent "a week, or more" up the water from Millrace with the miller's carter. At the cairn she looks up the path once, straight, and walks on; she gives the sum afterwards in three lines, as a sum and not a kindness. Nobody asks her (Cael wrote that he would not).
    19	4. **Money.** 127 → **123** on the 300th: four marks at the gate of the hold south of the cairn (two water-skins, meal, a cheese, corn for the mule; the six's own victual for the dry leg and the way back; Teague's four buy their own). No other spending. The watch's stores are not touched except its water, for which Brom leaves three coppers of his own on the keg (coppers do not move the purse).
    20	5. **Deployment.** One use in earnest: the read (Iron-adjacent), on the wet ledge at the seep on the 302nd, for the length of six breaths, paid in near sight for an hour. 32 + 1 = **33**. Nothing else is opened in the movement: not at the crest, not on the shelf, not inside the line. The twelfth's outward leans are unbidden and uncounted (the old iron at the Hall's stone; the fence; the arch; the piers; the blanket). The dry stance on the shale is a trial, not counted. **Fragments: thirteen** from the 304th.
    21	6. **"Known" under each date** (the five dated entries of the leg): 299, Karis's grey stone is gone from between the two irons and a square turf has been set where it stood; 300, the hold's keeper had skins filled and priced before Brom spoke, because a rider went through two days before and said that a crew would want them; 301, both holds on the east road have their gates barred at noon, a month after the thaw; 302, nailed boots have been at the seep's pan within the week, and a hoop of a keg lies there; 303, the watch is standing to at its barricade when they top the brow.
    22	7. **The Stair's bowl: the number that she already has.** The dead surveyor's "station to floor", which is also on every yearly sheet: nine lengths and a piece from the old iron at the stone to the floor's lip. She lays the kit's own cord (from Vastin's case) down the old road with Lira on its far end and Seln cutting turfs: she is checking the ruler, not the place, before she stands in front of a fence where the measure is double. It is what it was. Cael does not touch the cord (his ruling of the 168th). Teague's four stay on the brow; the tall man stands where he stood on the southern rim. Teague gives Cael a first lesson in reading ground (where two men lay on that brow for a day).
    23	8. **The seep (how it is solved; mine).** A fold in the ridge's southern flank; a band of dark shale across it; under the band's western end a ledge weeps into a trodden pan no bigger than a wash-tub; a herd of shale-backs stands on the slope and along the band. The carters' rule is "Never be below them", and the only water is below them. Teague's reading: they lick the weep at first light and at dusk and mow away from it between; the old cow at the east end is the one that the rest take their feet from; she has turned, and will be back over the pan in about an hour and a half; nobody stands on the fall-line under a beast; nobody steps quick near a calf; in by the western end along the foot of the band, out the same way; two at a time; the mule stays on the ridge and is watered from Oryn's canvas. The pan gives a skin in the time that it takes to say a slip over forty times, and there are ten skins and a mule. The tall man goes first. **The young one volunteers and is already moving; the tall man stops him with a flat hand on the breastbone, wordlessly.** Cael and Lira take the third turn; he lies on the ledge and opens the read on the rock: water stands in a seam behind a leaf of shale as thick as a hand, which is held only at its foot. He wants Pressure for it and goes without; two fingers bring Brom, who draws the leaf off with both arms. The pan fills three times as fast. The old cow comes back along the band and stands over them while the last skins fill; they go out sideways, off her fall-line, at her own pace, Cael with no near sight and Lira tying his skins. Nobody declares; no beast lifts its feet in a hurry. The mule drinks four skins.
    24	9. **The watch and its post (private staging).** North of the bowl a dry ravine comes down out of the hills ("the sink": the country's water goes underground). For a hundred yards it runs along the outside of the fence, and the level ground between its lip and the irons is a shelf a cart wide; then it bends inward, cuts the line, and runs to the middle, where the arch stands over it; a little beyond the arch it ends in a hole. Walking the shelf eastward: the sink's drop (three men) on the left hand, the irons on the right, the line a stride past the irons. Thirty yards in, at the shelf's narrowest, **a barricade of two timbers** on trestles, chest-high. Behind it the shelf widens to five strides: **a hut** of turf and shale, **a fire**, **kit under a hide**. At the east end the sink's lip comes to within a stride and a half of the irons (**the pinch**); beyond it a trodden **perimeter path a pace wide** goes on round the fence. **Seven hoods**, faces tied: **two crossbows**, four with shod staves, and **one low practitioner, empty-handed**, whose work on that posting is to find the line with his foot and to throw light.
    25	10. **What "nobody declares" is on the page.** Seln's definition (Book 7 chapter 50): "a crack, a light"; a thing that a pair of eyes could carry to a boat. Nothing is thrown at a person and nothing is called by its name. Brom's skin under his coat and Teague's line on a timber make neither sound nor light; both are known kinds; neither is aimed at a body. Inside the line nothing is opened by anybody.
    26	11. **The fight, exchange by exchange.**
    27	    - *Positions at first light (304th).* Seln went round the head of the bowl in the dark and lies in the grass above the perimeter path, sixty yards east of the pinch, outside the line: he is to count them out and to say if a second team comes. Oryn, the older healer and the mule are at the old ruts, a furlong up, out of bowshot. Nine at the shelf's western mouth: Brom in front; Teague behind his right shoulder, with Cael at Teague's left a stride back (carrying the shortened watchman's stave from the alley, on Teague's order: "Three breaths. Keep my left"); the tall man at Teague's right; Lira; the young one; Karis; Vastin; the broad man last with his crossbow.
    28	    - *One (the watch's).* At forty yards the practitioner declares: a white light thrown up and hung over the shelf, and a call to stand and declare. Nobody answers. Brom walks on. Two bolts: one on the breastbone, one on the left forearm, both on Iron under the coat, both turned, both paid for in bruises. The bowmen bend to span (twenty counts and more).
    29	    - *Two (Teague's).* Inside those counts Teague goes up the middle past Brom. Cael is at his left a stride back the whole way. At the timbers Teague sets his right heel against a rib of the shelf's rock and his palm on the upper baulk, and takes three breaths to line it. A stave comes over the top at his head on the first breath; Cael takes it on his own stave (the yard's *out* and *home*). A second catches Cael across the left collar-bone on the third breath; he keeps the place. The tall man keeps the right with the ash crosswise, steel high. Then the push: no light, no sound but the timber. Both baulks and their trestles go a body's length back, all of a piece, and take five people off their feet; one crossbow is under a baulk and its stock is broken; nobody is under it. The recoil: the wrist (the click), then the elbow. **[[TIER-B: "Through."]]** **The thirteenth arrives** at a stride: it comes up out of the ground through his own heels and stops at the right hand; it does not pour, set or turn; it stands to, like a prop under a beam, and then it listens. **Fragment thirteen, verbatim, in italics, as his own plain words, once.**
    30	    - *Three (width).* The watch reforms behind the timbers where they lie: one bow spanned, four staves, the light relit. Teague's arm is dead to the elbow and the Force cannot be put in front of that twice. He reads the ground a stride past the irons: as flat as a floor, and nobody's. He asks Karis (the reading is hers). **[[TIER-B: "Nothing was gained inside"]]** stands before the act. **[[TIER-B: "By count."]]** Five go over stake thirty-four, one at a time: Cael (grass); Lira, a stride in and a stride back ("Gone." / "Back." — the same as the others; the test that she asked for at this fence in Book 7); Karis; the young one, when Teague looks at him ("Four."); Vastin, fifth, on the stick ("I am forbidden a wall. I am not forbidden to stand."). Four stay on the shelf: Teague, the tall man, Brom, the broad man. The five walk east, two strides inside the irons, past the timbers and the fire, at a road's pace. The watch's bow cannot turn to them without giving its side to Brom, the spear and the broad man's bow. No hood crosses the irons.
    31	    - *The pinch; eleven seconds.* The five stop two strides inside the line, abreast of the pinch, and turn their faces outward. Cael counts. One–two: **[[TIER-B: "You'll die in there." / "We've been in three times."]]** Inside him the eleventh asks *This far?* at a gap a stride and a half wide, and the thirteenth listens at the rock of the lip; he tells both no. **Four: the watch's bowman turns and looses at the one bow that threatens him; the bolt takes the broad man's crossbow at the stock; he twists; the bad hip goes and the shale lip goes with it, and he is over the edge. Teague does not turn.** The young one, facing the shelf from inside the line, sees it; there is no hand near him; Lira's feet do not move, and he stands. Five–ten: the watch looks at an emptied bow, a grey man, a spear, a captain walking up the shelf with his left hand on his axe, and five people standing where nobody stands. Eleven: the first hood goes through the pinch, a stride from Vastin's stick; then the rest, one at a time; the practitioner last, with his light let go. East along the path. Seln counts seven and does not stir. Nobody follows.
    32	    - **Win sentence, verbatim, once.**
    33	    - *Costs carried forward.* The broad man's right shin, broken in the sink's bed (his good leg). Teague's right arm, wrist and elbow. Brom's breastbone and forearm ("a week of colours"). Cael's left collar-bone, a stave's bruise. A crossbow's stock, split.
    34	12. **Inside the line (who, how long).** The five are inside from the crossing to the last hood: by Cael's reckoning about three minutes — sixty-odd strides at a walk, eleven seconds standing at the pinch, and the time that seven take to go through a gap singly. Nothing is opened. The four with Paths are without them for the whole of it; Vastin's sigil is out for the first time in his life. They come out over the irons at the pinch.
    35	13. **After.** The leg: a fall of rock at the shelf's west end lets the healers down into the bed. Oryn asks and has her answer in a word that does not carry to Cael; the older healer draws the leg straight on her board; Oryn knits it and is grey for a quarter of an hour; **the two healers nod.** Teague's arm: **[[TIER-B: "A third time on that arm and I'll set it in a splint. That's not a healer's opinion. That's a fact about a line." / "Noted," / "Everybody says that now,"]]** (the third line is Oryn's by §13; the first two are not pinned). Brom's bill. Cael tells Lira first ("Thirteenth. His. A line."), then the other four of the six, and Oryn at the irons. **Teague is not told**: a second debt of the same kind as the first.
    36	14. **Teague's cutaway (the hut; two scenes).** He counts four by their breathing. The bow's leg on the old woman's board. The boy asleep sitting up with the cloth in his fist. What the watch left under the hide: a bundle of new irons stamped thirty-four with the stroke and bar, a mallet, a cord knotted at the fives, bolts with no fletcher's thread, a keg of water, meal. Why he did not turn. What he saw the boy with the book do at his left for three breaths (a stave, and nothing else). **Nothing of the mechanism and no suspicion of it; the boy is "the boy".**
    37	15. **The Span at noon.** Down the bowl inside the line: Cael, Karis, Lira, Brom, Seln and Vastin; Oryn at the irons with two fingers on Brom's wrist as he crosses ("Four. The same."). An arch on two piers over the sink, all of the grey, no join. No steps: the grey comes up out of the turf on either hand as a road rises, a cart wide, with no kerb, to a crown and down again. **Nothing happens on the crown.** The thirteenth listens at the grey underfoot (nothing goes into it) and the old three ribs ache where the line would come out. The view east: hills, and a notch, low and square. The piers' inner faces, reached from the sink's bed by a rib of rock, are cut in courses; Karis copies nine sheets. **The row counts one short for Cael, twice** (the Book 11 plant), and whole when Lira counts beside him; entered, ruling *hold*. Karis reads the one word that she can turn on the west pier: **[[TIER-B: the bearing runs "Through"]]**. The name: Teague has heard carters on a salt road two days north call the thing that they see from far off "the Sunken Span" (flag).
    38	16. **The 304th's log:** my head line (day, place, purse 123, Vastin twenty-five); **the S19 log, verbatim, in italics, once** (it holds the rate line; its "Tomorrow" is the source's and is flagged — the office's run is three days, a little less); then my count line with the arithmetic (thirty-three; thirteen).
    39	17. **The 305th.** East along the bearing from the fence's far side; two men on sticks; shale country, stepped; camp by the middle of the afternoon on a shelf of shale under a step as high as a man, with the notch four or five miles off. Seln goes on alone to look at it.
    40	18. **The blanket (order of work; mine).** Brom's blanket, on the flat of the shelf, twenty yards from the fire and upwind; Karis, Cael, Lira, Brom and Vastin; Seln when he returns. In the middle the hide with its pinned sheet, the brass on the pencil line; round it, each turned to lie as its stone lay, north to north: one sheet from each of the three floors and the nine of the piers; at the head the flat with its six rows; at the foot her notebook open at the list, Cleon's roll, the sheet of three boxes; under a pebble at one corner the fair copy from Cael's coat, folded, not opened. **The rows lean along the bearing:** a sign that bears heads every run, so each run has a head and a tail; laid as they lay, every run on that blanket has its head to the west and its tail to the east, along the brass. (The twelfth sees the same thing, unbidden, and Cael looks at the brass instead.) **The point:** the two signs in the fifth place's corner are a year in the old town's count, the count in which the head of a certain skin is dated; she lays the one against the other without saying the figure until the close. **The thumbnail circle** goes round the divider's prick.
    41	19. **The lamps finding (Karis's; Tier A 8, verbatim, once).** Its ground on the page: at the Stair and the Fallow and the hall, and yesterday at the irons, every sigil went at the same stride, whole, and came back whole — a Wind of the first rank, an Ember of the third, a boy, and a man who was at the top of the ladder for forty years; no dimming, no order by strength. Vastin: "If a man designed the apparatus, then the sigils —" (pinned; placed). Karis: the ruler rule (Tier B, verbatim). Two pages; the second has nothing on it. **Vastin's third "Noted."** Cael sees the foot of the second row, where a technician's sheet lies, and does not add a third page; Seln's eyes rest there once; neither speaks.
    42	20. **The directive and the objection (Tier A 7).** Vastin, reading the sixth and oldest row through for the first time, finds two slips pinned inside its tape under two seals: both texts read out by him, verbatim; then his three sentences and "That's a crack." (pinned; placed). **[[TIER-B: "The Compact … Yes. Part of it."]]** He does not name the seals; nobody presses him; it is not pried.
    43	21. **One fire.** The broad man is brought to it on the old woman's staff and a cut stick; Vastin, on his, moves his own stick's foot a hand to the left to make room for the other, and says a thing about the ground; **the broad man: "Aye."** (his first word to any of the six). **The healers' third exchange:** Oryn's hands inside the older healer's binding on the shin while the older woman holds the turns open; no word.
    44	22. **Force, dry.** Behind the step, at dusk, out of the fire's sight: heel on a rib of shale, palm on a slab; the line set joint by joint as he saw it set twice. The thirteenth listens and finds both ends. The ribs warn: the line would come out there, short of the hand. He takes his palm away. Trial; not counted. Lira saw. Reasons: a thing that has never been fired is not tried first beside a camp; and its owner is forty yards off with his arm on a board, and has not been told.
    45	23. **Karis's cutaway (two scenes), the night after.** The refusal held in her own head. She knows that a sentence exists; she knows how long it is (six words), as she once knew the length of a word under a glove; she does not let it be said, and she does not write it. Nothing in her head lays the one page on the other.
    46	24. **Seln on the pass:** "I looked at it." (pinned; placed). A notch, low and square; he did not like it; he will look again from nearer. **The close:** **[[TIER-B: the S20 close]]**, then the fixed last line of narration, verbatim.
