    11	Karis had laid it out at dusk without saying she was going to, as she laid everything out, and by the time the fire was a fire the blanket had the whole of the year on it and he catalogued it before she started, because she was going to change it, and he wanted the before.
    12	
    13	The carter's sheet, with the four circles and the pencil line through them and the blank sheet pinned to its edge with the line running on across it to the divider's point. The kit's ledger, open at the index. The Compact's records in their rows — five rows now, because the Span's sheets had a row of their own, and the sheets that had never been laid out at a spring had a row too, at the end, that she had not read aloud to anyone. The fifty-two sheets of copied walls from the autumn, rolled, and the Span's piers copied that morning, flat, weighted at the corners with shale. The leaf's copy, in her hand and his, with the gap drawn. The list of parts. The dividers, closed. The two brass edges, side by side along the line.
    14	
    15	Everything they had found, on one blanket, on a shale step, with the wind going east over it, and a woman with one working hand about to reconcile it.
    16	
