    59	"That's the finding. That's all of it. A hundred people, a hundred Paths, a hundred lives spent on them, and they all go out at once, at the same place, at the same stride, as if somebody had cut one thing. Not a hundred things. One. And I don't know what the one thing is, and I'm entering it as an observation I can't explain, because a person's — " she stopped, and found the word she wanted, which was the office's, and used it as if it were a stone she had picked up and did not like the weight of — "a person's *spiritual entity*, which is the phrase, which is the thing they tell a boy at fourteen he's been given, doesn't have a *wire*. A soul doesn't fail identically. A soul fails the way the person does. Differently. Late or early. Bravely or not." She took the hand off the column. "These don't. So either the phrase is wrong about what the thing is, or I'm wrong about what a wire is, and I can't tell which from a blanket, and I'm not going to pretend I can."
    60	
    61	Lira, across the fire, said nothing, and looked at her own two hands. Brom looked at the fire. Oryn, who had read flow all her life and had said on a bench that the thing at the middle of a man was not it, looked at Karis as she had looked at a hold-woman's hand — a thing being read, by someone else, correctly — and said nothing either. Seln was looking at the hills.
