    68	
    69	"I know where that sentence goes," she said, to him, not unkindly. "I've known since the leaf. You know where it goes. Everyone at this fire knows where it goes. There's a leaf that says a man made the measure and the seat and the tier and the mark, and set an apparatus over the land, and there's a column that says every mark goes out like a lamp when a wire's cut, and there's a word on both that's the same word, and the sentence that joins them is about six words long and I could say it in my sleep. I'm not going to." She opened the notebook, with the bound hand, and turned to a page, and wrote on it, and turned to another page, and wrote on that. "Two findings. Two pages. What the leaf says, which is a record and says what it says. What the column says, which is a measurement and says what it says. I've put them on separate pages and I'm not drawing a line between them, because a line's a thing you draw when you've measured both ends, and I've measured one end and read the other, and reading isn't measuring. I've spent a year at the foot of a stair learning the difference. I'll not throw it away at a fire because the sentence is easy."
    70	
    71	Vastin looked at her a long moment. Then he said, "Noted," and it was the third time, and he meant it as she meant her rule.
