   144	
   145	Cael said nothing to that. He had the fair copy of a leaf inside his coat, against a pair of brass legs; and he had two things under his breastbone that the man in front of him had never been told of, of which one was the man's own.
   146	
   147	That was the weight of the morning, and it was all in those two yards. He had entered a wall once, at this man's asking, and had entered more than was asked for. He could not pay it. A thing of that kind could not be told by itself; whoever heard how the eleventh had come would have heard how all of them came, and that was not Cael's to hand over. It belonged to four others who walked in front of the dray, and to a healer.
   148	
   149	"You are thinking that I am owed something," said Vastin.
   150	
   151	Cael's step did not alter. He had been trained to that by a healer's hand on his chest.
   152	
