    77	Then he lifted the stick.
    78	
    79	He did not lift it far. The ash came up off the gravel by the breadth of a hand, and was held there, level, in his right fist; and the whole of him came over onto the bound leg, as a load is let down onto a prop that nobody is yet sure of. Cael saw the slat take it. He saw the grey mouth shut hard. It was one breath, and the half of another.
    80	
    81	The stick came down.
    82	
    83	The knee had held. It was not mended; anybody could have seen that, by the sweat that had sprung on the old forehead in that cold shade. But it had borne a man once, with nothing beside it, on the very ground where eight hounds had been stopped by it and where four holes had been torn in it afterward. He had not been told to try it. He had not asked leave of anybody; and Cael noticed that for once he did not turn to see who would enter the thing.
    84	
    85	Brom had turned on his stone, below. He had put out one flat hand, without knowing that he did it, as a man steadies the foot of a ladder that somebody else is on; and when the ash came down he let the hand fall.
    86	
    87	"Held," he said, low.
    88	
    89	The old man looked from the big man to the stones. He had said one word in that cut, and had been given one. Seventeen days ago, on the same ground, the loudest thing on the whole moor had been his.
    90	
    91	"Two words," Vastin said. "Both of them ours."
    92	
    93	"Noted," Cael said.
    94	
    95	He had given that word to a captain three times, at a hearth and on a landing. He had never before given it to this man. It was a clerk's word, and he had kept it from him as a person keeps his purse from somebody who once had the counting of it.
    96	
    97	It did not feel like a clerk's word on that road. It felt like a stroke made at the foot of a slip.
    98	
    99	Lira's two fingers had gone up, below, where the dray's tail was going out into the light. They went on down.
   100	
   101	---
   102	
   103	He stopped once more in that cut, and it was not for the old man.
   104	
   105	It was a stride farther down, level with the middle of the road, where the water ran now. Brom had gone by his own flat stone without a look, since he was not a person who visited places. But Cael had stood in that road on the two hundred and seventy-ninth day, behind the middle of an invisible thing, with his face two feet from it. He could not have lifted a hand there, while eight broke on what was in front of him.
   106	
   107	*This far?* said the eleventh.
   108	
   109	It had asked that of every sill and kerb and milestone for six days. It had never asked as it asked now. It had the voice of the place in it. There were two banks, and the gap between them, and one line where a line had been proved; and the thing under his breastbone knew that ground as a key knows its own ward.
   110	
   111	He had stood two feet from that wall, and nothing had come of it. He had written so in a byre loft. *Ten.* He had not understood then why not, and he thought that he did now. A thing came to him when somebody meant it, with the whole of himself, at something that could mean it back. On this ground the man had stood a wall before hounds, on his own judgment, for a rule that he did not yet believe in. In a stair he had stood one before six people with hoods, for nobody's rule, with nothing behind him but the choosing; and it was there that the thing had come.
   112	
   113	*No*, he told it.
   114	
   115	It lay down. It did not sulk. But he felt the weight of it as he went on, as he would have felt a coin in his boot; and he knew whose coin it was. He had six people's leave to find before he could hand it back.
   116	
   117	The three carts were gone from the foot.
   118	
   119	Somebody had broken them up for their timber. A single wheel leaned against the bank's end, with two spokes out of it, and there was a black ring on the turf where the staves of a barrel had been burned. A cairn stood beyond, where the foot-track left the ruts and went up the spur to a wall with smoke behind it. The factor had gone back along the lip by the sheep-walk. Nobody had been asked up to the pot, and nobody had expected to be; a hold that has been told no does its civilities afterward by leaving a person alone.
   120	
   121	Seln's fist came up at the cairn.
   122	
   123	It did not go out to the side, where a hound would have been. It went back, over his shoulder, toward the hill that they had come down from. Cael looked where it went.
   124	
   125	The cut was a black notch in the spur behind them, with the road coming out of it. Above the notch the shoulder of the moor stood against a grey sky; and on that line, a long way off and quite small, there was a man on a horse.
   126	
   127	He was not moving. He sat where the ruts came over the skyline, and it was not possible at that distance to tell which way he faced. He wore something dark, and the horse was a bay or a brown; a mile of grey air took the rest. Then the horse put its head down to the bents.
   128	
   129	The fist opened, and lay flat. They stood. A carter stood at his cob's head as a carter does, and an old man on a tail-board looked at his own knees.
   130	
   131	Nothing came of it. It was a rider on a road, on a morning when carts were using that road again, behind six people who had no quarrel with any horse. After the space of forty breaths Seln lifted two fingers, and the file went on round the foot of the spur. But the small grey man had let it come up to him, which he never did; and as Cael went by he saw the right hand go out at the hip and turn over, palm upward, not empty but asking.
   132	
   133	*Nothing?*
   134	
   135	Cael did not know. He made a fist of his own, low, where it could be seen: *seen*. It was all that either of them had.
   136	
   137	They did nine miles that afternoon, down off the shoulder, with the wind at last a little on the quarter. The moor fell away in long steps toward the east. There was a beck across the ruts in a spread of wet stones, where a grey thing had once walked beside them for half a mile and stopped as if at a wall; the cobs drank there, and nothing paced them. Twice Cael turned his head by the width of a hand. The first time there was only heather. The second time the horseman was on the last rise but one, a mile behind, coming on at exactly the pace of a dray.
   138	
   139	---
   140	
   141	The halting-ground lay in its fold beside the beck, with a dozen rings of stones on it, and there was ash in three of the rings that had not been there before.
   142	
   143	Carters had lain on it since the cut was opened. They had not lit fires; the black was old, and the white was only what a wind had turned over. But somebody had left a bundle of dry heather under a flat stone for the next comer's bed, and a crust for the birds.
   144	
   145	They ate by the same dead ring as before. Cael marked who sat where, since it was the kind of thing that he marked.
   146	
   147	The old man sat.
   148	
   149	He did it without a word, on the stone that he had stood beside on the two hundred and seventy-eighth night with his bread in his hand. He let himself down by the stick and one palm, with the bound leg out before him across the ring; and when he was down he looked round the six faces, in the order that he kept, as though he expected the thing to be remarked.
   150	
   151	"You sat," said Lira, under her breath.
   152	
   153	"I did. I have sat on a board in front of six people for two days, going backward, at a copper a day. I find that the ground is no farther off, and that it is free."
   154	
   155	"You'll have to get up again. In front of us. With that."
   156	
   157	"I am aware of it. I have been got up by one or another of you a good many times since a stair, and I have not yet been charged for it." He laid the stick across his thighs. "I am told that a road has no keeper."
   158	
   159	Brom had been ladling cold barley into the seventh bowl. He stopped with the ladle lifted, and a slow look went over his face.
   160	
   161	"It hasn't," he said, and put the bowl into the raw hands.
   162	
   163	No copper was laid upon a stone that night.
   164	
   165	Seln had not sat. He stood at the fold's upper end, where he could see the ruts in both directions, and he spoke when the bowls were empty, at the pitch that the dusk allowed.
   166	
   167	"There is a horse and a man. He kept a mile from us for the whole of the afternoon, as nearly as I could pace it against a dray. He did not come into the cut while we were in it. He came through afterward, at a walk." The hands were in the sleeves. "I did not see his boots. I should not have known them at a mile."
   168	
   169	"A hold's man," said Brom. "Or a carter's lad sent on for the boat."
   170	
   171	"He may be either. A hold's man does not ride a mile behind a dray for nine miles when he could be past it in a quarter of an hour. A lad sent for a boat does not stop when a file stops." Seln looked down the fold. "I do not say that it is the same man. I say that I told you a road would be watched, and that somebody is on it who behaves as a watcher would. I can put it no higher, and I take no comfort from how low I must put it."
   172	
   173	"What does he learn?" said Cael.
   174	
   175	"How many. Which way. That one of you rides, and five carry, and that there is rope on the cart. That you stop at a cairn and do not go up to a gate." He was silent a moment. "It is very little, and it is exact. I have sold less."
   176	
   177	Lira had been watching the old man across the ring with her chin on her knee.
   178	
   179	Her right hand came out, flat, palm to the ground.
   180	
   181	It was done without warning, as she had done it to him on this same road, walking backward down the ruts. Every person at that ring went still, since that was what the hand meant; a big man stopped with his thumb in a bowl. But she was not looking at any of them.
   182	
   183	Vastin had not risen. He could not have, in the time. He had let his shoulders come forward, and his head hang, and both hands go loose on the ash across his knees. He looked like an old carter who has nodded off on a stone after his supper. There was nothing upright in him anywhere.
   184	
   185	She turned the hand over, empty.
   186	
   187	"That's bent," she said. "You said you didn't know that you could do it bent."
   188	
   189	"I had it from a girl on this road. She told me that a post is a thing which nobody looks at, and that she had looked at me first of everything on the moor." He straightened by degrees. "I have since been a hand on a card, and matter on a cart, and I was not looked at for eleven days by a town that used to send a horse to its bridge for me. I find that I have the way of it."
   190	
   191	"You've the way of sitting," said Lira. "It's a start."
   192	
   193	Cael wrote by feel, on his knee, when the light was gone.
   194	
   195	*Two hundred and ninety-sixth day. The halting-ground in the fold; thirteen miles. The cut walked east. Water in it, and no glass, and sheep on the top. Vastin walked it: seventeen of thirty. He stood on the leg once, with the stick off the ground, where the line had been. The factor offered twenty at each first frost. Brom said no. A hundred and twenty-eight.*
   196	
   197	*Known, twice. The factor had been told what we should answer, by Teague, who took the salt up; he asked how long the wall stood and whether its maker moved. And a man on a horse kept a mile behind us from the cut to the dark. Seln cannot put a name to him. The one kind of being known offers a wage. I do not know yet what the other kind is for.*
   198	
   199	*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
   200	
   201	---
   202	
   203	It was noon of the two hundred and ninety-seventh day when the road came down through the alders, and the river lay beyond them, wide and brown, with the cable swung across it.
   204	
   205	It had fallen two feet since they left it. The lowest plank of the landing was dry. Up the bank the long stack lay under its tarred sheets, and a brisk man with ink on his cuffs was walking along it with his clerk; and when he saw two brown cobs come out of the trees, with forty coils behind them, he began to walk a good deal faster.
   206	
   207	Then he saw who walked beside the near wheel, and he stopped.
   208	
   209	"That's him," said the lean man, quite loud, to nobody. "That's the wrong kind."
   210	
   211	Brom had gone a dull red again. He kept his eyes on the sheet. Cael and Lira had the lashings off before the wheels had stopped, and they drew out the one coil that was not the broker's, and ran a stave through its eye. It came off between them like a dead calf. It was the weight of half a man, as it had been called.
   212	
   213	"I've four," said the broker. He had come up, in spite of himself. "Round a heart. Forty coils, and more laying."
   214	
   215	"I see that you have. It's good four. I looked at it on the peg." Brom said it gravely, and he meant it. "I'd have had mine of you a fortnight since, and been glad."
   216	
   217	"You'll not want any now."
   218	
   219	"I've what I was asked for. I'd not have said a word against the other, to anybody, if I'd known that it would be carried about."
   220	
   221	The broker opened his mouth, and found nothing in it. Somewhere down the landing a lad laughed. The lean man took the straw out of his own mouth and held up one open hand to the six, as carters did; and it was all the leave that he took of them, after three days and forty-odd miles.
   222	
   223	Vastin got down off the tail-board for the last time, and paid his third copper.
   224	
   225	The ferry was at the planks. She had come over for the afternoon's carts, set a little aslant on her two straps, and the ferryman stood at her rail with a long sour face and watched a coil of rope carried down a bank toward him on a stave.
   226	
   227	They laid it on the planks at his feet.
   228	
   229	He did not speak. He went down on one knee, which Cael had not seen him do for anything; and he took the rope's end between finger and thumb, and bent it back on itself, and opened the lay with his nail. He looked in for some time. Then he drew a fathom through his two hands, slowly, with his eyes shut, as a woman feels a cloth.
   230	
   231	"That's four," he said.
   232	
   233	"Round a heart," said Brom. "Sixty yards. I paced it off against her wall."
   234	
   235	"Where's it from?"
   236	
   237	"Treswick. The walk under the town wall."
   238	
   239	"I know the walk. She lays tight." The ferryman got up. He looked along his own hauling rope, where it came in over the sheave from the oak post, grey and fretted; and Cael remembered that two yarns had been gone from it since a flood. "I've crossed on that since the thaw, forty times, with my heart in my mouth and a turn of old line for a stern strap. I lent you a rope before the snow. I said what I wanted back, and I never looked to have it."
   240	
   241	"It's what you asked for."
   242	
   243	"It is." He stooped, and took the coil by its eye in one hand, and dragged it up the planks toward his rope-house without help. He said the rest over his shoulder. "She's a copper a head. Not today. Today she's paid."
   244	
   245	It was the nearest that Cael had ever heard that man come to thanking anybody, and it was a good deal nearer than he had come for a strap held with a back.
   246	
   247	They went over with the carts. Seln stood at the stern and watched the western bank grow small.
   248	
   249	"He has not come down to the landing," he said. "There is a horse tied in the alders, above the last bend. It has no rider on it. I do not know where the rider is."
   250	
   251	Nobody answered, since there was nothing to be done about a horse in a wood.
   252	
   253	Lowmarch came up out of the dusk on the eastern side as it had come up on the first evening that Cael ever saw it. But the meadow was not as he had left it. There had been nineteen fires along that water on the night before the first boat. There were more than forty now; the holds had opened, and every crew that had lain up for the cold was down to hear what the plank would say. Carts stood wheel to wheel by the smith's rail. There was a light in every window of the lower inn.
   254	
   255	He counted the meadow, since he counted anything.
   256	
   257	Then his eyes went up the bank, to the house on the rise. It had one window on the river side. There was a lamp in it; and against the lamp, for a moment, before the scow's bow touched, he saw the shape of a man who was standing quite still, with his thumbs in his belt.

