   120	
   121	He had not had a picture for that until three days ago, and he had one now. He had stood on a bridge and counted piers so that he need not look at them, and he had seen what a brown river does at a cutwater. It does not stop there, and it does not go in. It divides upon the stone, and runs down either side, and joins again below; and a person on the bank can tell the whole shape of the pier by nothing but the water.
   122	
