editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:5:The outermost was the carters' road, two pale ruts going the whole way round the low ground. Forty paces inside it lay the edge that could not be seen, where a grey stone sat on the turf between two iron bars. And in the middle, two hundred and twenty yards from the stone, was the floor, as round as a coin, with a square hole at its centre no bigger than a cart. The line across all three was the old road. It came down off the ridge from the east, over the brow where the ruts turned aside, between the two bars, and straight on down the frosted grass to the floor's edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:13:She said it quite quietly, at the stride. She had crossed such an edge more often than any of them, and she had told Cael at the fire that she did not expect ever to grow used to it; but she did not break her pace.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:31:Cael looked up the old road, into the sun, and had to put his hand over his eyes. On the brow, where the ruts turned north, there was a shape standing that had not been there when Seln walked the ruts in the dark. It stood forty paces above the stone. There was a second shape beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:45:He was twelve feet from the stone when he made up his mind. He had refused Anchor, the thinnest of his ten, at these two irons the evening before, and again in the frost an hour ago. He let it look now, on purpose, with his eyes open; and since he had stopped dead to do it, he stood on a frozen road for the length of two counts while a friend with a bolt in him was being turned about by the arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:51:They came from the old iron, on the north side, and they went to the newer one, eight feet away, at the height of a knee and a chest and a hand above a tall man's head. He saw them arrive, and they did not creep; they swung across as a gate swings to, all three together, and caught on the newer bar, and ran on past it to the south until they were beyond his seeing. On the north side they ran away from the old iron along the turf in the same three courses, and went out of his sight in that direction too.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:95:And there was one on the north, kneeling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:119:She had seen it on the Fallow from a foot away, and she saw it now from three yards. He watched her know what he was doing, and why, and what it was going to cost somebody before the day was out. Then her eyes left his face, and went up to the brow, where a man had got his string behind its catch and was reaching to his belt for a bolt. They went south, and west, and north, right round, in the time of one breath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:155:They were not aimed, or not as the first had been. Cael saw them only when they were falling, two dark strokes dropping steeply from the north and from the south. They had been lofted from better than two hundred and fifty yards by people who knew to a hair how far their bows would throw. One struck the plate ten feet from Brom and leapt up again, end over end, and the other came down beyond the hole and slid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:213:It was a plain square opening in the wall, the width of the stair. Seln stood in it with his back to the room and looked up forty steps at a small square of blue.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:217:He was speaking to himself, as a man does who is taking an inventory. He put his hand on the stone of the door's edge, and then on the wall of the stair, and measured something with his eye.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:219:"They can't shoot the stair."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:229:"I can span it. I trust that I shall not need to." He took the leather sleeve from Brom's belt as he spoke, and laid it on the step beside her. "Go up six. Sit so that you can see the square and it cannot see you. If anything stands in it, do as the man on the gate-walk taught you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:239:She had cut his coat away from the shoulder with Seln's knife. Karis held the lamp where she was told. In its light the thing was plain enough, and Cael made himself look at it, since it was his crew's: the bolt had gone in behind, a hand below the top of the shoulder, and its iron head stood out in front, under the collar-bone, by the length of a thumb. There was not much blood. What there was came slowly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:269:"Tape," said Lira's voice, from up the stair. A small hard roll came down the steps, one at a time, and lay on the floor in the light. "It's what I've left from the ankle. It's hers anyway."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:279:She stood up with the lamp, and turned, and held it toward the stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:303:When Lira's boots had stopped on the stair, the healer looked into the lamp. It had held a finger and a half of oil when Seln lit it. There was a finger now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:7:It was done as at the Stair, with one difference: nobody needed a sigil to find the way. Lira went along the reeds with the cord's end shut in her right fist and her boot half over the line, saying *gone* and *back* under her breath, and at every station her toe was within a hand of the first stem. Seln cut the turfs, which came up black and dripping. Cael wrote. His eyes had come back to him in the night, and he set down sixty-two stations in a better hand than the last.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:9:They were the whole of the forenoon about that perimeter, with the marsh on their right and the frost going grey on the turf. Not once in that round did the water stand a yard inside the line, or a reed a yard beyond it. A spring had filled that basin for longer than anybody could say, and it had filled it to a line that five people could feel in their heads and no farther. Cael could not have said what water knew about such an edge, and not one of them tried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:11:Karis went down her page with the pencil when Lira's fist came back to the grey stone. She turned to the leaf of the Stair and to the leaf of the Fallow, and wrote one line under the two that were there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:23:They came over the crown of the rise as he finished. Cael knew the man in front by the way he stopped there, with his thumbs in his belt, and looked at everything before he came on. He looked at the hall first, and for some time. Then his eyes came down the old ruts to a rope of stiff breeches between two bundles of reeds. They went to a big man lying on his back with an arm strapped across him, and to a girl on a grey stone with a crossbow on her knee. Last of all they went, as they always went, to the number of people who were there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:51:Cael waited to see how a Tide, Iron, of the fourth rank, would take that from somebody who had never mended a thing in her life by laying a palm on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:71:It had been the hundred and seventeenth morning, three days before, when Teague took his crew up the notch from the mill with one more in it than had come down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:73:He had not sent for her. She had come of her own motion, as she did every year. She went up the north water when the roads dried, to her daughter's hold, and set whatever that valley broke until the first hard frost. Then she walked down to Lowmarch, and asked a one-armed man where the crew was. This year he had told her Millrace. She had ridden down on the mill's cart, on the sacks, and had been sitting on the flat stone by the ford when Teague came off the ridge with a stake still to set.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:75:He had not set it. The woman with the net had not put her mark on the shingle, and the four marks were lost, as he had said they would be. He had sent his word north by the mill's carter, in so many words, for the man with the book: five strokes to look at, and all of them walking; and the three hundred was to stay where it lay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:79:He went over it as he went over a load. Six people had lain round that low ground for a day with their faces covered, and had gone off it north and south and west when a boy stood up with a light. They had gone as people go who have practised leaving. He had read where they lay, by lantern, and had found nothing above the height of a boot. People who take such pains over what they leave behind are not people who go home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:103:"Three days, by now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:113:They were on the brow of the low ground before noon. The four crossbows had lain where he left them, by a grey stone, and the frost had kept every print about it; and from between two bars of iron the trail went off south and east, as plain as a ditch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:115:He walked behind it for three days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:117:It kept a road for the first of them, and a grown-over track for the second, and he read the six as he went. The big one was carrying on his left side. The girl was setting her right foot down whole, at every stride, and had been told to. The small one stopped at each halting-place and knelt, and laid something on her knee; and the quiet one, whom he had needed two looks to find in an inn, had been out in front of the rest the whole way, alone. On the third morning the track gave out, and the trail did not so much as waver. That was what he could not get past. People go to water, or to a roof, or along whatever road there is. These had gone over wet grass with no mark to steer by, round nothing, toward nothing that he could see, as a plumb line hangs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:127:"Then we've walked three days after a crew that's in no trouble."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:129:"We've walked three days and found that out. I'd call it cheap."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:131:It was the middle of the hundred and twentieth day when the ground fell away in front of him, and he saw reeds, and grey walls standing in water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:133:He had never been within two days of that place. He knew what it was. Every salt man on the cold road had a tale of it, and not one of the tales had a man in it who went down to the reeds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:139:Teague stood on that rise with his thumbs in his belt, and added it up. They had gone into the Fallow, six of them, and come out; a one-armed man had said so twice under his eave. They had gone into the Stair and one had walked out of it declaring. And here was the drowned hall, which nobody went near, and a rope of wet breeches, and six at a fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:205:He looked at that for a little. It was a smaller thing than a fight in a hall, and it had not cost him a rib. A wrist had lain a foot from his hand, and he had left it there. He thought that a healer at a well would have known which of the two was harder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:5:It was Seln's hour, and Cael had given it to him without argument. A loaded cart took the whole of a short day to reach the mill; a crew that walked as they walked could be on the ridge by sunrise and at the fork before noon, and Seln wished to have the afternoon in hand. So Brom loaded the mule by feel. Lira ate standing. They went past Pike's bare plank and the smith's cold chimney, and took the south road in file, with the frost ringing under five pairs of boots and four small hooves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:7:They passed the well at the fork in the dark. It had a new rope on its windlass, and somebody had mended the curb where six stones had been set; and Cael, who had hauled foul air out of that shaft for three days on a bitten leg, put his hand on the stonework as he went by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:11:"That's water," said Lira. "Under the white. It's the same that she walked down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:13:"The mill water," said Karis. She had the calfskin half unrolled against her chest, and she was reading it as she walked. "He draws it with three strokes where it is forded and with one where it is not. It comes round out of the hills to the north and east. A person leaving that hold by its northern wicket would meet it on the second morning, and it would bring her here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:19:"The fork," said Seln. He had walked the first hours of that road by himself, in their second week at Lowmarch, and had never said what he found. "The left hand is ours, later. The right is the mill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:23:It came up to them out of the notch while they were still among the rocks: a slow heavy knocking, with water falling under it, at about the pace of a tired man splitting wood. Brom stopped the mule to listen. Then the track came round a shoulder, and the valley opened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:25:It was a narrow green place, a good deal greener than anything on the ridge. The water came down it fast and brown over a weir of stakes and stone, and above the weir a channel had been cut away along the bank to a tall grey building with a wheel on its side as high as a barn door. The wheel went round steadily in a white fall of water. There was a fence of split posts about the mill and a dozen roofs, and below the fence a ford, with a flat stone on each bank for the wheels to aim at. Smoke rose straight up from four chimneys.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:41:The lean-to had no front. It was three walls of board and a roof of turf, with the mill's warm stone for its back; she had her plank on two kegs in the middle of it, and her things laid out along a shelf as they had lain on a sill at Thornwater. He could see the whole of her work from where he sat, and he watched it as he had once watched a sixth-year's bout from a rail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:45:It was a bad hand. He was one of the miller's men, and a rope had taken it nine days before, against the drum of the hoist that lifted the sacks; it had been bound up in a meal-bag since, and it lay in her palms like a thing that had been trodden on. She had her eyes shut. Her lips were moving, and Cael found that he knew where she was by the count: at three she had the banks, and by seven she had gone along the bed to where it was stopped. He had lain under those hands for twenty minutes. He had a fragment in him of what she was doing, the tenth, and it sat quiet under his breastbone and did not stir toward her, since it stopped at the length of his own arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:133:He put it down, with her initial; and he had hardly lifted the pencil when a hoarse voice from the lean-to said that the arm had taken no harm on two days of frozen road, that all eight were as they had been, and that the knot had been retied by somebody who tied like a carter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:147:He gave it in order and left nothing out: a line that was said twice at a hearth, and a nail, and a sheet of cream paper that had lain sealed in a broker's box beside the first. He gave her the sum on it. He told her what Seln had found in its margin, and what the five of them had made of it at a fire, and that one of the five had said walk away and been entered. He said that the place was half a day up the ridge from the fork over her head, and that he meant to be at its edge by dusk and across it in the morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:171:"No. There isn't one." She got up off the keg. "You've a girl there with a fortnight's arm, and you're taking her onto a floor that somebody chose for her. I said that somebody should stand outside with her sleeves up. I meant it when I said it. I didn't think that I'd be shown the place inside three days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:185:A man came round the corner of the mill with a sack on his shoulder. He was a carter by his whip, long and stooped, and he had a cart at the ford half loaded with meal for the gates up the water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:187:"You go north tomorrow," said Oryn. "You'll say this at every fence you stop at, in these words, and you'll have it said on to the ford with the oxen. The healer's late. She was seventeen at the ford, and she's nineteen now, or more. She's a half-day south of the mill, at the edge of bad ground, since there's a crew gone onto it that she's had her hands in." She waited until his lips had done moving. "And at the gate beyond the ford, where the little one is: if the breathing changes, they're to send down the water to this mill, and the mill sends up the ridge. I'll come from wherever I am."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:209:"And I go when I go. The day that the round can't spare me, I turn north, and nobody says a word."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:243:At the fork there was a flat rock, and Cael sat down on it with Hesk's book while the two mules were let to blow. The grey had come up the notch backward for a good part of the way. The mill's wheel was still to be heard below them, very small. He wrote under what he had written on a bench.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:245:*Hundred and thirteenth day, the fork above Millrace. Oryn came. On her terms. She'll turn north the day she has to and I won't ask her not to.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:5:She had called the halt herself, an hour south of the fork, where a grey rock as big as a cart stood up out of the ridge and broke the wind. The two mules were nose to tail in its lee. Brom had the bread out. She had sat down on a ledge of the rock with her coat pulled round her, and had looked at Cael across the trodden grass for as long as it takes to count ten before she spoke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:49:And he knew it. That was the thing that he had not been ready for. Nobody had ever told him how a healer learned a Path from a count of ten, and he had supposed that it was a sort of reckoning, with rules, which took years. It was no more a reckoning than knowing a friend's step on a stair. Those banks had a shape, and the shape was hers, and it was a kind; and the name of the Path was in his mouth before he had asked for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:93:He had not known that he expected more until he found the one. In himself there were ten things going ten ways, each running into the next like water into water; he had lain in a loft and failed to find where any of them ended. Here there was a single bed and a single thing in it, which was deep, and did not wander.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:97:That was the difference, and it took him some of his minute to find the word for it. His own went along, quick or dragging, as he was glad or tired, and it had never seemed to him to be going anywhere in particular. Hers was going somewhere: it ran as a river runs in its last mile, when it can smell the sea. Wherever his attention rested on it, he found it already leaning that way, toward his left hand, and then toward his right; it came to meet whatever touched it. It did not pool in any place, and there was no slack water in her at all. The whole of that woman, from the neck to the heels, was on its way *to* something, and had been for so long that the banks had worn to fit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:131:The ridge did not run down into a valley. It broke off, as a table breaks off at its edge, and its western face went down in one long slope of pale grass to a low place that lay below them like the print of an enormous thumb. It was a bowl, though so shallow that a cart might have been driven anywhere in it. He put it at five hundred yards from side to side, which was far wider than the swell that the Fallow Ring had stood on, and it had not half that swell's height.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:133:The carters' ruts did what he had seen them do once before. Halfway down the slope the ground eased into a kind of brow, and at the brow the live ruts swung away to the north and went round the whole of that low place, keeping their distance from it, and came back to their line on the far side. He could see them all the way, two pale threads on the grass, and inside them an older road went straight on down, grown over, with a hundred years of turf in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:145:"A stair," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:151:Seln went down first, as he went down everything: he walked the old road to the brow and stood there; then he went along the live ruts to the north until he was small, and came back, and went a little way south. When he returned to them his face told nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:153:"There are runs in the grass, on the north side. They are old. Nothing has used them since the frosts began." He glanced at the healer. "I should have looked for a den, and I find none from outside. I do not know why, and I shall not guess. On the ruts there is no mark newer than a salt cart's, a week old."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:165:"Here," she said. "The edge of the Quieting. It is the same as the other; there is no warning in it at all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:175:It was Lira who found the iron.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:177:She had gone a few steps along the line to the north, with her eyes on the turf, as she went along any new ground; and she stopped with the toe of the great boot against something that stood a hand's length clear of the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:181:It was the same pattern as the one that Cael had knelt by on the ninety-sixth day, a cord north of a grey stone: a square bar as thick as two fingers, with its head beaten down by a mallet. This one was scaled all over with rust, in leaves, so that it looked more like a thing grown than a thing made. A thumb's breadth under the head there was the registry's stamp, and under the stamp, punched deep, were two figures.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:185:"You said *like as not*," said Karis. "I shall enter that you said it, and the day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:189:"I shall enter the figure. It will do the same work."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:207:He had not raised his voice, but Cael came and knelt beside him. Under the two punched figures, on the newer iron only, there was a mark that the old bar did not have. It was small and deep, and made with two blows of a different punch: a short upright stroke, and under it a bar laid across.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:215:"I do not know. I carried sheets, and I never saw it on a sheet." Seln got up, and brushed the frost from his knee. "I can tell you what a second bar is. Somebody came back to a place that was already measured, with the first iron standing in plain sight, and measured it again, and wished it known that he had. I cannot tell you why he came. I shall not put a meaning on two strokes of a punch."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:221:There were two bars of iron, eight feet apart, with a road going between them. Anchor had been asking at every sill and gate and rail for the better part of a year, and it asked now, in its thin held note: here was a doorway, and here were two posts to it, and would he not like something set across? He told it no, as he had told it at a stake on the Fallow. He left unexamined whatever might have been set there already, since he had no cause to examine it; and that refusal he was to go over more than once in the day that followed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:223:They made their camp on the outside of the stone, between the two irons, with the brow forty paces above them and the mules hobbled a little up the slope. Brom laid out six bowls, and he did it without looking at anybody, and the healer ate out of the last of them standing up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:235:"Then sleep, if that quiet one lets you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:239:The quiet one did not let any of them sleep until he had said his order, and he said it at the fire, when it was full dark, with the six of them close about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:241:"I have walked the ruts," said Seln. "All the way round, at dusk. It is sixteen hundred paces and a little more. I found no person and no sign of one, and I shall walk it again before light. That is a fact about the ruts. If I had laid this, I should not be on the ruts tonight either. I should be a quarter of a mile back from them, on my face, in grass of this colour, and I should come up to them when you were over that stone and not before. So I shall tell you what I can see, which is nothing. And I shall tell you what I should assume, which is that they are here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:249:"We go at first light. We go in file, ten yards apart, down the old road: myself, and then Lira, and Karis, and Cael, with Brom last. The bow is spanned before we cross, with a bolt in the groove, and Brom carries it. Each of us has a day's water and a day's bread on his back. I have known a morning's errand to take a day." His eyes went to the boxes. "Nothing else goes over. The three boxes stay at this stone with both beasts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:253:"It will be there at noon. If the floor is as empty as it looks, you will come up for it yourself, and I shall carry it down." He turned to the healer. "You said that somebody should stand outside the line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:273:He wrote the night's log with his back against the old iron.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:275:*Hundred and thirteenth day, night. At the edge of the second one. A stone, and two bars, both numbered thirty-two; one five cold terms old, one a cold term or two, with a mark under its number that Seln won't read. A floor with no stones on it, and a hole, and a stair. Nobody on the ruts.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:283:He slept a little toward morning. When Brom woke him the stars were going out, and the frost lay so thick on the old road that it showed like a chalk line all the way down to the floor. Seln was coming back along the ruts from the north, and he shook his head at them from thirty yards away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:7:Lira sat on the thirty-fourth step, six above the floor, with the bow along her knee, and Seln stood below her in the doorway at the stair's foot. Brom was on the ground against the wall to the north of that doorway, with three coats on him, and the healer sat on her heels at his left hand. Karis had put her back against the wall opposite; and in the middle of the floor, where nobody went, a case lay in the dark with its cord.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:11:Seln went up for them himself. He climbed to the fifth step from the top and no higher, and lay along the stair with his chin on the stone, so that his eyes came level with the floor outside and nothing else of him did. From there, he said, the rim stood all round against the sky like the edge of a dish, and he counted what was on it. Then he came down, without a sound, and said it to the room in his mild voice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:13:"Six. Two at the gate, two south, one west, one north. None has left his place."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:23:"That is five," said Seln. "He means to learn whether he can put one into that hole from where he stands. The stair runs down to the west, and he is to the east of it and a little above. He is the only one of the four who could."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:25:"Can he?" said Lira, from the stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:37:It was in the stair. It began high up, a crack and a rattle, and it came down toward them very fast, striking one edge and the next, like a stick drawn along a fence. Cael heard Lira's breath stop, and he heard the thing leap, and strike, and slide.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:49:"He has the hole. He has not the stair. That bolt came in at the top and spent itself on thirty steps of stone; it would not have gone through a coat when it reached her." Seln had not raised his voice. "He knows that as well as I do. He wished us to know that he can reach the door. It is a kind of knock."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:51:Cael had gone to the foot of the stair, where he could see her against the white, a dark shape with one arm, and the bow lying along her knee with its nose toward the square. It had not stirred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:65:There was the water, of which they had five flasks among six, since the healer had come over a stone at a run with nothing in her hands; and Brom, who could not lift his right arm, had them all set down in front of him in the dark and shook each of them at his ear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:69:"You're very sure that we get out," said Lira, from the stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:71:"I'm sure about the water. Somebody's got to be sure about something."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:85:"I learned at the other one how far a person can be carried with a thing bound up," she said, after one of those counts, to nobody. "It came to about two hundred yards." He heard her rub her palms together. "It's a little over two hundred from the head of that stair to where I'm a healer. It's a short walk. I never saw a longer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:87:The cold was the worst of it. There was no frost in that room, and no wind; but the stone drank the warmth out of whatever touched it, as dry ground drinks water. Cael had given his coat to Brom, and by the middle of the white he could not feel his feet. He walked the south wall with his fingers on it, ten strides and back, and at the fourth turn his hand met wool.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:95:"I am touching it. That is a different thing, and I had better be honest about which." Her voice was small and exact in the dark. "It is cut as the bands on those stones were cut. I can find a hooked stroke with two bars across its stem under my second finger, and I cannot tell by touch whether the bars stand close or wide; so I shall not say that it is the same hand." She let her hand come down. "I have a wall here that I would give a year of my life to read. It is against my spine. My paper is in a box at a stone, and there is a finger of oil in that lamp, and the oil is for his shoulder if it turns. I have gone over that three times, to see whether I could make it come out otherwise. It will not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:111:"Because there will be a frost tonight like the last. We have no fire. We have a man with iron in him, who will be a good deal worse by morning in this cold, and a healer who can do nothing for him until he is carried past a certain stone. They have only to stand where they are. Some time tomorrow we shall come up that stair with our hands open, to buy two hundred yards of grass."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:125:Cael got up from the wall, and his legs were stiff. He went across to the foot of the stair, to be nearer the light, and found that Lira had turned on her step to meet him, six above the floor, and was sitting with her chin on the stock of the bow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:137:"There were six of them looking, on the road. Down here a light shows at the top of a stair, and a warm man is a thing that somebody up there might wonder about tomorrow." He said it low. "Seln's rule is the only thing we have that they haven't. If I spend it to be comfortable, I've spent it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:149:"There's a paper, priced so that we'd come. There's a floor at the end of it where a Wind can't run, and an Iron Skin is a big man, and an Ember can't light her own lamp, and a Shadow has a knife and fifteen years. There's a bolt, placed to bring a healer over a line. There's a fence behind her. There are four bows on a rim, far enough back that we can't reach them and near enough to shoot anybody who climbs. And there's a night." He drew a breath. "Every piece of it does one thing. It takes away what one of us can do. It's a list with six names on it and a line through each."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:177:"I shall say the condition, since it is to be used, and since I am the one here who was trained to count who is looking. That one was given, as I understand the matter, to be kept from any person who could carry the seeing of it west. Count who that is, today. It is six people, and every one of them is two hundred and sixty yards from that hole and can see nothing below its lip. If you take it on the stair, under the floor, there is no line from any of them to you; and from that step onward they have nothing to see, which is the whole of what the thing does." He folded his hands. "You let it go again where the ground hides you. Then what they have is a fence that failed, and a boy getting up out of a ditch."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:185:He unbuckled Hesk's satchel, with the book in it, and carried it up the stair and laid it on the stone beside Lira's boot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:203:He turned to the stair, and Lira's fingers had closed on his sleeve. He had a good deal that he wished to tell her, about a road that morning and an arm against a wall; and he had got as far as drawing breath for it when she shook her head, once, against the grey.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:215:At the fourth from the top he stopped. The square was over his head, and it was the colour of slate; he could see one star in it. Below him the stair went down into black, and he knew that a girl with one arm was looking up it at his back. He knew that Seln had turned round in the doorway and was facing the room, which was his way of saying that he had seen the thing before and did not need to see it again. Three more people were beyond the corner of that door, and could have seen nothing if they had tried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:223:The sound of his own breath stopped. That was how it came: the world went on exactly as before, and he went out of it. He lifted his boot to the third step and set it down, and no stone answered, and he could not hear his coat. Far below him he heard a small sharp breath that was Lira's, since she had been looking at a boy on a stair, and now she was looking at a stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:227:He had made up his mind about the next thing in the dark. There were ten bolts lying on that grey somewhere, where they had skated and stopped, and one kick would send any of them ringing; so he gave the read the ground, which was the oldest and quietest thing that he owned, and it went out under his soles for its pace and a half, as it always did. It told him what was there, and did not tell him what anything meant. But a spent bolt has a weight, and he felt the first of them lying a yard ahead and a little to the left, like a pencil on a table, before his boot could find it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:263:He had known that there would not be. He could see the iron itself, a dark stroke above the frosted turf, and the grey stone beyond it; and the air between that bar and its fellow was as empty as any air. He took a stride, and another, and let Anchor look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:271:He stood in a ditch with the cold in his elbows and looked again. The three courses came to the newer bar and touched it, and there was a point where each of them met the iron; but they did not end. They went through those points as a rope goes through a ring, and on, southward along the edge, out of his sight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:275:He turned his head, slowly, to the north.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:277:The old bar stood eight feet away across the grown-over road, under its five cold terms of rust. He was eleven feet from it, perhaps, where he stood, and at that distance he could make out only that there was something on it. He climbed out of the southern rut, and crossed the crown of the old road on his hands and knees, since the turf there stood a foot higher and he did not trust the thing that hid him to hide him against the sky; and he let himself down into the northern rut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:281:It was on the far side of the iron, the side toward the brow, a hand above the turf, and he saw it through the bar, as a person sees a lamp through his own fingers. It was a single point, as big as a fist. Three courses came into it from the south, across the road; and three came into it from the north, round the whole of that great circle, the other end of the same rope. Nothing came out. Everything that held six people in a low place on the hundred and fourteenth day was hauled taut through that one point and belayed there, like the whole of a ship's rigging brought down to a single pin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:285:He lay down in the rut on his left side, with the bar at the length of his arm. He put his right hand out toward it, and round it, and could not tell when he had touched it; he had to watch his own fingers close on the scale to learn that they had. Past the iron, his hand was on the far side of that edge, and he laid the dead palm against the outer face, low down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:293:It went as a breath goes that a person has been holding, and the point was simply not under his palm. The three courses to the south dropped across the old road, and he saw the nearest ten feet of them go, like wet rope falling; and from the north, away round the dark edge of the low ground, he felt the rest come in and go slack and run out through the place where a knot had been, a great deal of it, for as long as it takes to draw a bucket. He had not known how much ground it held until it stopped holding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:3:The healer had Brom down on a blanket by the old iron before the last of them was over the stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:21:It took her an hour by the sun, which had come up white over the rise while they were in the hall, and Cael watched the whole of it from where he sat. He had seen that woman mend a shin, and an arm, and this same shoulder by lamplight, and he had never seen her do it shaking. She shook with the cold from the first quarter onward, steadily, in the legs and the back, while her two hands lay round the joint as still as if they had belonged to another person. Seln put a blanket over her from behind, and she let it lie.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:27:She sat down where she had knelt, on the frozen turf, with her knees drawn up and her forehead on them, and it was as long as it takes to count a hundred before she lifted her head. Nobody went to her. Cael had heard her say on a mountain that her reserve was a purse; he had watched her empty it at a mill, and at two stones, and he understood by now that what she spent on such a morning was the very thing that a child at some northern gate was waiting for, and that she knew it to the grain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:49:Cael had not moved from the foot of the iron since Seln set him there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:57:And his eyes would not hold a near thing. That was the read, which he had leaned on harder than ever in his life, through water and stone, and when he looked at his own hand it swam.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:63:"You went in the water."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:75:It was only three words. But it had not come out as her other sentences came, each one finished and set down like a bowl on a board; the middle of it had given a little under her, as a plate gives underfoot. He made no answer, since he did not think that she could have borne to be answered. She had told a mill that she would be nobody's sixth, and a board at Thornwater that she was a stop; she had eaten out of their bowl for a week, standing; and this was the only way that she had of saying what a person became to her when she had gone into a marsh after his friend and watched him go into worse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:81:He lay down on the turf where he was. She came the stride and a half and got down on her knees, and he felt two fingers on the inside of his wrist and two under his jaw, as cold as the iron behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:103:"I told you a night that's only a night, and I meant one with nothing in front of it. You've not had one since, and you'll not get one on this road. But I've been looking at you for a quarter of an hour, and there's another kind." She nodded at his chest. "You read it frightened, in a loft. Tonight there's nothing left in you to be frightened with; I doubt that you could be afraid of a wolf. So you'll read it tonight, when the rest are down. Then you'll sleep, and you'll read it once more when you wake, before you've had time to think. That's three, on three days, with a night between each."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:127:"It's not coming out of that water. He read the mud."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:131:He left her to it, since she had sat on a stair for a day once with that same bow, for the same reason, and had been right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:139:Cael had been on roads with that woman for nineteen days in all, on a mountain and a moor and a ridge, and he had never once seen it. She lay down last at every camp, a stride and a half beyond the farthest of them, with a mule between, and at Thornwater she had shut a door. She had told him on a rock that there was nobody there who had not seen her asleep, and he had taken it for a manner of speaking; she slept as a person pays a debt, privately, to have it over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:157:*The read: the floor, through water and a plate. All of it, for three counts of warning.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:171:Seln had the watch. He sat on the grey stone where Lira had sat, with his back to the fire, and whatever he heard behind him he gave no sign of hearing; the rest were four long shapes under blankets. Cael lay on his back between the embers and the old iron, and laid two fingers on the inside of his wrist and a palm on his breastbone, and let his breath go out until he had none.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:191:He had stood on a wall that morning and looked down at a spring. The water there had come up against a fallen slab and piled, and curled back behind it in a slow eddy, as water does when it meets a thing lying in its road. He looked for the piling now, and there was none. Not one of the ten came up against that place. Each of them began to lean a long way short of it, and came back to its line a long way past; they went by it as a river goes round a stone that has lain in its bed for as long as there has been a river, without a ripple, along a way that was worn smooth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:203:*Read myself. Ten currents, and I can't find the banks either — Oryn's right. And the anomaly's there. Not a fragment. Not a flow. A still place, and the rest of me has been going around it since before I knew it existed. It isn't the Quiet. The Quiet is outside me and turns Paths off. This is inside me and the Paths — the fragments — go around it like they were built to. I don't know what it is. I know it was there before Oryn, before Brom's read, before the ring. Session nine caught the edge of it. I'm not going to guess. Guessing is what the registry did to me. Observation, then the log, then patience.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:239:He put his right hand on her wrist, which was broad, and cold, with the white scar across the heel of the hand; and the tenth turned toward it, and went. At three there were the banks, close and high, and at ten he knew her, as a person knows a step on a stair. He laid his left palm on the back of her neck, under the cropped hair, and she let out one long breath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:253:"They don't meet it. That's what I had last night that I hadn't before. Water piles against a stone that's fallen in its way. These don't pile. They lean off a long way short of it, and come back a long way past. They go round it as yours goes down its bed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:13:"Before anybody on this side of the water had seen it. Whoever sent them knew, when he sealed that wrapper, what he would wish to ask next if the first were answered."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:17:"The first was a question, and I said so at a stake. It sent a crew to fetch what was not there, in order to learn what became of the crew. A hundred marks is a fair price for a question. It is high, since nobody had ever taken such a paper; but a carter could believe in it." His finger moved to the bracket. "Nobody who has kept a book believes in this. It is two days out and two home, for a case that one person can carry on a strap. There is not a hold on that sheet that would pay thirty for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:23:The fire settled, and Cael kept his eyes on a sheet of cream paper and went back, since he could not help it, to an evening at an inn's boards twenty-two days before. Four of them had given reasons for the first paper. He had given his own last but one, and it had been two words, and he had been rather proud of how plain they were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:35:Nobody at that fire needed the list read out. There was a Wind on it, and an Iron Skin, and an Ember, and a Shadow, each with a tier and a rank, in files that had been kept for years on the far side of the Line. And there was a boat gone west ten days ago to say that every one of those four had walked onto a certain kind of ground and been able to do nothing there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:97:"I am saying it first, so that it is not taken for a vote on anybody else's arm. I have a cup of barley and a bowl in the middle box. In the cup are the kinds that I should swear to. In the bowl are the kinds that only a second floor can give me or take away, and I have had my thumb over the figure for nine days." Her voice was small and quite steady. "A man with apple trees told me that I should not tell a keeper from an eater in the one basket. I have one basket. I shall have one basket until I have stood on another of those floors with paper in my hand, and here is another, a day and a half from this fire."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:179:"One rule, before it is marked, and I shall say it again at the place. You have told us what you are on that floor. You are that only for as long as nobody finds it out." His mild eyes were on Cael's. "If there are hands, in the dark, at arm's length, do as you think fit. If there are eyes, then you are a boy with a knife, and you do nothing whatever that a pair of eyes could carry to a boat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:211:Pike looked at it once, and then at Cael, and then at the sheet again; and when he had done he opened the horn pot and dipped his pen without a word. He wrote five short strokes at the foot, side by side, as he had written them on another paper twenty-two days before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:221:He got up, which he seldom did once he was down, and turned his shoulder to what was left of the room. He gave it out as he gave out a line: the recovery, and the Stair road, and the ridge, and the three hundred. He said that it was taken, and by whom. And then he said the other word, and he said it slowly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:235:"You're taking the Stair contract."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:245:"My crew's at Millrace in two days," Teague said. "If you're late back, we'll come look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:271:He had spent fifteen years as the first of those, and he knew what it was to write a true line at another person's bidding without being told what the line was for. He had written sixty-three subjects into bundles in that way, each of them a person with a face that he could have drawn; and he had sent every bundle up a stair to people whom he had never once seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:297:It was not revenge, though he looked for that first, since it was the commonest thing. He turned out every pocket of it, and found that he did not want anybody on that rim hurt, and had no wish to see a hood pulled back by force. He had spent too long being the quiet man at the edge of a room to suppose that the people who would wait at the foot of that ridge were anything but quiet men at the edge of somebody else's room, sent by a stair that they had never climbed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:5:Karis took her hands away from it. It was a strip no longer than her forearm, and it had come from a shelf at Ternhall, in the bottom of a box, across a Line and a mountain with a herd on it. It lay now on a carter's hide, on the lid of that same box. Its upper end rested on the ring that she had walked on the ninety-sixth day, and its edge passed through the middle of the ring that she had walked that morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:13:At the fourth sliding the brass ran in among the strokes of his water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:23:"The ruler is brass. It is the two rings under it that I answer for, and I set one where a carter bent his road and the other where a broker's paper said a day and a little aside." She laid a finger beside the third. "This one I drew round a place where he had lifted his hand. He told me, on the evening that he gave me the skin, that he had seen that corner once, from a hill, with water standing in it, and had never had a wheel within a day of it. So I have three things on this lid, each of them a little wrong, and I cannot say by how much."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:27:"I should know. I shall not move a ring to meet a ruler, and I shall not bend a ruler to meet a ring." She took the brass up and wrapped it in its cloth. "We walk the brass. When we come to the place, the ground will say which of them was out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:33:"Then that is the order in which we shall walk it: a road, and a track, and whatever a man remembered from a hill." Karis rolled the skin. "A ruler on a map of days gives me a direction that is good to the breadth of this pencil at the length of my arm, and by the third evening that is a strip of country a mile wide. I am not going to walk a line. I am going to walk a strip, with six pairs of eyes, and look at all of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:39:"Three days, by his ticks?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:45:"I shut that shoulder last night. A mend so deep wants a week of somebody's eye on it, as her arm wanted its fortnight, and I've said in a yard what I think of a healer who spends an hour of herself on a thing and walks off in the week that it's deciding. That's the hundred and twenty-first." A finger followed the thumb. "There's an edge at the end of that brass. I said in a loft where somebody ought to stand when you cross one, and I learned yesterday what it's worth to stand there." Another finger. "And I've sent word north that I'm nineteen, or more. It's the *or more* that I'm spending."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:55:It was Brom who answered her, and he did it by going to the left-hand box for the bowls. He set out six on the turf between the two irons, as he had on every night since a grey stone on the Fallow; and the healer, who had told a girl at a mill that she would eat out of the sixth and not sit in a place, watched him do it and held her tongue.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:59:They left the stone at first light on the hundred and sixteenth day, and for three days the country was kinder to them than it had any need to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:79:They slept that night in a halting-ring, with a fire of Seln's flint, and Cael sat his watch with Hesk's book shut on his knee. He owed a healer two readings of himself yet, on two different days, and he had not laid a finger on his own wrist since she told him *not tonight* between two bars of iron. He left it so. She had asked for a night that was only a night; every one since had been the eve of a floor or the morrow of one, and he thought it likely that this was the eve of another. The debt weighed on him a good deal less than it had on a halting-ground above the wash, and he took that for a sign that he had begun to believe her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:83:There was a track where Seln had said there would be: two ruts, grown over, with a tree as thick as Brom's leg standing in one of them. Nobody had driven it in a lifetime. It ran on along the low ground between pale grass and standing pools, and the day was so mild that gnats hung over the water in the afternoon, and a few yellow leaves were still on the silver-backed trees. Brom walked with his coat open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:87:"When we come to it, I shall tell you what is there," said Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:93:She had the brass on the hide again as she said it. In three days he did not once hear her use the word that Brom had used; she spoke of *the third*, and of *the place*, and of what she would do with her cord when she stood at its edge, as another person might have spoken of a town that she had lived in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:97:It did not end at anything. The ruts grew shallower for a mile, and then there were none, and they were walking on wet grass with the hide for their only road. Karis carried it open before her in both hands. Where the carter's single stroke stopped, his wavy lines began; and inside the hour there was water on either hand of them, black and still, lying in the low places among the reeds exactly as a man had set it down on a calfskin twenty years ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:103:The ground fell away in front of them into a basin as round as a plate. It was full of reeds, the colour of old straw, with black water shining between the stems; and the reeds all stopped at one edge, on every side, as though somebody had gone round them with a pair of shears. From the foot of the rise the old ruts came out of the turf again and ran straight down to that edge. Beyond it something pale and narrow went on across the marsh in the same line, as straight as a ruled stroke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:105:It ended at a hall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:107:He had no other word for it. Four grey walls stood up out of the water in the middle of the basin, square, and roofless, with the sky in them; they were broken down in some places and whole in others, and through a gap in the nearest he could see water inside that moved, and caught the light. It was the grey of two floors that he had stood on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:113:Karis laid the brass along the hide once more, with all of them looking, and it ran in among the water-strokes as it had run on the lid of a box. The bare round that she had ringed lay a finger to one side of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:115:"The brass was not out. His ring was, by a finger, toward the hill that he drew it from." She did not touch the ring. "I shall draw it where it is when I have had a cord round it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:117:The healer had come up last, with the grey mule, and she stood looking down at that water as she looked along a bench.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:119:"I know what that is," she said. "I never saw it. I had it off a salt carter with a crushed foot, at the mill, two rounds back; he'd come by this country once with a string, on the high side, and they wouldn't let the mules drink. He called that the drowned hall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:133:She looked along the reeds to the right, and then to the left, as far as the marsh's curve would let her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:137:"You shall, with the cord," said Karis. "Not today."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:139:She had brought a stone down from the rise in both hands, grey, and as big as a loaf. She set it on the turf where Lira's toe had been, as she had set one on the Fallow and one between two bars of iron.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:141:"The edge of the Quieting," she said. "A third time, and no more warning in it than in the others."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:149:"I shall enter the figure," said Karis. "And that you said, in a barn, that one does not stamp a bar thirty-one and stop."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:153:"That has stood five cold terms. It is of an age with the Fallow's, and with the old one at our last stone, and I should say that the same mallet drove all three." He brushed his knee. "There is no second. Nobody has come back to this place with a newer bar and a mark under his number; or if he came, he drove nothing. I shall not choose between those."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:157:"He wrote it in a room under a floor, three days from here. I can tell you nothing more of him than that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:161:Cael had taken it for the ordinary margin of a marsh. It was black, and wet, and trodden by nothing; but all along the water's edge, a yard or two inside the reeds, it had been raised into long low ridges, each as broad as a cart's bed, like the runs that a mole throws up in a meadow. They went curving away on both hands with the curve of the basin. The reeds leaned off them to either side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:165:"Those are heaved from below. Something a good deal broader than a man goes along under the shallows and lifts them with its back. That one is old; the reeds have come up through it. This one was made since the frost went, for it has split along its crown, and the split is wet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:179:Lira had not been listening to either of them. She stood at the stone with her right hand over her eyes, and she was looking along the pale straight thing that crossed the marsh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:181:"That's a path. It's the grey, a stride wide, standing just clear of the water. It goes to a kerb, a long way short of the walls, and the kerb goes round." She was silent for a breath. "There's a stake on the kerb. One of theirs; I can see the square of it. It's not standing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:187:It was a hundred and eighty-five yards from the stone to that kerb, as Karis paced it afterward, and nobody else among them could have told iron from a reed at such a distance. Cael did not doubt her. Somebody had come here five cold terms ago with a cord and a mallet, and had driven one stake on the line, where he was sent to drive it. Then he had walked in along that grey path to set another on the lip of the thing itself; and whatever lay under the lip had not cared for the weight of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:191:Karis would not go into the hall, nor let anybody else, until she had put some questions to it, and she meant to put them from the only place that she could find to stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:197:"It is the old work. It comes up out of the mud as those stones on the Fallow came up out of their floor, with no bed under it and no join, and whatever heaves that mud has not shifted it by a hair. I think that it will carry us." He looked round the five of them. "We shall go as we went down a mile of grass once with a grey thing behind us: at one pace, in file, and nobody stops to look at anything."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:205:They crossed. Karis's sigil went out at the stride, and she said *gone*, quietly, as she had said it at two other stones. Behind her the healer's lips were moving, and they stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:209:The path was a stride wide, and it stood a hand above the water. The reeds rose on either side of it higher than Brom's head, dry and rattling, so that they walked in a kind of lane, with a strip of grey sky over them and black water at both elbows. Cael went fourth. He had his ten fragments lying quiet in him, on the far side of a line, and he kept his eyes on Karis's back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:215:He looked. A little way off in the stems there was a place where they leaned apart and closed again, and the place was moving. Nothing showed above the water. It went along beside the path as a pike goes along beside a boat, at exactly the pace of six people walking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:223:It was two strides broad, of the same grey, and it went away to the right and the left in a great curve; Cael put the whole round of it at seventy yards across. Within it there was water, a foot or two deep and as clear as a well's, moving slowly outward and spilling over the kerb's low places into the marsh. Under the water lay the floor. It had been one floor once. It was broken now into great plates, like the ice on a pond that has gone to pieces and frozen again, and every broken edge was round. Thirty yards off, in the middle of it, the walls stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:231:She timed it. The knocking went away round the dish to the left, behind the hall, and was lost; and when the hand of the clock had gone a quarter of the way round its face, it came back from the right, plate after plate, and passed below their boots. She let it go round three times before she would write the figure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:235:He had carried four stones in from the track's end, in his coat, on her orders. He took out one as big as a loaf, and lobbed it left-handed, underarm, ten yards out. It went in with a clap and lay on a plate under a foot of water, grey on grey.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:239:She counted aloud, at the clock's pace. At seven the knocking stopped, a third of the round away. At nine there was a swell in the water, coming.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:243:The plate tipped. Cael saw only two things: a darkness as broad as a door rising at the plate's edge, and the two halves of it parting like the shells of a mussel. Then the stone was not there. The plate fell back with a slap that wetted the six of them to the knee, and the water rocked, and was clear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:255:The pebble struck the water over a plate and leapt, and struck again, and again, five times, each a little shorter than the last, going away toward the hall in a line of bright rings. The swell had been a quarter of the round off. He saw it check, and turn, and come; and it did not strike. It ran along beneath the line of the rings, a little behind the last of them, like a dog that has lost a ball.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:303:The water was as cold as the trough at Thornwater. He reached down through a foot of it and laid his palm on the nearest plate, which was smooth, and furred a little with silt. He knew what he was about to make. He had been inside two of them, and had felt one against the front of his coat in a chalk cut: a thing set against a surface, and told to stay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:307:He took the hand away, and the point remained where he had put it. He could see it, at the length of his arm, a greyness in the water the size of his fist; and he could feel it, as he would have felt his own finger kept upon a line of a page while he looked elsewhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:317:He felt the thing get its head under, and heave; and a slab of grey that was only lying on the mud stayed where it lay as if it had grown there. The heave came again, harder. Then the two halves opened over the plate's edge, in a rush of water, and closed on the place where his palm had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:319:It was like having a tooth drawn. Every other fragment that he owned went thin and far away, Wind and Storm and the tenth together, and the cold of the water, and the kerb under his knees; there was only the one point, and something very strong that wished to take it up, and himself saying that it should stay. The jaws came down a second time, and a third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:333:He let go. There was no point, and the jaws shut on water. The plate tipped at last, with nothing on it, and fell back; and for as long as it takes to count five the ring of lifted grey stayed as it was, like a dog standing over a hole. Then it sank, plate by plate, and the knocking went away round the dish.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:381:Cael wrote the day's log with his back against the old stake, as he had written one at another stake three days' walk behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:383:*Hundred and eighteenth day. The edge of the third one, at the track's end. A marsh, and the reeds stop where the sigils stop. One bar, thirty-three, old, and no second. A hall in the middle of it, and a thing under the hall's floor that a carter told the healer nobody has seen whole.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:391:*In at first light. Karis hasn't said if since the Stair.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:3:He let Shadow go while he lay in the ditch, with his arm still round a bar of the registry's iron.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:11:The six on the rim were where they had been all day: two on the brow above him, two away to the south, one north, and one far off across the low ground against the last of the light. The two on the brow were on their feet; the one with the bow had it up across his chest, spanned, and the other was holding his own hands out before him and looking into them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:19:He had priced this on the stair. There was one bolt in that groove, and when it was gone, a boot must go into a stirrup and a hook onto a string, and a back must straighten; he had watched that man do it once, from a frozen road, and it had taken him something over twenty counts. He had been told on a bluff never to spend the Wind near a thing whose single blow he could not price. He could price this one to a hair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:23:*Front. Back. Shoulders.* He loaded nothing on the third. He stood through it, with Wind lying quiet in his soles, and he watched a hooded shape forty paces up a hill. A girl had come down off a gate-walk eight days ago with a sore palm and three words, which a lean man had given her. Shooting was stopping. The stock on the brow settled into a shoulder. The shape behind it grew smaller, as a person does who is letting out his breath. And then it was entirely still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:27:The air cracked like a whip over the stone, and the frost went up off the grass in a white fan. He was twenty feet to the north, in pale stems to the knee, and something had gone through the place where he had been with a sound like cloth tearing. He heard it strike the frozen road, a long way down behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:39:It was like shutting an eye. The pace and a half that had been under his soles since a stair went out from under them. He let Storm, the heaviest thing that he owned, have the air instead, and he laid the lane up the rut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:87:The two of them went north along the carters' road, side by side, at a trot. They did not run until they were sixty yards off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:95:When he looked to the north, there was nobody there at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:109:He heard them long before he saw them. There was no need for quiet now. Brom's breathing came first, heavy and careful, and then a shuffle of boots on frozen turf; and at last five shapes grew out of the dusk on the old road, with a small one in front that made no sound at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:129:It was Seln's. She had him kneel behind Brom's shoulder and cut through the shaft below the iron head, a notch at a time, while she held the wood still in both her fists so that it should not turn. It took as long as it takes to say a line over twice; then the head came away, and she threw it on the grass, and put one hand flat on the front of the shoulder and took hold of the feathered end with the other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:143:"I've seen it. Be quiet." She turned on her knees. "You. Give me that arm, and give me the lamp to somebody else."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:149:"It held a stair. It's not going to hold anything else this season."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:157:He paid the rest of his own bill sitting against the old iron, with his knees drawn up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:159:It came all at once, as it had come at the stone on the Fallow. Across his left palm there was a white weal as long as a finger. He had held a knife with a flame on its point in a hand too cold to know that the hilt was hot, and he had found it out only when he tried to open that hand again. He could not stop shaking. He had had a week of barley sacks and tables to rest in, and he had spent the whole of what it gave him between a stair and a brow, in less time than a kettle takes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:173:"A fence that failed at dusk, at its knot, which was set on an iron bar that stands upon the very edge of the place; and directly afterward a boy, who stood up out of the old road beside that bar and declared. Nobody on that rim saw anything between the stair and the bar. I asked Lira what she saw from the thirty-fourth step, and she says that she saw a stair. They will write that he crept up a rut in bad light while they were watching a hole, and that a knot tied a hand's breadth from such an edge is a knot which nobody should have trusted. A man who lays fences for his living would far sooner put that on paper than the other thing, and he has no cause to think of the other thing at all. It holds."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:181:"There was a maker's stamp here. It has been taken off with a file, and the place oiled. There is no armoury number. It was bought for one day, by somebody who meant from the first to leave it lying." His voice had not risen. Cael had never heard it so quiet. "I walked past the place where that man lay, twice. I have sat in a hole since sunrise with his bolt on the stair. I have his bow. And I could not tell you tonight whether he was tall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:193:*Hundred and fourteenth day, night. Outside the stone. All six out. Brom: a bolt through the right shoulder, drawn and mended, an hour. Lira: the arm opened on the stair wall and is shut again, a quarter. Thirteen bolts loosed at us in the day, and one hit. Ours: five, and none shot.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:217:*Nine. With the reading on the road, ten since Thornwater. Deployment since the Line: twenty-three.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:243:"You walked out of the Quiet declaring," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:251:It was the second time in three days that Cael had been told so. A one-armed man had said it of a line in a book. This man said it of him, to his face, across a lantern, with no heat in it and no fear; he might have been reading out the weight of a load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:263:*Millrace. Three stakes of the weir, gone in the last high water. To be set before the ground shuts. Four marks.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:279:He had told a boy at a door that his crew would be at Millrace in two days, and it had been true when he said it only because he had decided, between one sentence and the next, to make it true. A man does not walk up to another crew and hand it the hair on his own neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:283:They walked at noon, and slept at the fork above the mill, without a fire, a crew's length apart; and before he lay down he counted his three by their breathing, as he did every night of his life. The bow's was the short one, since that hip had not liked the ridge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:285:It was the boy who asked, at that fork, before the blankets were down. He had a heel of bread half way to his mouth, and he stopped it there, as he stopped eating whenever his captain was about to speak.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:307:They were at the weir by first light on the hundred and fourteenth, and the woman who kept the ford told him the news before he had asked for it. Five had come off the ridge the day before, at noon, with a mule; and the healer had gone up again with them, and had taken the mill's jenny, and had left word with a carter for a child at some gate to the north.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:311:He set two stakes with the spear and the boy, in water to the thigh, while the bow sat on the bank and sharpened the third. He did the sum as he drove them: it was an afternoon up to that place, by the ruts, and a morning to go in and bring a case out, if it was a walk and a carry, so that they would be down at the ford again by the middle of the day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:313:The middle of the day came. He stood in the mill water with a maul in his hands and looked up at the notch in the ridge, and nobody came through it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:349:On the brow, where the ruts turned, two people had lain all day, and the frost showed him the two shapes, two yards apart, each a man's length. There was no ash of any fire, and nobody had dropped a crumb there, or a thread, or the paring of a nail. Beside the northern shape he found the round print of a stirrup pressed into the turf again and again in the one place. He counted, and there were eleven prints, and a twelfth half made.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:353:He said his piece to the boy at the stone, and kept it short: that he had seen a man walk out of the Quiet and declare. He did not say three, since he could not price three, and he put no question about what else there was. A captain who asks a question that he has no column for has only given himself something to carry, and he had three to carry already.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:355:He sent the spear south-about by the ruts with the lantern, and the boy with him. They were gone the better part of two hours. They came back by the north, having walked the whole of the ruts, with a crossbow each and a third across the boy's shoulders.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:367:Teague did not answer him. He was looking down the old road, to where a lamp burned beside a grey stone, and a boy sat against a bar of iron with a book on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:381:He put the spear on the southern rim, where two had stood, and sent the boy west, since the boy had the legs for it. The bow he set on the north, which was the shortest walk, and he took the brow himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:15:Cael had lain against a bar of iron the night before, shaking, and had watched a lantern go away round the ruts to look for whatever six people had left; he had thought then that it was more than a captain owed to a crew ten lines under him, and he had not known how to say so. He did not know now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:25:It was the same that she had made at Thornwater, two box-ropes spliced end to end and marked off with twists of red wool; and she had it laid along the turf from the grey stone northward, with its near end under her boot. She looked as though she had not slept, and she looked very happy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:43:They went the whole way round, with the Quieting on their right hand and four people watching from the rim.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:47:They passed a good many bars of iron. Every one was old, and scaled, and stamped with the same two figures, and each stood within a boot of a turf that Seln had just cut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:67:They were going down with two lamps. Karis had filled her brass one to the neck from the can in the left-hand box, and Teague's horn lantern swung in Seln's hand at the head of the file; and by the pair of them the stair was a different place from the one that Cael had gone down at a run. The steps were the grey of the floor, cut out of it and not laid. Every edge was as sharp as on the day that it was made, since no foot had ever worn one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:73:"It's stood like that since a good way back. Like a nail. No lamp burns so on a stair."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:83:"It's colder than the frost up there," said Oryn. "And it smells of nothing. A cellar smells of earth, and a well of water. I've been in sick-rooms shut up for a month that had more in them to breathe than this."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:91:None of them had been. They had seen that room once, for as long as a healer needed a lamp, with a bolt in one of them; and Cael went round it now as he would have liked to then. The roof was the underside of the floor that they had crossed, without a beam or a crack, and when he held the lantern up to it he found his own light looking back at him dully, as from still water. The floor was the same grey. There was a dust on it as thin as the bloom on a plum, which had taken their boots of yesterday along the northern wall and beside the door, and nobody's anywhere else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:101:"No. I have six hours of oil and a roll of paper; I can sample it, and I shall have to choose." She had not taken her eyes from the stone. "It is the same hand. I may say that now, since I can see the bars."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:131:"A mallet," said Brom. He was naming them as he came to them, like a man going along a bench of tools. "Iron on the head of it. A bar. And that's a book, on its face." He sounded almost injured. "Is that all? I said that a kit was a cart's worth. I said brass."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:133:"It is a kit for one thing," said Karis. She had come away from her wall at last. "A cord to find how far, and a bar to mark where; a mallet for the bar, and a book to say that it was done. He did not come to map this country. He came to put iron on an edge."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:137:"They lie in the order that they came out," said Seln quietly. "The cord, and the mallet, and the bar; and the book last and nearest, since a man writes last. Nobody packs so. He meant to take them up again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:147:"No." Karis had bent until her nose was a hand from the iron, and she spoke slowly. "Look at the head. It has been beaten flat, as they all are, at the forge; but there are two bright places on it, here and here, where something has struck it since. And look at the point."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:173:"Or he could not come back. I shall not choose between those, since I have no leaf that tells me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:195:"Then there are distances, measured in cords, as she measures; his cord is that one, knotted at the fives. He begins at the edge. *North station to floor.* *East station to floor.* There are eight of those, by the points of the compass and the half-points, and against every one of them he has put the same figure."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:197:"His stations are the old irons," said Karis, to the wall. "There was a bar at each of those eight points this morning, scaled as the old one at the stone is scaled. I had a turf within a boot of every one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:199:"Then this is the book of whoever drove them." Seln kept his eyes on the leaf. "I put five cold terms on the old iron at the stone, and I see no cause to alter that for his book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:203:"It has. So that mark lay in this man's case five cold terms ago, and somebody set the same on a bar at our stone a good deal later. Those are two visits, and I can tell you no more of them. I said that I should not put a meaning on two strokes of a punch; I shall not put a date on them either."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:211:"Below that he has a few lines on the floor and the stair, such as she has in her own table. I shall read them to her when she has a hand free." Seln turned the leaf. "And on the second page there is one line only."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:235:"A bearing is a line with a direction to it." It came out of her very fast, in the small exact voice. "It is a thing that a person takes with an instrument, between two places, in order to find out what else lies along it. He had two places. He had put iron on both, and he drew through them. And he is saying that there is a third thing on that line, and that he has confirmed it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:239:"He does not need to say where. That is the whole of what a bearing is." Karis's eyes had gone to the middle box, which Seln had carried down forty steps on his back and set against the northern wall. A calfskin was in that box, with two rings on it that she had walked, and a strip of brass. "It does not have to be written on his leaf. It is written on mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:243:"No. Not down here, by a lamp, on my knee. I shall do it once, in daylight, on a level thing, with every one of you looking." She drew a breath that shook. "Lira. I want a sheet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:273:The cup and the bowl stood on the lid of the box, where the lamp could reach them; she had carried them from Thornwater in a twist of cloth. One by one, through the afternoon, he watched the grains leave the bowl: some went into the cup, and some she laid on the stone by themselves, in a row, and those were the ones which the second floor had shown her to be no kind at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:329:"Give it to me on the stair," said Cael. "It's forty steps."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:343:He had been over the floor, and through the grass below the stone, and he had an armful of ash shafts, which he laid on the turf in a row beside four crossbows, one of which was in two pieces. Lira drew the one from her belt and laid it at the end of the row. There was an iron head lying by the stone that had no shaft to it; the boy looked at that, and at Brom, and went rather red.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:357:*Hundred and fifteenth day. The stone at the Stair. All six up, and nothing spent. Deployment since the Line: twenty-three. A hundred and two in hand. The three hundred lies in Pike's box against a broker's mark, and that mark is never going on this paper.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:359:*Instruments recovered. The contract's paid, and the man who posted it is never going to see them, because Seln's keeping the ledger and I'm keeping the kit. Someone measured this place five years ago and wrote bearing and stopped. Karis has a straightedge. She's been waiting her whole life to use it. The Ring, the Stair, and whatever's on that bearing. Pike said a handful. We have two. The kit says three. We're going to go look.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:363:Karis had the calfskin flat on the lid of the middle box, with a pebble on each corner. There were three rings on it in pencil: one lay two days east of a square with a dot, and one lay a day south of a ring with a bar, and a little aside, and she had struck out the word that stood beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:365:She took the strip of brass out of its cloth, which had come from Ternhall, across a Line and a mountain. She had told them on the ninety-second evening that the carter's hide was a map of days, and would not take an edge; and she had not forgotten that she said it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:3:The bar came out of Thornwater's western gate on the hundred and eleventh morning for five people, three boxes and a mule that belonged to none of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:11:It was the hardest cold that they had yet walked in. The ruts of the valley road had frozen as the last grain cart left them, with every hoof-mark standing up like a thing cut in iron, and the pale grass on either hand was white to the knee. Their breath went ahead of them. On the gate-walk a lean man stood with his long bow at the length of his arm, as he had stood on every morning that they had spent behind that fence; and when Lira looked up, he lifted two fingers from the stave. She had nothing in her right hand, and she lifted two of her own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:25:The row of white stones stood whole along the lip, as the hold had set it, and the water went over the sill no more than shin-deep, with a rim of ice along both beaches. Seln crossed first, and then Karis, who lifted her coat. Brom led the mule down to the edge on her off side, where he had walked all morning, and she set her forefeet on the wet rock and would not move them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:29:"Near hand," said Lira. "You're on her off side, and the water's moving. She'll not cross with anybody on her near hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:43:Cael had the first watch. He sat with his back against a chock-stone and Hesk's book shut on his knee, and for some time he left it shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:61:The cold had been at it. Where forty fires had burned when the roads were dry, he made out twelve, drawn in close about the great ash, and half the trodden grass had gone back to white. The ferry's cable stood across the water like a chalk line, with frost on it. The scow lay on the near bank. A brown gelding with one white foot was walking his circle in the capstan-shed's doorway with a blanket on his back, and Cael was glad to see that somebody had thought of the blanket. Both inns were smoking. There was a skin of ice along the eastern bank as wide as a cart, and beyond it the river ran dark and fast.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:75:"Ours. She came in at Millrace last night, down the mill water off the hill track, with a rope on her shoulder and no beast. It's the next gate on her round after that grain hold of yours, and she's sixteen days behind at it." He jerked his chin at the canvas. "I'm the mill's carter. I left a bench full when I pulled out this morning, and my own sister's boy on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:79:"A carter's day. Less, for legs." He looked at the brown mule with some attention, and then at the boxes, and Cael watched him decide that it was no business of his. "You'll want to be quick, if it's her you want. She's never more than two days at our fence."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:187:*Recovery. Surveyor's instruments abandoned at a site on the Stair road. South of Lowmarch; the first day; where the road drops off the ridge and the ground opens. Three hundred marks (three hundred).*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:201:"The Stair road," said Lira. "Which is that?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:203:"South. Past the well you cleared, and up onto the ridge. It's a carter's day to Millrace by it, and the road forks above the mill." Pike rubbed the stump of his arm through the pinned sleeve. "One branch drops to the ford. The other keeps the ridge half a day more, and comes off the end of it, and the carts go round what's there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:5:Seln stepped over the grey stone first. Lira went second, with the healer's bow hanging from her right hand by its stock, spanned, and one of the five bolts held in the groove under her thumb. Karis had a roll of thin paper under one arm and her pencils in her hair. Cael walked fourth, and Brom behind him, with nothing on his back for the first time in three days and his right arm bound across his chest. The healer came last, with a pannier on her shoulder and her sleeves pushed up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:11:She stood on the kerb, where it was two strides broad, and set her pannier down between her feet. She was a long way inside the line, with no Path to count by, and she knew it. She folded her arms, and looked across thirty yards of water at a gap in a grey wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:17:The water came to the middle of his shin. He did not stand in it; he went straight on, at the pace of a man crossing a yard, and the four of them went down after him one by one. The plates were slick under Cael's boots, and each of them gave a little as his weight came onto it, and rocked back as he left it, so that it was like walking over the lids of a row of great chests. He counted his own strides. He did not let any one of them be longer than its fellow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:23:The gap in the wall had a sill of the grey, a hand above the water. Seln went over it without breaking step, and the rest of them after him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:25:It was a room. He could see that much while he walked, and he took it in without stopping. It was twelve strides each way. The walls were a yard through, and they stood three times Brom's height at the far corner and were broken down to the height of a man's chest in two places on the left, with every break worn as round as a river stone. There was no roof and there never could have been a beam; the mist lay across the top like a lid. And the inner face of every wall was cut. The sunk lines ran in their courses from the water's skin to the broken tops, course over course, straight and hooked and crossing, nine deep where the wall was whole.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:29:The floor was a ledge. It went round the foot of the four walls, three or four strides broad, under a foot of water; and in the middle of the room there was no floor at all. The plates had gone in there, a long time ago. He saw a hole as wide as a cart is long, with green depth in it. The water over it stood up in a low smooth dome and slid away on every side, and that was the spring. In the very middle of the hole one great slab lay aslant, like a table that has lost two legs, with its upper corner a hand under the surface.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:33:He went at the first of the breaks without slackening, and put both palms on the round of it, and was sitting on the wall's top before Cael had seen how. Lira held the bow up to him by its stock. He laid it behind him, and took her right hand in both of his, and she walked up the cut face of the wall on her whole sole as though it had been a stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:47:Cael was two strides behind him. He heard the knocking stop. It had been coming round the ledge after them, plate by plate, under the northern wall; and it left off between one plate and the next, as a footstep leaves off outside a door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:57:It opened as a trap-door opens. The plate under Brom's boots went up at its inner edge, toward the hole, with a roar of water; and Cael saw Brom do the only thing that was in him to do. He did not jump. He straightened his back. He drove up from his heels with the whole of his strength, and Karis went off his shoulders like a sack off a hoist, and landed on her chest across the top of the wall with her legs kicking. Seln had her by the coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:61:He fell along the tipping plate toward the gap they had come in by, on his left arm, and rolled. Where he had stood there was a darkness as broad as a door, and the two halves of it came together on a yard of water and the skirt of his coat. Cael saw the cloth tear. He saw a head as blunt as the bow of a skiff, the colour of mud, and no eye in it anywhere. Then it had gone down, and the plate dropped back, and Brom was up on his knees in the wash of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:67:Cael ran. He went along the ledge under the northern wall, away from the place, since a second weight there was the last thing that Brom wanted; and at the next break he got his hands on the stone and hauled. His right forearm had stopped a crossbow's stock five days before, and it told him so. He came over the top on his belly, and turned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:71:He had been thrown toward the gap, and he had gone on as he was pointed, wading, with the water flying from his knees. The wall beside the gap, on its right hand, was the lowest in the room; it was broken down to the height of his waist. He threw himself across it, chest first, and dragged his legs up after him, and lay there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:85:Cael looked at where each of them was, since there was nothing else that he could do for them. Karis and Lira sat on the northern wall near its low end, with the bow between them. Seln had gone along the same wall-top at a crouch, past Cael, and round the corner, and stood now on the eastern wall, which was the tallest, where he could see the whole of the hole. Cael was at the corner between them. And Brom lay by himself on the far side of the water, on a strip of wall no longer than a cart, which ended at the gap on one hand and at nothing on the other. Its top was green, and wet, since the spring's water slopped over it on the way out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:93:He had a pebble of Lira's. He dropped it from the eastern wall onto the ledge below, where it lay under a foot of water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:101:"A person who lets himself down off a wall stands for as long as it takes him to find his feet. It took a man at three, and he had been walking the breath before." Seln's mild eyes went across the water. "And that man has one arm to come down with."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:107:It was cover, and it was a trap, and the same yard of stone was both. Cael understood it: nothing could come at them where they sat, and they could not leave it. He watched the plates lift below him, in a line that went round the hole and came back; and when it passed beneath his boots he could see a length of back under the clear water, as broad as the ledge itself, going by for a great deal longer than he liked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:139:Out on the kerb, thirty yards beyond the gap, a square brown shape had taken one step toward the water and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:149:The hand opened. He swung outward on his left, away from the sill, and his heel struck the stone and turned him. He went down on the wrong side of the wall altogether, outside the room, onto the plates between the hall and the kerb. Cael heard him strike the water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:153:Through the gap Cael could see a boot, and a length of grey coat, lying in a foot of water. The boot moved. It did not move far. Brom had his face out of it, and that was all that anybody on a wall could tell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:161:There was nothing fine about how she went. She had stood on a kerb for an hour with her arms folded, thirty yards from a shoulder that she had mended, on the wrong side of a line, with nothing in her hands. She had watched that shoulder go, through a gap in a wall; and she was the nearest living person to a patient who had fallen. She stepped down into the water as she would have stepped off a bench.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:167:Under the northern wall the knocking had stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:171:The line of lifting plates began again, and it was not going round. It went straight along the ledge below Karis's boots, toward the gap. The plate nearest the sill rose, and dropped. For a breath there was nothing; and then, beyond the wall, out in the dish, a swell stood up on the water and turned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:177:Lira had the bow up, and nothing to loose it at but water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:179:He had ten fragments. He went through nine of them in the time of one breath, as he had on a frozen road, and it was different, because here there was nobody to see, and he need hold none of them back. Wind was in his feet already. It would put him over the wall in a count. Then it would lock him where he landed, dumb, for a count more, the stillest thing on that floor, at a time and a place that he had not chosen. Storm would lay the water flat, and what was that to a thing with no eyes? Ember's fire was for things that could see it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:193:It was the oldest thing that he owned and the quietest, and it had never cost him so much. It went out from under his soles into a foot of moving water and a slab of grey, and what it brought back was dull, and late, like a voice through a wall; he had to lean the whole of his mind upon it to have anything at all. He shut his eyes. There was the plate, and under the plate a hollow, and in the hollow nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:215:The head lay at the ledge's lip beside his knee, half out of the water, quite still. He could have put his hand on it. It was the brown of the marsh, and smooth, and seamed down the middle where the two halves met; and it had no more face than the toe of a boot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:223:The water took him to the waist, and it was so cold that it had no feeling in it. He waded two strides, with the ground gone soft and sliding under him, and fell forward across the great slab that lay aslant in the middle. It was a hand under the surface. He got his right palm down upon it, and told it to stay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:229:He was already moving, since he had not yet begun to pay. He threw himself back across the two strides of deep water, and got his chest onto the ledge, and his knees; and behind him the whole of the hole stood up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:231:It came after the slab as it had come after a stone, and the slab stayed. He felt the jaws close on the place. Everything that he had went thin. He was on his hands and knees on a plate with water to his elbows, and he could not have said which way the wall was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:269:Seln had the bow out of her hand before the word was finished. He stood up on a yard of stone, with his boot in the iron and the hook on the string, and straightened his back; the string came up the stock and dropped. He laid a bolt in the groove from the sleeve at his belt, and put the bow into her hand again, and she had not once moved her knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:295:There was no point. The jaws shut on water, with a sound that he felt in his teeth, and the slab went over at last and slid down into the green.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:297:For as long as it takes to count five the thing lay as it was, heaped round a place where there was nothing; and in the clear water about the four grey feathers a darkness had begun to spread, like ink let into a basin. Then it loosened. The coils went slack along the ledge and drew off it, one after another, down into the hole; the pale went under, and the brown; and the head went last, slowly, without turning toward anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:311:There were thirty yards of plates between the hall and the kerb, and they were the longest that Cael had ever walked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:315:On the kerb, which was the hall's outer coping, Seln stopped, since a person could. He set his boot in the stirrup once more, and spanned, and laid the last bolt in the groove; and he held the bow out to Lira by its stock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:325:Nobody proposed going back for anything. They left it in its water, with four of a healer's bolts in it, and went out along the grey path in file, at one pace, as they had come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:3:It had been the afternoon of the hundred and nineteenth day when Karis sat down at the far fire with three clean leaves before her, and by the dusk of the hundred and twentieth she had risen from them only to sleep and to pace a marsh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:13:She wrote small. At the head she set the three places by the names that carters gave them, since she had no better: the Fallow Ring, and the Long Stair, and the hall. Under each went its perimeter, which was sixty-two cords and twenty strides, three times over; and its radius, ten cords and a piece; and the number on its stake. Thirty-one. Thirty-two, on two bars. Thirty-three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:15:Then the walls, and here she was careful, since she had been greedy that morning and knew it. She had eleven sheets from the Fallow, where the bands ran round the feet of eleven stones, and twenty-nine from a room under the Stair. She had twelve from the hall. That made fifty-two, and the last twelve were all from one wall, rubbed upside down in an hour, by a person who was being held by the belt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:21:She had seen it. It had been a hand below her chin for a quarter of an hour, on a wall above a spring: the line that stood by itself, with its small divisions, as it stood between the courses under the Stair. She had counted those divisions there against her short cord, twice, and they had given her the radius of the circle. Here she had had a pencil in one hand and her own weight on the other, and no cord at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:35:She had her own reach for each of the three, in cords. What she wanted beside it was the other man's: the figure that a surveyor had gone over twice with his pencil, in a room under a floor, until it cut the paper. She had heard it read out once. She had written it on her cuff, in the dark of that room, and the cuff had since been in a marsh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:51:"Then he found what I found, at the Stair. I should like it entered beside mine that he did, and by what."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:61:Then the middles. She had stood on all three. One was the centre of a white floor with eleven stones about it, and one was a square hole with a stair in it, and one was a spring. She made a dot in each ring with the point of her pencil, as small as she could make it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:67:She did not trust that, and she set about breaking it. A straight thing will lie through any two marks on a skin, and so two of those dots proved nothing whatever; the whole question was the third. She moved the brass. She laid it through the Fallow's dot and the hall's, and looked at the Stair's: it sat under the edge. She laid it through the Stair's and the hall's, and the Fallow's sat under the edge. She took her pencil and made each dot again a pencil's breadth to one side, which was as far out as she believed that she could be on a map of days, and laid the brass a fourth time, and a fifth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:71:And she had not chosen the third. She had laid that edge on two rings at a grey stone, in daylight, with five people looking, before any one of them had seen a reed. It had run off into a corner of the skin where a man once lifted his hand. They had walked along it for three days, in a strip a mile wide, and had come over a rise onto a marsh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:111:"That is an inference about an order," said Karis. "It says nothing of what is being ordered. I shall enter the order."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:121:"Three days out along it," he said. "No farther. If there's nothing under the brass by the third dusk, we turn for Lowmarch, and I'll say so then without anybody having to ask me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:145:"See that you do." She looked past him, at the hide. "There's a road a mile east of this water; I saw it from the rise. That quiet one says that it lies under your brass for a day, and that it forks. The left hand goes up through a gap, north and west, to Thornwater, in four of that carter's ticks." She took up the rope. "So I walk your road for a day, since it's mine as well. Where it forks, I'm the route."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:149:Cael was the last to leave the grey stone. He looked down the ruts to the reeds, and along the pale straight path, to four walls standing in water with the morning in them. A thing lay under those walls with four bolts in it. It would go its round again, he supposed, once in a quarter of an hour, when it was able; and no carter would ever know that anybody had been in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:155:They struck the road an hour from the marsh, and it rang under them. Every rut had a spine of ice in it. The pools on either hand had shut in the night, and the pale grass stood stiff and white to the knee, and their breath went up from eight mouths, counting the mules', and hung behind them along the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:195:"A quiet in there," said Brom slowly. "Under the noise. I said that. On a bench, with my hand on you, when there were only three of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:207:"Then I shall not ask where it goes in the box until you tell me that it goes." She put her bowl down. "I have nothing to measure it with, and I am saying so before anybody supposes that I have."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:215:The healer ate out of the sixth, standing, with her back to the gable and her eyes on the road north.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:3:They came to the fork two hours after sunrise on the hundred and twenty-second day, and the healer stopped the grey mule in the middle of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:5:It was a plain enough place. The road divided at a heap of chock-stones that carters had left, and the right hand went on south and east under the foot of a long bare ridge. The left climbed away toward a notch between two hills, with the frost lying blue in it, and beyond the notch there was nothing to be seen but sky.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:19:"All eight. They've taken no harm from a stair or a wall, which is more than you deserved of them." She drew the wool up and tied her own knot, high, with the hand at the collar-bone. "It holds nothing. I said first snow, near enough, and I'll say when; you'll not say it for me because a flake's come down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:31:"I'm three weeks late there, and three weeks is three weeks, and there's an old woman at Thornwater whose knee I said I'd see, and I said it after the thaw."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:49:She shifted the rope on her shoulder. She was looking up at the notch, and her face was as tired and as level as he had ever seen it; and whatever had given under her at a marsh's edge, three days before, had been put back where she kept it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:51:"Find me at Thornwater," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:63:*Hundred and twenty-second day, the fork. Oryn north. On her terms. I didn't ask. Five on the bearing.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:111:It was true, and he let it stand; and the other thing was in the same coat with it, and had been all morning. They were walking along a ruler. Three floors lay in a row, as stones lie along a path; and when he looked ahead down the ridge, to where the strip went on into the blue, he was more afraid of what was under the brass than he had been of a thing in a marsh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:119:The ridge ended there. It broke off in a long brow, and under the brow a valley began: a great shallow head of pale ground, scooped out of the hills as a bowl is scooped, and open toward the south. The light was going. What there was of it lay level across the grass and showed him the size of the place, which he could not at first believe. Seln gave it one word, as he gave everything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:149:She went down from the ruts by herself, on the whole of her foot, counting under her breath, and he counted with her. The line had lain forty paces inside the ruts at the Stair, and the ruts at the Fallow had kept forty yards. She passed forty with the iron still a long way below her. She passed a hundred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:153:"The iron marks it," she said. "I'm not going over."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:157:"Whoever drove a cart round this one kept a furlong off." Lira had not moved from where she stood. "The iron tells us where to stop. Without it you'd be well down the hill before you thought to look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:165:It was iron, of the registry's pattern.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:167:He had seen four such stakes. There had been one at a grey stone on the Fallow, and two at the Stair with a road between, and one at the track's end beside a marsh. Each had stood alone at an edge where a cord had been laid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:169:These did not stand alone. Anchor had known it before his eyes did; it had asked at two bars with an old road between them, ten days ago, and here it asked at every pace, in its thin held note, as far as he could hear. There was a bar at his right knee, a hand's length clear of the turf, and another a pace beyond it, and another a pace beyond that. They went on so in both directions, one to a pace, round the head of that valley, for as far as he could see iron against the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:185:Karis had her lamp lit by then. She had set a point on its wick with her own hand, outside that line, and she had the long cord laid along the feet of the bars with Cael at its far end. There was no call for anybody's foot; the line was marked at every pace in iron.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:195:Her voice was quite steady. She opened her notebook against the lamp, and did the sum in it, with her lips moving. She had said two hundred of the Stair, by her eye, and had been twenty short and a stride; and he saw the figure that she wrote now, which was four hundred and forty yards, and two strides, from a line that she had not crossed to a middle that she had not seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:203:"There was a sheet with two," he said, after a breath. "I told you that I could not place it. A marsh three days behind us, and this: I can place it now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:205:Lira was standing a pace above the iron, where she had stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:209:It was the question, and all five of them knew it. A line half this size had put out five sigils at a stride and given them back at a stride, the same, with nothing lost. Would one of twice the size give them back? There was no cord that could tell her that. Somebody would have to walk over that iron with a Path in her and come out again; and the one person who could have stood at the fence with her sleeves up was two days gone through a gap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:213:"I have not a single sheet of this place," said Karis. "I should like that entered beside whatever is ruled. I want it as I wanted the Stair. I am not going to say that I would go alone, since I have learned what that sentence costs other people."

