CHAPTER 50
3:They had the day in that room by the colour of one small square.
29:It took him the better part of the forenoon. He did not hurry. A tick came, and a skittering, and then nothing for as long as it takes to walk a quarter of a mile; and then another. Some fell short and rang on the far side of the roof, and one struck so near the hole that they heard it skate past the lip.
33:The square had gone from grey to white. It was near noon, and bright up there, and a little of the brightness came down as far as the tenth step and lay on it like spilt milk.
67:"Two swallows apiece at noon," he said. "And I'm keeping two full, shut, for the top. I'll not have us get out of this hole and find that we've nothing to walk on."
85:"I learned at the other one how far a person can be carried with a thing bound up," she said, after one of those counts, to nobody. "It came to about two hundred yards." He heard her rub her palms together. "It's a little over two hundred from the head of that stair to where I'm a healer. It's a short walk. I never saw a longer."
105:It was Seln who said what the day was for. He waited until the square had begun to lose its white, and then he spoke from the doorway to the whole of the room.
107:"I have had since this morning to read it, and you should have my reading. The bolt she read for herself, on the road, and she read it rightly. A crew with a hurt man and a healer at the stone might have fought its way back to her. A crew with a hurt man and a healer who has no hands will sit still." His mild voice did not alter. "There are six of them, for a rim of sixteen hundred paces. That is a quarter of a mile to each station, and nobody to spare, which is why they have not come down; if one leaves his place, there is a gap in the dish that five people might run for. They will not come in, and they do not need to."
111:"Because there will be a frost tonight like the last. We have no fire. We have a man with iron in him, who will be a good deal worse by morning in this cold, and a healer who can do nothing for him until he is carried past a certain stone. They have only to stand where they are. Some time tomorrow we shall come up that stair with our hands open, to buy two hundred yards of grass."
131:"On that road, this morning. I watched you. Every one of them was up in you, and you put them all down. And you've sat in this hole since with fire in your hand that'd warm him, and you've not lit so much as a wick."
137:"There were six of them looking, on the road. Down here a light shows at the top of a stair, and a warm man is a thing that somebody up there might wonder about tomorrow." He said it low. "Seln's rule is the only thing we have that they haven't. If I spend it to be comfortable, I've spent it."
145:He had been at it since the morning, as he went at anything: a thing at a time, from the beginning, leaving nothing out. He did not have an answer. But he could state the whole of the question, and he had learned at a healer's table that this was most of the way.
149:"There's a paper, priced so that we'd come. There's a floor at the end of it where a Wind can't run, and an Iron Skin is a big man, and an Ember can't light her own lamp, and a Shadow has a knife and fifteen years. There's a bolt, placed to bring a healer over a line. There's a fence behind her. There are four bows on a rim, far enough back that we can't reach them and near enough to shoot anybody who climbs. And there's a night." He drew a breath. "Every piece of it does one thing. It takes away what one of us can do. It's a list with six names on it and a line through each."
153:"No. That's the part I've been all day finding. It's got six. It thinks the floor did the same thing to all six of us."
165:"There's the one that I keep shut. It was made for not being seen. And there's the one that I had from the hand on the Ostrand road." He looked toward the place where the healer was. "I was inside a thing like that fence once, with nineteen points round me. I let it go from where it touched me, and the whole of it came down like a row of stitches. I've done that one time."
169:"I don't know. I tried it a second time, on a doorway, with Lira on the far side, and I couldn't get hold. I could see it and I couldn't reach it; seeing isn't reach. I'd have to find where this one's made fast, and get my hand on that place, from the inside. I can't see any of it from farther than ten feet." He made himself finish. "And it's better than two hundred yards from here, with six of them looking down."
173:"Then you go up alone," said Seln. "At dusk. With the one that you were given."
177:"I shall say the condition, since it is to be used, and since I am the one here who was trained to count who is looking. That one was given, as I understand the matter, to be kept from any person who could carry the seeing of it west. Count who that is, today. It is six people, and every one of them is two hundred and sixty yards from that hole and can see nothing below its lip. If you take it on the stair, under the floor, there is no line from any of them to you; and from that step onward they have nothing to see, which is the whole of what the thing does." He folded his hands. "You let it go again where the ground hides you. Then what they have is a fence that failed, and a boy getting up out of a ditch."
181:"After, he is to do everything that he has forbidden himself all day, as loudly as he can, on the far side of that stone. Their whole plan is that nobody on this ground has a Path. The instant that one of us stands outside the fence with something in his hands, and the fence is down behind him, there is no plan. Five more of us may walk out and be what we are." Seln's head turned toward Cael. "They were sent for a case. They were not sent to fight in the dark for it. I know what my own orders would have been, and so do they."
183:"It comes at dusk," said Cael. "It always has. I'll go when the square's nearly gone."
203:He turned to the stair, and Lira's fingers had closed on his sleeve. He had a good deal that he wished to tell her, about a road that morning and an arm against a wall; and he had got as far as drawing breath for it when she shook her head, once, against the grey.
219:It was dusk. Shadow had been lifting in him for an hour, as it lifted at every dusk, like a tide coming up a beach. He had told it no on the fifth evening past the Line, and at a ford with the light going, and on a gate-walk; he had refused it on something over a hundred evenings, and let it have him once, for ten strides.
233:Then there was frozen grass under him, and the old road.
235:He took the southern of its two ruts, since that was the side that the newer bar stood on. It was a trough a foot deep and as wide as a wheel, with turf grown down into it, and it ran straight up the fall of the ground to a stone that he could not yet see. He had a hundred and ninety yards of it to walk.
239:He had been waiting for it. A thing made for hiding costs from the moment that a person wakes; he had paid that on every day since it came to him, by carrying it shut. To have it open was a different bill. It began in the ends of his fingers, as it had begun in a lane on the Fallow, and it was not the frost, since it came from inside. At fifty strides he could not feel his nails; at a hundred his hands hung at the ends of his arms like things that he was carrying for somebody, and the cold had gone into his wrists.
241:He looked up once, at a hundred and twenty.
249:At a hundred and fifty there was a noise that nearly stopped that heart altogether.
251:It was the grey mule. She was hobbled above the stone with the brown one, and she had put up her head and brayed at the dusk, long and rusty, as she had no doubt brayed at every dusk of her life. The brown mule did not bray. She had turned her ears, both together, down the old road. She was looking straight at the place where he stood.
255:It looked at them for as long as it takes to count five, and saw a mule that was making a noise and a mule that was not, and three boxes under a blanket. Then the hood came round again to the floor, and to the square hole in the middle of it, where nothing had stirred all day.
267:There were three courses across the old road, at the knee and the chest and over the head, exactly as he had seen them thrown in the morning; they were as thick as his wrist, and they had not slackened in a day. He went along them with that other sight to the newer bar, where he had watched them arrive, and looked for the place where they were made fast.
273:He had got it wrong. He had watched three lines fly across a road toward a bar, and had taken the bar for the end of them. A rope is not made fast where it lands; it is made fast where the hauling starts.
277:The old bar stood eight feet away across the grown-over road, under its five cold terms of rust. He was eleven feet from it, perhaps, where he stood, and at that distance he could make out only that there was something on it. He climbed out of the southern rut, and crossed the crown of the old road on his hands and knees, since the turf there stood a foot higher and he did not trust the thing that hid him to hide him against the sky; and he let himself down into the northern rut.
281:It was on the far side of the iron, the side toward the brow, a hand above the turf, and he saw it through the bar, as a person sees a lamp through his own fingers. It was a single point, as big as a fist. Three courses came into it from the south, across the road; and three came into it from the north, round the whole of that great circle, the other end of the same rope. Nothing came out. Everything that held six people in a low place on the hundred and fourteenth day was hauled taut through that one point and belayed there, like the whole of a ship's rigging brought down to a single pin.
293:It went as a breath goes that a person has been holding, and the point was simply not under his palm. The three courses to the south dropped across the old road, and he saw the nearest ten feet of them go, like wet rope falling; and from the north, away round the dark edge of the low ground, he felt the rest come in and go slack and run out through the place where a knot had been, a great deal of it, for as long as it takes to draw a bucket. He had not known how much ground it held until it stopped holding.
CHAPTER 51
5:It went out of him as it had come. The sound of his own breath returned, loud and ragged, and after it the creak of frozen grass under his hip and the grey mule shifting her feet above the stone. He was a boy lying in an old rut at dusk, under the turf's lip, where nobody on that brow could have seen more of him than a shoulder; and whatever they had looked at for the last quarter of an hour, it had not been him.
11:The six on the rim were where they had been all day: two on the brow above him, two away to the south, one north, and one far off across the low ground against the last of the light. The two on the brow were on their feet; the one with the bow had it up across his chest, spanned, and the other was holding his own hands out before him and looking into them.
15:He did it slowly, since he wished it seen. He set his boots down on the outside of a line that five people could not cross that morning, and turned his face up the old road to the brow, and stood there with his arms hanging. It was forty paces. In that light he was a dark upright thing against paler grass, and the man above him had hit a shoulder at five paces more.
19:He had priced this on the stair. There was one bolt in that groove, and when it was gone, a boot must go into a stirrup and a hook onto a string, and a back must straighten; he had watched that man do it once, from a frozen road, and it had taken him something over twenty counts. He had been told on a bluff never to spend the Wind near a thing whose single blow he could not price. He could price this one to a hair.
23:*Front. Back. Shoulders.* He loaded nothing on the third. He stood through it, with Wind lying quiet in his soles, and he watched a hooded shape forty paces up a hill. A girl had come down off a gate-walk eight days ago with a sore palm and three words, which a lean man had given her. Shooting was stopping. The stock on the brow settled into a shoulder. The shape behind it grew smaller, as a person does who is letting out his breath. And then it was entirely still.
27:The air cracked like a whip over the stone, and the frost went up off the grass in a white fan. He was twenty feet to the north, in pale stems to the knee, and something had gone through the place where he had been with a sound like cloth tearing. He heard it strike the frozen road, a long way down behind him.
35:He went up the slope on a slant, toward the old road, with the frozen grass breaking under him and forty paces of hill before him. He counted as he ran, and it was the bowman's count that he kept and not his own: at seven the hook was on the string, and at eleven the back was beginning to come up.
41:It was a corridor as long as a cart and team, set down in that trough with its high end at his feet and its low end up the hill, and everything in it went one way at once. Frost went, and the seed that hung heavy on the grass at either lip, and chaff, and the dust of a hundred dry days; it went up that channel in a grey flood and burst out over the brow, twelve yards on, into two hoods.
57:There was only one road open to it. The blow went down the bone into his shoulder, and down his spine, and through both his heels into the hill; and the hill took it. The stock stopped against his arm as it would have stopped against a gatepost. He felt it ring from the wrist to the elbow, and he knew that he would be paying for that for a week.
71:They were a man's hands, broad, and short in the finger. That was all that he was ever to know of their owner. There was a pale band round the third finger of the left, where a ring had been taken off before the work began.
87:The two of them went north along the carters' road, side by side, at a trot. They did not run until they were sixty yards off.
91:The lag had gone out of him. He stood on the brow in the last of the dusk, with a point of fire at the end of his arm where anybody on that rim could see it, and he looked for the other four.
93:To the south there were two shapes still. One of them, the one that had stood all day with its arms folded, raised a single arm above its hood and let it fall. Then both stooped, and straightened, and went away over the rim to the south, with nothing in their hands.
97:The west was the last. The sky there was green yet, low down, and the rim stood against it as black as a cut edge. A small upright shape had stood on that edge since first light and had never once loosed. It bent now, and laid something down. It walked away; and at the top of the rim, where another step would have taken it out of his sight, it stopped, and turned itself round, and looked back across five hundred yards of grass at a boy with a light.
101:Six. He counted them off that rim as Seln had counted them onto it. He let the fire go out, and walked down forty paces to the grey stone, and called one word down the old road into the dark.
109:He heard them long before he saw them. There was no need for quiet now. Brom's breathing came first, heavy and careful, and then a shuffle of boots on frozen turf; and at last five shapes grew out of the dusk on the old road, with a small one in front that made no sound at all.
111:Seln stepped over the stone, and stood a breath on the outside of it. Cael saw his shoulders come down by the width of a finger, as a man's do when a Path that he has done without all day is handed back to him.
123:"There. Back," she said. It came out of her on one breath, hoarse, as a person speaks who has been holding it since the morning. She opened her eyes and looked at her own left hand in the lamplight, back and palm. "The clock started again."
135:Brom made no sound that could be heard at ten yards. His boots moved on the turf. Her two hands were round the shoulder before the wood was clear, one above the place and one below; her eyes shut; and Cael, who had sat all day in a stone room and heard her count that man's heart with two fingers, saw her lips reach three and stop counting, since she had what she wanted.
153:"I know that it held. I felt where. I'm not scolding the arm." Oryn drew the wool up and tied it, shorter than before, so that the hand lay high against the collar-bone. "That's two, on a road, after three at a bench yesterday. There isn't a third in me tonight for anybody, and I'd thank you all to stay whole till morning."
167:He went round the three boxes under their blanket with the lamp held low, and stooped over each cord in turn, and touched nothing. When he came back his face was as it had been all day.
173:"A fence that failed at dusk, at its knot, which was set on an iron bar that stands upon the very edge of the place; and directly afterward a boy, who stood up out of the old road beside that bar and declared. Nobody on that rim saw anything between the stair and the bar. I asked Lira what she saw from the thirty-fourth step, and she says that she saw a stair. They will write that he crept up a rut in bad light while they were watching a hole, and that a knot tied a hand's breadth from such an edge is a knot which nobody should have trusted. A man who lays fences for his living would far sooner put that on paper than the other thing, and he has no cause to think of the other thing at all. It holds."
175:He turned, and went up the old road to the brow by himself.
181:"There was a maker's stamp here. It has been taken off with a file, and the place oiled. There is no armoury number. It was bought for one day, by somebody who meant from the first to leave it lying." His voice had not risen. Cael had never heard it so quiet. "I walked past the place where that man lay, twice. I have sat in a hole since sunrise with his bolt on the stair. I have his bow. And I could not tell you tonight whether he was tall."
193:*Hundred and fourteenth day, night. Outside the stone. All six out. Brom: a bolt through the right shoulder, drawn and mended, an hour. Lira: the arm opened on the stair wall and is shut again, a quarter. Thirteen bolts loosed at us in the day, and one hit. Ours: five, and none shot.*
199:*Shadow: once, from the fourth step to the old bar. Lira saw it go, from below. Seln turned his back. Three were round the door. The six were two hundred and sixty yards off, with a lip between; not one had a line to me. Let go in the rut. It holds. Dead to the elbows; I had to watch my own hand.*
217:*Nine. With the reading on the road, ten since Thornwater. Deployment since the Line: twenty-three.*
225:The light came down the old road when it was full dark.
247:Teague's eyes did not leave him, and Cael waited for the rest of it: how, and with what, and what a boy was who had a Wind's crack in him and a fire as well. He watched the man weigh each of those and set it down again, as he had set down a question at an inn's door two nights before.
251:It was the second time in three days that Cael had been told so. A one-armed man had said it of a line in a book. This man said it of him, to his face, across a lantern, with no heat in it and no fear; he might have been reading out the weight of a load.
255:He took up his lantern, and went back up the old road to where his three were standing.
259:It had been the hundred and thirteenth morning, a day and a half before, when Teague took a slip that he did not want.
261:He had been at the plank before the mist was off the river. The five had gone by it in the dark, two hours earlier, and he had heard the mule. Pike was setting out his book; and there was a shingle on the oak, six along from the left-hand post, which had hung there for nine days because no crew of any standing would trouble with it.
271:"It's on my road."
273:"You've no road south. You've forty marks of fence east, with my capitals on it, and you've a day's posts still to set."
CHAPTER 52
