59:They left the stone at first light on the hundred and sixteenth day, and for three days the country was kinder to them than it had any need to be.
61:The frost had gone off in the night. Cael woke to a low grey sky and a smell of wet earth, which he had not smelled for three weeks, and by the middle of the forenoon the ruts were dark and soft where they had rung the day before. The carters' road came back to its line beyond the low ground, as a one-armed man had said that it would, and went on south and east under the foot of the ridge. A salt cart had been along it a week ago, and nothing since.
63:Seln went a quarter of a mile in front, where he could be seen against the sky. Karis walked behind him with the hide under her arm, and at every halt she unrolled it on her knee, and laid the brass along it, and looked from the skin to the country and back again. Lira came next, on the whole of her foot, and a road gone soft was no better for her than a road gone hard.
71:"You're carrying on the one side and walking as if you had two. Let that strap out a hand, and take it across you." She waited while he did it. "That's a week I've given that shoulder. I'd as soon you didn't spend it for me in a morning."
79:They slept that night in a halting-ring, with a fire of Seln's flint, and Cael sat his watch with Hesk's book shut on his knee. He owed a healer two readings of himself yet, on two different days, and he had not laid a finger on his own wrist since she told him *not tonight* between two bars of iron. He left it so. She had asked for a night that was only a night; every one since had been the eve of a floor or the morrow of one, and he thought it likely that this was the eve of another. The debt weighed on him a good deal less than it had on a halting-ground above the wash, and he took that for a sign that he had begun to believe her.
81:On the second morning the road swung away south toward a hold whose smoke they could see, and the brass did not go with it.
