219:They walked. Though not one of them could have said so yet, that was the rule of the place, and it kept them company the whole of the way in: a hundred and eighty-five yards, which Karis counted under her breath, with something in the reeds holding its station on them for exactly as long as they did not stop.
223:It was two strides broad, of the same grey, and it went away to the right and the left in a great curve; Cael put the whole round of it at seventy yards across. Within it there was water, a foot or two deep and as clear as a well's, moving slowly outward and spilling over the kerb's low places into the marsh. Under the water lay the floor. It had been one floor once. It was broken now into great plates, like the ice on a pond that has gone to pieces and frozen again, and every broken edge was round. Thirty yards off, in the middle of it, the walls stood.
243:The plate tipped. Cael saw only two things: a darkness as broad as a door rising at the plate's edge, and the two halves of it parting like the shells of a mussel. Then the stone was not there. The plate fell back with a slap that wetted the six of them to the knee, and the water rocked, and was clear.
275:She gave it to him before she had thought, and then she stared, and so did the rest of them. He had read out a great many things to that notebook in a hundred days: the width of a road, and the age of a bar, and what a paper meant. He had asked each time that it should be entered as said. He had never once put a mark in it.
285:She wrote the day beneath it, and her initial, and did not alter a word.
329:The thing did not go away. All round the held plate the others were lifting now, in a ring, and staying lifted; it had brought the whole of its length to that one place, for the first time in an afternoon, and laid itself about the only weight on its floor that would not come up.
