65:Seln had not raised his voice. He had pitched it, as he had once pitched a single word down two hundred yards of old road, and it carried to every corner of that room.
67:Cael ran. He went along the ledge under the northern wall, away from the place, since a second weight there was the last thing that Brom wanted; and at the next break he got his hands on the stone and hauled. His right forearm had stopped a crossbow's stock five days before, and it told him so. He came over the top on his belly, and turned.
107:It was cover, and it was a trap, and the same yard of stone was both. Cael understood it: nothing could come at them where they sat, and they could not leave it. He watched the plates lift below him, in a line that went round the hole and came back; and when it passed beneath his boots he could see a length of back under the clear water, as broad as the ledge itself, going by for a great deal longer than he liked.
145:It shot from under him on the wet, and Brom's body did what any body does. His right arm came out of its binding. It caught the wall's end above his head and took the whole of him, on a shoulder that a healer had shut five days ago and given a week.
163:But she had been on that kerb the afternoon before, when a girl threw a flat stone.
165:She did not walk and she did not run. She skipped. She went from plate to plate with her arms out and her coat flapping, a short quick hop to each, and never the same plate twice, and never two counts in one place; a square woman in a dead man's coat, coming across thirty yards of broken floor like a child on stepping-stones. It would have been comical on any other morning of his life.
179:He had ten fragments. He went through nine of them in the time of one breath, as he had on a frozen road, and it was different, because here there was nobody to see, and he need hold none of them back. Wind was in his feet already. It would put him over the wall in a count. Then it would lock him where he landed, dumb, for a count more, the stillest thing on that floor, at a time and a place that he had not chosen. Storm would lay the water flat, and what was that to a thing with no eyes? Ember's fire was for things that could see it.
181:There was Anchor, which he had set for the first time yesterday, on his knees, on a kerb.
185:It had left its round for a stone on a plate. It had left everything, yesterday, for a point that would not come away. What would it leave a fallen man for?
199:It came back along under the ledge faster than he had thought anything of that size could come. He felt the mass of it, and which way it bore, and nothing else; it was as broad as the ledge, and it went on arriving. It had turned in its own length, out in the dish, and left the man who lay still there for the man who had just come down upon its roof.
209:There was one road for it, and it went down that road: through the bones of his legs, and his hips, and up his back and down again, and out through both heels into the ledge. He did not give. The plate, which had been tipping, was driven flat under him with a crack like a mallet on a post; and something that had taken stones off that floor for longer than there had been carters, and had never once met one that did not come away, stopped against the soles of a boy's boots.
215:The head lay at the ledge's lip beside his knee, half out of the water, quite still. He could have put his hand on it. It was the brown of the marsh, and smooth, and seamed down the middle where the two halves met; and it had no more face than the toe of a boot.
255:The pale side was six yards below her, and as broad as a barn door.
309:"You spent it in a morning. I said that you would." She had her shoulder under his left arm, and she was wet to the waist. "Walk. You can be sorry on the grass."
