9:It was Karis who lit it. She had come over the stone with a morning's pencil in her arms and her cheek bleeding, and she put the sheets into Lira's lap and held out her right hand; and a point stood on the kindling that Seln had laid ready before first light. It was the first thing that any of them had declared in a day, and Cael saw her look at her own palm afterward.
27:She sat down where she had knelt, on the frozen turf, with her knees drawn up and her forehead on them, and it was as long as it takes to count a hundred before she lifted her head. Nobody went to her. Cael had heard her say on a mountain that her reserve was a purse; he had watched her empty it at a mill, and at two stones, and he understood by now that what she spent on such a morning was the very thing that a child at some northern gate was waiting for, and that she knew it to the grain.
75:It was only three words. But it had not come out as her other sentences came, each one finished and set down like a bowl on a board; the middle of it had given a little under her, as a plate gives underfoot. He made no answer, since he did not think that she could have borne to be answered. She had told a mill that she would be nobody's sixth, and a board at Thornwater that she was a stop; she had eaten out of their bowl for a week, standing; and this was the only way that she had of saying what a person became to her when she had gone into a marsh after his friend and watched him go into worse.
85:"I know what I'll get. I've had it six times." She was counting already, under the words. "I've had my hands on every one of them this morning, to see what that place had done. I'll not sit down to my bread with one left out, only because he can't be read."
95:"They're not gone again; you'd not have walked that path. They've been leaned on, by a thing that I'd not like to put a weight to." She sat back on her heels. "I can't shut what I can't read. You've heard me say it till you're sick of it. That's ten days of knowing that you've ribs, and they'll talk when it turns cold, and they'll not do more."
97:"Ten days."
99:"From this morning. Count it yourself; you will anyway."
103:"I told you a night that's only a night, and I meant one with nothing in front of it. You've not had one since, and you'll not get one on this road. But I've been looking at you for a quarter of an hour, and there's another kind." She nodded at his chest. "You read it frightened, in a loft. Tonight there's nothing left in you to be frightened with; I doubt that you could be afraid of a wolf. So you'll read it tonight, when the rest are down. Then you'll sleep, and you'll read it once more when you wake, before you've had time to think. That's three, on three days, with a night between each."
113:The first was the one that she had lit, at the track's end, with wet people round it and a line of breeches steaming on a rope between two bundles of reeds. The second she built for herself, twenty yards off and upwind, no bigger than a hat, since she would not have sparks near paper. She had the lid of the middle box for a table there, and her morning's work laid out on the turf under pebbles, one sheet beside another, to lose the damp that they had taken from a wall. She had gone into that place for the inner faces of four walls, and had come out with as much of one of them as a person can rub upside down in an hour with a girl holding her by the belt; and she set about it now as if the hour had been a week.
125:"Seln says that a night won't hurt it." She did not turn round. "There's one. If I let it down, I've a stick with a string on, and nobody here to bend it who isn't asleep or mending. It stays in the bow."
129:"I know what he read. I watched it come up out of a hole this morning, the whole of it, when a carter had said that nobody ever saw so much. I'm not saying that it's coming. I'm saying that I've one, and it's in."
131:He left her to it, since she had sat on a stair for a day once with that same bow, for the same reason, and had been right.
133:It was the middle of the afternoon when he saw the other thing, and he saw it only because Brom was making a face at him. Brom lay on his back by the near fire, under two coats, with his right arm strapped to him in every strip of linen that the pannier held. He had his left forefinger laid across his lips, and with his eyes he was pointing at what sat beside him.
139:Cael had been on roads with that woman for nineteen days in all, on a mountain and a moor and a ridge, and he had never once seen it. She lay down last at every camp, a stride and a half beyond the farthest of them, with a mule between, and at Thornwater she had shut a door. She had told him on a rock that there was nobody there who had not seen her asleep, and he had taken it for a manner of speaking; she slept as a person pays a debt, privately, to have it over.
151:Not one of the four of them got up to put the blanket back, since she would have woken. They let her be, in the middle of them, with a big man's wrist in her hand, while the short day went over toward the rise.
153:He wrote the day's log then, with his eyes still swimming a little, and he kept it to what the day had cost.
155:*Hundred and nineteenth day. The track's end, outside the stone. All six out. Brom's shoulder opened on a wall and is mended a second time, an hour; it will not be mended a third. Four bolts spent; one in the bow, with Lira.*
159:*Compression: the strike, standing. The old ribs. Ten days, she says.*
169:He read himself that night when the others were down, by her order, and he was an hour about it.
175:At ten the short reading gave him his own gait, quick and slow, and tonight a good deal more slow than quick. He went on beyond ten, into the deep reading, and it was easier than it had been in a loft, since he had nothing in him tonight that could hurry it; he went along the dry hiss of the Wind at a walk, looking for a side.
177:He knew now what he was looking for. He had been in a river with banks, on a ridge, for a minute, and had felt them stand close and high on either hand like the walls of a mill-race. He went the whole length of the Wind and found none. The millstone was under it, and ran into it, and he could not have said where; he tried the wick, and the bellows, and the held note, which was thinner tonight than he had ever heard it, and they were ten, and not one of them had an edge.
183:He had been afraid of it for seventeen days. He looked for the fear as he went, and it was simply not there, having been spent that morning with a good deal else on a ledge; and he came to the crowded place under his breastbone as a tired man comes to his own gate.
187:There was no sound in it, and nothing in it moved. He brought the ten to it one after another, as he had in a loft, and every one of them leaned aside and went by and closed again beyond. Those were the three things that he had counted on the night of his birthday; he counted them again, and they had not changed by a hair.
191:He had stood on a wall that morning and looked down at a spring. The water there had come up against a fallen slab and piled, and curled back behind it in a slow eddy, as water does when it meets a thing lying in its road. He looked for the piling now, and there was none. Not one of the ten came up against that place. Each of them began to lean a long way short of it, and came back to its line a long way past; they went by it as a river goes round a stone that has lain in its bed for as long as there has been a river, without a ripple, along a way that was worn smooth.
209:He woke while the stars were still out, with frost on his blanket, and read it a third time before he had fairly remembered where he was. He was rested, and cold, and clear in the head, which was a third temper and as unlike the loft as it was unlike the night before; and the place lay where it had lain, with the ten going round it by the same worn way. It took him less than a quarter of an hour, and he wrote one line afterward by feel.
211:*Hundred and twentieth day, before light. The third: where it was, as it was, the same.*
217:"Three. The loft, and last night, and now."
219:"And a night between each." She rubbed her palms together. "Then I'll hear it. Come round here."
239:He put his right hand on her wrist, which was broad, and cold, with the white scar across the heel of the hand; and the tenth turned toward it, and went. At three there were the banks, close and high, and at ten he knew her, as a person knows a step on a stair. He laid his left palm on the back of her neck, under the cropped hair, and she let out one long breath.
245:"There are ten in me. You found more than one and couldn't count them. It's ten, and I've counted them three times. Not one has a side. I looked for an hour last night for a bank like these, and there isn't any."
253:"They don't meet it. That's what I had last night that I hadn't before. Water piles against a stone that's fallen in its way. These don't pile. They lean off a long way short of it, and come back a long way past. They go round it as yours goes down its bed."
263:"I gave it one a long while back, on an old leaf, when it was only something that another person's hands had caught the edge of. The still place. I never had anything to lay the words on till last night."
287:He did, and it surprised him how little he wished to move it. Nine days ago he would have given a good deal to hear that one word; he had tried to buy it at a well with a thing half looked at, and had been told what a single morning was worth. He had it now for the full price, three readings and a night between, and it was only a word about a debt, and it left the rest exactly as heavy as she had made it.
293:"I don't know what it is, and nor do you. I know what a person does with a place in himself that he's found: he goes back to it every night with his fingers." She was already turning toward Brom's blankets. "You've looked three times. You know where it is, and that it keeps. Let it keep."
CHAPTER 56
7:It was done as at the Stair, with one difference: nobody needed a sigil to find the way. Lira went along the reeds with the cord's end shut in her right fist and her boot half over the line, saying *gone* and *back* under her breath, and at every station her toe was within a hand of the first stem. Seln cut the turfs, which came up black and dripping. Cael wrote. His eyes had come back to him in the night, and he set down sixty-two stations in a better hand than the last.
9:They were the whole of the forenoon about that perimeter, with the marsh on their right and the frost going grey on the turf. Not once in that round did the water stand a yard inside the line, or a reed a yard beyond it. A spring had filled that basin for longer than anybody could say, and it had filled it to a line that five people could feel in their heads and no farther. Cael could not have said what water knew about such an edge, and not one of them tried.
11:Karis went down her page with the pencil when Lira's fist came back to the grey stone. She turned to the leaf of the Stair and to the leaf of the Fallow, and wrote one line under the two that were there.
19:It was a little after noon when Seln came down off the rise, where he had been since the cord was coiled.
27:"You're a long way off any road," said Teague.
41:"A bolt, six days gone. I drew it and shut it on a road. Then he came off a wall yesterday and hung by it, and I shut it again, in that ditch."
53:"It's what I've been telling it since yesterday," said Oryn. "I've no linen left that isn't on him already. Have you?"
57:The two of them knelt, one on either side. He could not follow the whole of what they did. The shirt-tail came off, and a pad of raw wool went up under the arm. Then a strip of stout webbing as broad as a hand was carried round Brom's chest, and over the point of the shoulder, and back beneath the elbow, three times; and the lean woman laid that figure without once looking at her fingers. Oryn held where she was told to hold. Half way through she stopped the other's wrist.
71:It had been the hundred and seventeenth morning, three days before, when Teague took his crew up the notch from the mill with one more in it than had come down.
73:He had not sent for her. She had come of her own motion, as she did every year. She went up the north water when the roads dried, to her daughter's hold, and set whatever that valley broke until the first hard frost. Then she walked down to Lowmarch, and asked a one-armed man where the crew was. This year he had told her Millrace. She had ridden down on the mill's cart, on the sacks, and had been sitting on the flat stone by the ford when Teague came off the ridge with a stake still to set.
75:He had not set it. The woman with the net had not put her mark on the shingle, and the four marks were lost, as he had said they would be. He had sent his word north by the mill's carter, in so many words, for the man with the book: five strokes to look at, and all of them walking; and the three hundred was to stay where it lay.
77:That was his business with that crew done, by any reckoning that he had ever used, and he sat on the bank at dusk with his boots off, and it was not done.
79:He went over it as he went over a load. Six people had lain round that low ground for a day with their faces covered, and had gone off it north and south and west when a boy stood up with a light. They had gone as people go who have practised leaving. He had read where they lay, by lantern, and had found nothing above the height of a boot. People who take such pains over what they leave behind are not people who go home.
81:And the five had gone off with their healer and two mules and three boxes, across open country, in a line. He had seen the start of it from the brow. A crew of six with two beasts leaves a road on wet ground that a boy of ten could follow at a trot.
99:"There's a man with them who had a bolt through his shoulder," Teague told her. "Their healer shut it that night, with her hands, on the grass. She's a good one; I've seen her work. He walked off next day with a case on his other side."
103:"Three days, by now."
113:They were on the brow of the low ground before noon. The four crossbows had lain where he left them, by a grey stone, and the frost had kept every print about it; and from between two bars of iron the trail went off south and east, as plain as a ditch.
115:He walked behind it for three days.
117:It kept a road for the first of them, and a grown-over track for the second, and he read the six as he went. The big one was carrying on his left side. The girl was setting her right foot down whole, at every stride, and had been told to. The small one stopped at each halting-place and knelt, and laid something on her knee; and the quiet one, whom he had needed two looks to find in an inn, had been out in front of the rest the whole way, alone. On the third morning the track gave out, and the trail did not so much as waver. That was what he could not get past. People go to water, or to a roof, or along whatever road there is. These had gone over wet grass with no mark to steer by, round nothing, toward nothing that he could see, as a plumb line hangs.
121:There was no print over theirs but his own crew's. He had gone back along it himself at each dusk, a mile, to be sure. He had lain on his face on a knoll for an hour on the second night, with the cold coming up through his coat, and watched the way that they had come. Nothing moved on it. Nobody was behind them, and nobody had been.
127:"Then we've walked three days after a crew that's in no trouble."
129:"We've walked three days and found that out. I'd call it cheap."
131:It was the middle of the hundred and twentieth day when the ground fell away in front of him, and he saw reeds, and grey walls standing in water.
133:He had never been within two days of that place. He knew what it was. Every salt man on the cold road had a tale of it, and not one of the tales had a man in it who went down to the reeds.
135:There was a camp at the track's end with two fires. He counted his own first: a spear, and a bow, and a boy, and an old woman with a board. Then he counted the camp, and there were six in it.
139:Teague stood on that rise with his thumbs in his belt, and added it up. They had gone into the Fallow, six of them, and come out; a one-armed man had said so twice under his eave. They had gone into the Stair and one had walked out of it declaring. And here was the drowned hall, which nobody went near, and a rope of wet breeches, and six at a fire.
147:It was dusk before he went himself. The boy was sitting alone at the near fire, against a scaled stake, with a book shut on his knee. Teague had stood over a great many men in his time, and it had got him the answers that men give to somebody who is standing over them.
199:He said it to the dusk, as he had once told it to a lantern, and went up the rise to his three.
203:*Teague sat down next to me. Asked what I am. I could have read him. I asked instead. The day I stop asking is the day I'm the registry. Write it down. Again. Every time.*
CHAPTER 57
3:It had been the afternoon of the hundred and nineteenth day when Karis sat down at the far fire with three clean leaves before her, and by the dusk of the hundred and twentieth she had risen from them only to sleep and to pace a marsh.
7:She had never done such a thing in her life. A leaf was written once, and dated, and laid in its place, and the place was entered; that was what a box was for. But there were three boxes, and she had seen what a mountain could do to a cart. On the seventy-eighth day everything that the five of them owned had gone into a gorge except those three, and they had come off the hill only because a very large person went back for two of them, and a boy broke three ribs over the third. She had counted the loss that night, when she could count again. It had been blankets, and a kettle, and a rope.
9:It would not be blankets now. Until the Fallow there had been nothing in those boxes that did not also stand on a shelf somewhere to the west, where she was no longer let in. Since the Fallow there had been a good deal, and since this morning there was a thing that existed nowhere in the world but on the paper in front of her.
13:She wrote small. At the head she set the three places by the names that carters gave them, since she had no better: the Fallow Ring, and the Long Stair, and the hall. Under each went its perimeter, which was sixty-two cords and twenty strides, three times over; and its radius, ten cords and a piece; and the number on its stake. Thirty-one. Thirty-two, on two bars. Thirty-three.
15:Then the walls, and here she was careful, since she had been greedy that morning and knew it. She had eleven sheets from the Fallow, where the bands ran round the feet of eleven stones, and twenty-nine from a room under the Stair. She had twelve from the hall. That made fifty-two, and the last twelve were all from one wall, rubbed upside down in an hour, by a person who was being held by the belt.
21:She had seen it. It had been a hand below her chin for a quarter of an hour, on a wall above a spring: the line that stood by itself, with its small divisions, as it stood between the courses under the Stair. She had counted those divisions there against her short cord, twice, and they had given her the radius of the circle. Here she had had a pencil in one hand and her own weight on the other, and no cord at all.
27:A blanket had come onto her shoulders at some hour of the first afternoon. She had not seen it arrive. She had noticed, some time later, that she was no longer shaking, and had put up a hand, and found wool; and under her fingers at the corner there was a darn that she knew, since she had watched it made by lamplight in a loft, in very small stitches, by a person who mended his own things and nobody else's.
35:She had her own reach for each of the three, in cords. What she wanted beside it was the other man's: the figure that a surveyor had gone over twice with his pencil, in a room under a floor, until it cut the paper. She had heard it read out once. She had written it on her cuff, in the dark of that room, and the cuff had since been in a marsh.
51:"Then he found what I found, at the Stair. I should like it entered beside mine that he did, and by what."
53:"A cord knotted at the fives. It is in Brom's case." Seln shut the book. "I can tell you no more of him. He writes a small upright hand, and presses. I have been through a page and a half of it every night since that room, and I could not tell you whether he was tall."
59:She unrolled it on the lid of the box and set a pebble on each corner. There were three rings on it still, and one of them was a lie: she had drawn it on the ninety-second evening round a place where a carter lifted his hand, and since noon she had known to a stride where it ought to have been. She took the crumb of bread that she kept for such work and rubbed the old ring out. She drew the new one a finger nearer to the corner, where a cord and a girl's left foot had put it, and wrote the day beside it, and *moved; paced.*
67:She did not trust that, and she set about breaking it. A straight thing will lie through any two marks on a skin, and so two of those dots proved nothing whatever; the whole question was the third. She moved the brass. She laid it through the Fallow's dot and the hall's, and looked at the Stair's: it sat under the edge. She laid it through the Stair's and the hall's, and the Fallow's sat under the edge. She took her pencil and made each dot again a pencil's breadth to one side, which was as far out as she believed that she could be on a map of days, and laid the brass a fourth time, and a fifth.
69:It would not miss. She could make it lie a hair to this side of a dot, or a hair to that; she could not make daylight show between the brass and any of the three.
71:And she had not chosen the third. She had laid that edge on two rings at a grey stone, in daylight, with five people looking, before any one of them had seen a reed. It had run off into a corner of the skin where a man once lifted his hand. They had walked along it for three days, in a strip a mile wide, and had come over a rise onto a marsh.
81:She had laid leaves side by side on a trestle once, before it was light, and had known what they said when they lay so. There had been three of them. It had been a smaller thing that morning, and she had been able to put a clean leaf back into a box.
91:She laid it out for them on the hundred and twenty-first morning, in daylight, on the lid of the middle box, with all of them looking.
119:He went over it as he went over anything. There was meal for six days in the left-hand box, and a little bacon. There was a shoulder that was bound and would not be mended a third time, and an arm in a sling that was to hold nothing till the snow. The frost had come back in the night, harder than they had yet had it, and a healer had said at a stone that the snow would not be long. And there was a line. He had been walking toward something for a hundred and twenty days without knowing that it had a direction, and it had one now, and it went off the edge of a calfskin.
121:"Three days out along it," he said. "No farther. If there's nothing under the brass by the third dusk, we turn for Lowmarch, and I'll say so then without anybody having to ask me."
129:"I've a fence at forty with a day's posts to set," he said, "and a one-armed man to tell what I saw. I'll tell him what I saw."
135:Cael went and stood a stride and a half from her. He had come to her at a mill, eight days before, with this same errand, and he remembered how he had put it.
145:"See that you do." She looked past him, at the hide. "There's a road a mile east of this water; I saw it from the rise. That quiet one says that it lies under your brass for a day, and that it forks. The left hand goes up through a gap, north and west, to Thornwater, in four of that carter's ticks." She took up the rope. "So I walk your road for a day, since it's mine as well. Where it forks, I'm the route."
149:Cael was the last to leave the grey stone. He looked down the ruts to the reeds, and along the pale straight path, to four walls standing in water with the morning in them. A thing lay under those walls with four bolts in it. It would go its round again, he supposed, once in a quarter of an hour, when it was able; and no carter would ever know that anybody had been in.
155:They struck the road an hour from the marsh, and it rang under them. Every rut had a spine of ice in it. The pools on either hand had shut in the night, and the pale grass stood stiff and white to the knee, and their breath went up from eight mouths, counting the mules', and hung behind them along the road.
159:He did it on purpose. He had counted that crew on a hundred roads without thinking; today he looked at each of them as he would have looked at a room that he was to leave in the morning. Seln went far out in front, small against the sky, with a case on his chest and a book in his coat. Karis walked with the hide rolled under her arm, and her lips moving. Lira had the healer's bow slung across her back on its strap, and she set the great boot down whole at every stride; she had been given no order to hand that bow back, and she had not offered. Brom led the brown mule on her low side, with the kit on his left shoulder and four yards of webbing holding the rest of him together.
163:It was what six people sounded like on a hard road. He knew the sound as he knew his own breathing, and he knew that he would learn it better tomorrow, when it was shorter by a pair of boots.
165:They met one cart all that day.
167:It came toward them a little after noon, behind a single mule, with a load of raw hides roped under a cloth and a smell going before it. The carter walked at the near wheel with a scarf over his nose. He looked at six people, and two beasts, and a sling, and an arm bound to a chest. He lifted one hand to them, as carters did on that side of the Line, and they lifted theirs, and nobody broke step.
169:There were two holds on that road, and both were shut. Each had its fence of split posts and its barred gate, and smoke going straight up from the long house; and at neither did anybody come to the gate-walk to see who went by. The cold term had come down on that country as a lid comes down on a box.
177:They came at dusk to a third hold, and it had been dead for years.
191:Cael waited until the bowls were empty. He had made up his mind to it on the road. A thing had been the healer's to hear before anybody, and she had heard it; and there were four people at that fire who had been told at a loft's ladder that it existed, and had asked him nothing since.
201:"It's been entered since the day you said it. There's a leaf with a session's number on."
215:The healer ate out of the sixth, standing, with her back to the gable and her eyes on the road north.
CHAPTER 58
3:They came to the fork two hours after sunrise on the hundred and twenty-second day, and the healer stopped the grey mule in the middle of it.
5:It was a plain enough place. The road divided at a heap of chock-stones that carters had left, and the right hand went on south and east under the foot of a long bare ridge. The left climbed away toward a notch between two hills, with the frost lying blue in it, and beyond the notch there was nothing to be seen but sky.
27:"It rode on my mule for thirty rounds in a cover, and I looked at it at noon one day, at a stone, and left it where it was. It's done more across your knee than it ever did for me." Oryn did not put out a hand. "There's one in it. See that it stays in till it's wanted."
29:She looked along the five of them then, once. Her sum ran to the oxen ford on the hundred and twenty-fifth, three weeks after it had looked for her; then she said the thing that she had stopped the mule to say.
33:"I've been a stop on my own route for nine days. I'm going back to being the route."
37:She clicked her tongue at the jenny, and took the left-hand road.
41:He said nothing through the whole of it. There were a good many things that he might have asked on that piece of road: whether a child's chest had changed, and how a round was walked in snow, and when he would see her. There was the other, which was whether she would ever tell him what she had refused to tell him on her table. He asked none of them. Each was hers to give, and she had a road to walk.
45:"That's far enough. You've four people standing in a road."
49:She shifted the rope on her shoulder. She was looking up at the notch, and her face was as tired and as level as he had ever seen it; and whatever had given under her at a marsh's edge, three days before, had been put back where she kept it.
55:"Bring the map," said Oryn, to the road in front of her.
59:There were four of them behind him, and one mule. They stood at the heap of stones as she had left them: a small man with a case on his chest, and a small woman with a hide under her arm, and a very big one bound up like a parcel, and a girl with a bow on her back and one sleeve empty. It was the crew that had come down off a shelf above a valley with no name, on the second day past the Line, and it looked a good deal the worse for wear.
63:*Hundred and twenty-second day, the fork. Oryn north. On her terms. I didn't ask. Five on the bearing.*
67:The road bent away south before noon, and the brass did not.
73:They went up, with Brom on the low side of the mule and three boxes swaying. It was a great whale's back of pale grass, a mile broad on its crown, with the country falling off on either hand. In every fold of its flanks a line of white lay, where the frost had not lifted for a week and would not now lift before the thaw. From above they looked like chalk strokes ruled on a slate.
81:It was the word that he gave at the end of every watch, and Cael had heard it some hundreds of times. He heard it seven times more before dark. Each time Karis nodded, and wrote the hour, and went on.
85:Lira walked beside him for a mile in the afternoon, on the frozen turf, with the bow knocking against her back.
91:"Good." She put the great boot down whole, and the next. "She'd have answered you, and then she'd have had to carry having answered for four days through a gap. I thought that you'd know that. I wasn't sure of it."
93:They slept on the ridge in a hollow, without wood, in every blanket that they had. Brom laid six bowls on the turf. He looked at the row, and took one back; and it passed without remark, since he had done exactly that on a hundred nights.
95:It was that night that the ribs began to talk.
97:She had told him that they would. The cold got into the three old breaks some time after the stars came out, and stayed there, like a thumb pressed on a bruise. He could breathe, and he could walk, and he could not find a way to lie. He counted it: this was the fourth night of her ten.
99:The second day on the ridge was the clearest that he had seen past the Line.
101:By noon he could see for thirty miles. There was no road in any of it, and no smoke, and not a fence or a roof; there were hills going away blue behind hills, with the white lying in their folds, and a sky over them so large that he could not look at the whole of it at once. Four people were strung out along the turf ahead of him, small and dark. A man who knew every road out of a town was walking toward a place where there was none. A girl who had been ranked every week of her life was a mile from the nearest thing that could have measured her, with a bow on her back that she could shoot and could not bend.
111:It was true, and he let it stand; and the other thing was in the same coat with it, and had been all morning. They were walking along a ruler. Three floors lay in a row, as stones lie along a path; and when he looked ahead down the ridge, to where the strip went on into the blue, he was more afraid of what was under the brass than he had been of a thing in a marsh.
117:Seln was standing on the crest when they came up to him at dusk, and he did not say the word that he had said seven times the day before and nine times since morning.
127:Cael could not have said what. It was grey, and upright, and it was taller than a man; how much taller he had no means of telling, with nothing beside it to measure by and the better part of half a mile of dusk between. It was not a ring of stones. It was not a floor with a hole in it, or four walls. It had a top that was not level. That was all that the light would give him, and when he tried to hold more of it, the dusk kept the rest.
139:Below the ruts, a furlong down the slope, there was something else going round. It was a line of small dark uprights, set close, running away on both hands with the curve of the ground until the dusk had it. From the crest it looked like a row of stitches.
149:She went down from the ruts by herself, on the whole of her foot, counting under her breath, and he counted with her. The line had lain forty paces inside the ruts at the Stair, and the ruts at the Fallow had kept forty yards. She passed forty with the iron still a long way below her. She passed a hundred.
151:At a hundred and eighty-nine, or within a pace or two of it, she stopped one pace above the dark uprights.
155:"A hundred and ninety," said Karis, at the ruts. "Not forty."
167:He had seen four such stakes. There had been one at a grey stone on the Fallow, and two at the Stair with a road between, and one at the track's end beside a marsh. Each had stood alone at an edge where a cord had been laid.
169:These did not stand alone. Anchor had known it before his eyes did; it had asked at two bars with an old road between them, ten days ago, and here it asked at every pace, in its thin held note, as far as he could hear. There was a bar at his right knee, a hand's length clear of the turf, and another a pace beyond it, and another a pace beyond that. They went on so in both directions, one to a pace, round the head of that valley, for as far as he could see iron against the grass.
193:"Half the bend is twice the round. Four hundred. Near enough. Twice the radius. The same shape, at twice the size, round a thing I can't see."
195:Her voice was quite steady. She opened her notebook against the lamp, and did the sum in it, with her lips moving. She had said two hundred of the Stair, by her eye, and had been twenty short and a stride; and he saw the figure that she wrote now, which was four hundred and forty yards, and two strides, from a line that she had not crossed to a middle that she had not seen.
203:"There was a sheet with two," he said, after a breath. "I told you that I could not place it. A marsh three days behind us, and this: I can place it now."
209:It was the question, and all five of them knew it. A line half this size had put out five sigils at a stride and given them back at a stride, the same, with nothing lost. Would one of twice the size give them back? There was no cord that could tell her that. Somebody would have to walk over that iron with a Path in her and come out again; and the one person who could have stood at the fence with her sleeves up was two days gone through a gap.

