29:It took him the better part of the forenoon. He did not hurry. A tick came, and a skittering, and then nothing for as long as it takes to walk a quarter of a mile; and then another. Some fell short and rang on the far side of the roof, and one struck so near the hole that they heard it skate past the lip.
33:The square had gone from grey to white. It was near noon, and bright up there, and a little of the brightness came down as far as the tenth step and lay on it like spilt milk.
67:"Two swallows apiece at noon," he said. "And I'm keeping two full, shut, for the top. I'll not have us get out of this hole and find that we've nothing to walk on."
