243:The plate tipped. Cael saw only two things: a darkness as broad as a door rising at the plate's edge, and the two halves of it parting like the shells of a mussel. Then the stone was not there. The plate fell back with a slap that wetted the six of them to the knee, and the water rocked, and was clear.
255:The pebble struck the water over a plate and leapt, and struck again, and again, five times, each a little shorter than the last, going away toward the hall in a line of bright rings. The swell had been a quarter of the round off. He saw it check, and turn, and come; and it did not strike. It ran along beneath the line of the rings, a little behind the last of them, like a dog that has lost a ball.
275:She gave it to him before she had thought, and then she stared, and so did the rest of them. He had read out a great many things to that notebook in a hundred days: the width of a road, and the age of a bar, and what a paper meant. He had asked each time that it should be entered as said. He had never once put a mark in it.
329:The thing did not go away. All round the held plate the others were lifting now, in a ring, and staying lifted; it had brought the whole of its length to that one place, for the first time in an afternoon, and laid itself about the only weight on its floor that would not come up.
349:They made their camp at the track's end, a few yards outside the grey stone, with the two mules hobbled on the rise. It had come on cold again with the dusk. Seln lit the fire, and the six of them sat close about it while Karis said what she wanted.
353:"There's a way to do it," said Seln. "It has given us its rule, and a rule is a plan. On the floor, we move. We rest only on what comes up out of the old work: the path, and the kerb, and the tops of those walls, which are a yard through. A wall-top is a kerb set on end." He counted it off. "We go in at first light. Lira carries the bow, spanned, since she can loose it and cannot bend it. I carry the hook and the sleeve. Karis has her paper and nothing else. Thirty yards of plates, at a walk, with nobody standing; in at the gap; and up."
383:*Hundred and eighteenth day. The edge of the third one, at the track's end. A marsh, and the reeds stop where the sigils stop. One bar, thirty-three, old, and no second. A hall in the middle of it, and a thing under the hall's floor that a carter told the healer nobody has seen whole.*
391:*In at first light. Karis hasn't said if since the Stair.*
