   161	It was a reduction, and there was nothing in it that asked her to be clever. She had two letters, a hooked upright and a thing like a gate. She had the old town's way of counting, which she had taken from the spines of its tally-books by pattern alone before she would let herself read a word of them. She wrote out every year of that count whose first word began with the one sign and whose second began with the other. Then she struck out those which no clerk could have meant.
   162	
