   280	
   281	She was sitting on a stone with the box before her. The hide lay across its lid, under the lamp, with the clean sheet pinned to its eastern edge; and she had turned the whole so that the pencil line ran away from her toward the dark.
   282	
   283	"I have it," she said. "As near as it can be had. Come round to this side."
   284	
   285	He came round, and stood at her shoulder, and looked down at a calfskin with four rings on it. He had first seen that skin on a board in a meadow, with one ring. He had walked to every one of the four.
   286	
   287	Beyond the last of them, on the bare paper, there was a mark that he could see only because the lamp stood low. It was a ring pressed with a thumbnail, with a grain of sand at its heart.
