6:## Per-chapter table
50:## Markers left
78:- **Placed on a table's pin, not a quotation's:** 50:203 the young one, "Four" (BOOK_MAP §3 D: "Steps over the iron when Teague looks at him ('Four')").
81:## New canon minted
85:- **The calendar of the leg, laid against Book 7 (47–49).** The 299th: the Stair's brow a little after noon; camp at a beck a mile short of the halting-ring. The 300th: the road's swing at noon; the track's end above the hall's marsh at dusk. The 301st: the east road; two living holds; the dead hold at dusk. The 302nd: the heap of chock-stones two hours after sunrise; the ridge by noon; the seep in the afternoon; a hollow on the ridge's back. The 303rd: the ridge; the brow at dusk. Stair to hall in a day and a half of hard walking against Book 7's two days and a morning at a survey's pace (said on the page at 47:253).
86:- **The cairn (47:197–201).** At the bend where the carters' road swings south to the hold, a built cairn as high as a man's hip marks a mule-path that goes north over a saddle to "three gates"; it is not Book 7's heap of chock-stones, which is passed on the 302nd (48:109–117).
87:- **Oryn's reason and her word north (47:237–249).** Brom's shoulder, shut on the 298th, wants a week of her eye (the 305th); the knee on her linen; an edge twice the size with ten going at it. She sent "a week, or more" up the water from Millrace with the miller's man before the eleven came down the notch. She is counted from the cairn in each log (one day on the 301st … five on the 305th).
88:- **The hold south of the cairn (47:205–233).** Four marks at its gate for two new water-skins, meal, a cheese and corn for the mule: **123**. Teague's four buy their own (a side of bacon). The keeper had skins filled and priced since the day before: a man on a bay rode through two days earlier, bought nothing, gave no name, and said that a crew would want them.
89:- **The Stair's bowl on the 299th (47:35–49; 47:53–81; 47:131).** Karis's grey stone is gone from between the two irons and a square of turf has been laid in its place with a spade, since the thaw; Seln: "There is one to a year." She sets another stone. Her number: the dead surveyor's *North station to floor*, nine lengths and a piece from the old iron to the grey's lip, which is also on the Stair's sheet in each of six years; laid with the kit's own cord from Vastin's case, by Karis, Lira and Seln, in an hour and ten minutes; "I am not checking the place; I am checking the cord." Cael does not touch it (a hundred and thirty-one days). Teague's three hold the rim unasked, the tall man on the spot where he stood in Book 7.
90:- **Teague's two lessons.** The brow (47:83–127): "A piece of ground's a sum … you put down what a man needs, and it comes out as a place"; the bow goes on the short walk and the legs go far; the captain stands where a voice carries to the other three. The dead hold (48:65–97): he sits down beside Cael a second time; "Tell me what I've seen as well"; **three things worth a fist** — a thing out of its place; a thing stopped that ought to be moving; a thing moving that ought to be stopped. Cael gives him "Noted" there (48:93).
91:- **The boy and Lira (47:153–181).** He has had a sigil since he was fourteen and has never been without it; carters say that in such a place "it's like being a child again"; her account: the last step of a stair in the dark; it has never come back short; "Don't find out on a morning when somebody's shooting at you." On the first dry night he wrings the cloth for Teague's wrist out of his own cup of water and goes to his blanket dry (told by Lira, 49:15–19).
92:- **Vastin on the leg.** He finds for himself how a stick keeps a file (47:11–13). At the hall's rise: "I have put my name to a good many papers which said that such a thing could not be … I am glad to have been spared the sight until I had left off signing" (47:265–271). His stick and the older healer's staff fall into one beat (48:23). At noon on the 303rd he says before he is asked that he will not stand a wall on day twenty-four, and that he does not know what he is for without it (49:33–49); Lira: "You can stand."
93:- **The country east of the marsh (48:5; 48:9–39).** Every beck goes into the ground within a mile. Both living holds on the east road have their gates barred at noon and no head on the walk; the second lets down one bucket for the mule on a rope, for a copper. The dead hold's well is ten feet of steining on dry rubble: "That's what kills them, out here."
94:- **The seep (48:131–143; 48:147–209; 48:213–285; 48:289–311).** A fold in the ridge's southern flank; a band of dark shale across it; a weeping ledge and a trodden pan under the band's western end; nineteen shale-backs, four of them calves. Teague: they lick the wet at first light and at dusk and mow along the band between; a Force shifted a block under a ledge herd on the cold road in Pike's time and "they found two mules" (this is Book 7's tale, with Teague now saying that he has stood where it was done). His rule for the fold: in by the west along the band's foot, never on the fall of the hill under a beast, two at a time, three steps and a stand, nobody steps quick near a calf. A skin takes more than six minutes at the drip. **The tall man stops the boy with a flat hand on the breastbone (48:197–209).** The read finds a bucket of water behind a loose leaf of shale; Pressure is wanted and refused; Brom draws the leaf with both arms; the tall man lifts his free hand from his knee and brings it down once. The old cow stands over them for the last skins. The mule drinks four skins from Oryn's canvas; the tall man and then the boy fetch four more at dusk; the bow takes one skin out of the boy's hand. Seln finds a keg's hoop and five made heels at the pan, the newest a week old: "a posting sends for its water."
95:- **The name (49:77–83).** Teague has had it from three carters on a salt road two days north that looks down into the place from far off: a hole where the country's water goes, and a span over it, sunk in the dish. "The Sunken Span." He calls the hole "the sink".
96:- **The fourth place's ground (49:85–97; 49:127–145; 50:3–9; 50:155; 50:231; 51:203–207; 51:261–267).** A dry ravine comes down from the north; for a hundred yards it runs along the outside of the fence, and the level strip between its lip and the irons is the shelf (a cart wide; the drop three men deep on the left hand going east; the irons on the right; the line a stride past them). Two squared timbers on trestles at the shelf's narrowest, forty yards in from its mouth; behind them five strides of width, a hut of turf and stone with a blanket for a door, a fire, a keg, a hide weighted with stones; at the east end the ravine bends inward and its lip comes to within a stride and a half of the last bar; beyond that gap a trodden path a pace wide goes on round the fence. The ravine cuts the fence there (the bars stop on one lip and begin on the other, twelve yards off) and runs to the middle. **The Span:** two piers of the grey rising out of the ravine's rock walls, with no course and no join, and an arch in one piece; on its back the grey comes up out of the turf a cart wide, with no kerb and no step, rises to a crown and goes down into the grass again; no wheel has marked it. Twenty yards beyond the arch the ravine's bed ends in a round hole as wide as a cart is long, with no bottom to be seen.
97:- **The watch (49:95–111; 49:223–255; 50:9–27; 50:131–135; 50:219–225; 50:293–299).** Seven, hooded, faces tied even at night among themselves: two crossbows, four shod staves, one empty-handed practitioner whose Path is not named; he makes a point of white light the size of a fist and hangs it over the shelf, and calls, "This ground is posted. Stand where you are, and declare." He keeps the ravine's side of the shelf always and never comes within three yards of the bars. One of the seven has a wet cough; one walks the path two hundred paces east and back. Leaving, they take the broken bow and leave the sound one on the baulk, with a stave and their fire.
98:- **Seln's reading of the iron (49:187–219).** Three ages in the fence: most bars scaled like the Stair's old stake; here and there a newer one driven into an old hole; and every thirty or forty paces a thicker bar under a heavier mallet's head, whose scale "had scaled again". All three kinds carry thirty-four with the stroke and bar. The path is a groove a hand deep in the shale. He thinks of the sheets that a posting of seven needs, and of "that house's hands"; the case is on his breastbone, shut; nothing about it is altered. "That made eight, with himself, for fifteen years."
99:- **The fight's choreography inside the plan's four exchanges (50).** Positions: Seln in the grass above the path sixty yards east of the gap; the healers and the mule at the old ruts; nine at the shelf's mouth with Brom in front. *One:* two bolts on Iron under a buttoned coat — the breastbone and the left forearm; the neck and hands go "the colour of a slate that is drying after rain"; no sound. *Two:* Teague walks, since the Force wants three breaths of stillness; Cael keeps his left with the shortened watchman's stave from the alley (Seln's, handed over behind the brow) and takes the third stave across the left collar-bone without giving the stride; the tall man keeps the right with the ash crosswise and the steel high; both timbers and their trestles go a body's length back in one piece; five hoods go down; a crossbow's stock breaks under the lower baulk; nobody is under a timber; the wrist clicks and then the elbow. *Three:* the watch reforms behind the timbers where they lie, one bow spanned, the light relit; Teague cannot shut his right hand; he sees the width past the iron and asks Karis whether it is ground. Cael over first ("Grass"); Lira a stride in and a stride back ("Gone." / "Back. All of it." — "It's the same"); Karis; the boy when Teague looks at him; Vastin ("I am forbidden a wall. I am not, I think, forbidden to stand."). Sixty strides, two paces inside the iron; the watch's bow cannot turn without giving its side to Brom, the spear and the broad man's crossbow; the empty-handed man hauls back by the collar the one stave who starts for the iron. *The pinch:* eleven counts; at four the watch's bowman looses at the broad man, the bolt takes his crossbow in the stock, his hip goes and a yard of rotten lip goes with it; Teague checks for half a stride and does not turn; the boy looks at Lira's boots and stays; at eleven the first stave is dropped and seven go through the gap singly. Seln counts seven through the gap and again over the second rise, a mile on (51:11).
100:- **How the thirteenth comes (50:117–129).** Up through his own heels, out of the same stone, to the right hand, where it finds nothing to be laid against; "It lay along him from the heel to the palm, as a prop lies under a beam before the wedges are driven. Then it listened." It does not ask. It attends to whatever braced thing stands in front of him (the timbers; the ravine's lip; the grey of the Span's crown; a slab on the shale), and the old three ribs, low on the left, ache where its road would leave him.
101:- **Costs carried forward.** The broad man's right shin (the good leg), broken in the ravine's bed; drawn straight on the older healer's board, knit by Oryn in a quarter of an hour, bound to the board in four turns of webbing; he walks on a trestle's leg from the 305th. Teague's right arm, wrist and elbow, in linen to the shoulder on a slat; he cannot feel the fingers. Brom: a round bruise the size of a saucer on the breastbone and a ridged one along the left forearm; Oryn will not spend on them ("You'll have a week of colours"). Cael: a welt across the left collar-bone, "a bruise on a bone", ten days. A crossbow with a bolt standing in its split stock.
102:- **After the fall (50:307–367; 51:3–75).** The boy runs when Teague lifts two fingers and holds the broad man's head off the stones. Oryn asks; a nod; "I don't go in on a nod. I'll have it in a word."; the word is given and does not carry up the rock. The two healers nod, once each, across the leg. Vastin, coming out over the iron: "It is all there … To the grain." Brom carries the broad man's pack as the contract's second head ("I can carry what was on his back") and leaves three coppers of his own on the watch's keg for a skin of water. Cael to Lira: "Thirteenth. His. A line." / "It listens. I'd have liked it better if it asked." The tier in the fragment's lines is the thing that he once declined to read off that wrist at a marsh's edge.
103:- **Teague's window (51:79–175).** He counts three by breathing and the spear by a shadow at the gap. The elbow has gone once before, "a long while ago, on a ledge". The bow to him: "And you never turned your head." / "If you'd turned, I'd have had something to say to you from the bottom of that hole"; "It's the good one"; of Oryn, "She asked me first. With my leg lying two ways." The boy asleep sitting, the dry cloth in his fist. Teague's reckoning: the boy "would go wherever he was looked at, a half-beat before he had seen what was there", and held at the grass by a girl's boots; "He would have to think about whose boots a boy watched." Of Cael: a left side kept with a stick and nothing else; "Teague could put that in a column." **Under the hide:** a sack and a half of meal, a bundle of bolts with no thread on their feathers, a mallet, a tarred cord knotted every few feet, and a dozen new bars, never driven, each stamped with two figures and the stroke and bar. He does not read it further; "It was not his to read."
104:- **Noon at the Span (51:179–241).** Oryn at the irons, two fingers on Brom's wrist, asked first: "Four. The same." She will not be told how many "across a row of iron". Six go down: Karis, Lira, Brom, Seln, Vastin, Cael. The twelfth leans at the whole of the thing and is refused by counting bootlaces. On the crown: nothing; the thirteenth listens to the grey; the ribs.
105:- **The notch (51:245–259).** Lira sees it from the crown: a gap low in the second ridge, square; "The brass goes through it. I'd lay my knife." Seln looks at it longer than either.
106:- **The piers' inner faces (51:261–327).** Reached from the ravine's bed by a rib of rock on the western side; five courses to the height of a man, in the floors' hand, unworn; Karis draws nine sheets in two hours on a board of Brom's, with a tick where each run ends. **The lowest course of the west pier has fifty-two signs; Cael counts fifty-one, twice, and fifty-two with Lira's finger beside his** (the Book 11 plant; ruling *hold*, 51:351). The twelfth takes that course for a breath: *Goes*, eastward, out of the face toward the other pier. Karis can turn one group in the second course (marker 8). She keeps one of the new bars from under the hide, by Teague's leave (51:333).
107:- **The 304th's log (51:335–353).** After the fixed text Cael sees that he has written *tomorrow* for three days and lets it stand: "It was not a true count; it was the truth about how near the thing had come."
108:- **The 305th's road (52:3–37).** The tall man cuts the broad man's stick out of the watch's trestle. The file goes round the outside of the iron by the trodden path; from the far side the road on the Span's back lies end-on and the same line runs out to the gap. A captain's pace "that a child could have kept"; two sticks at the back that stop within a breath of one another and never look round. Stepped shale with a thin red moss and no water. Camp in the middle of the afternoon under a step a man's head high, with forty yards of flat before it and the gap four or five miles off. Seln goes on alone.
109:- **The blanket (52:41–101).** Brom's blanket, twenty yards upwind of the fire. In the middle the hide, turned until its pencil line runs at the gap, the Ternhall brass upon it; one sheet from the Fallow, three from the Stair, one from the hall and the nine of the piers, each turned to lie as its wall stood (she had noted on every sheet which wall and which way it faced); the flat at the head with its six rows; her notebook at the list, Cleon's roll and the sheet of three boxes at the foot; the fair copy from Cael's coat at one corner, folded, under a pebble, not opened. **The lean:** kneeling to hold a corner, Cael has the twelfth take all fourteen sheets in a breath: *Goes*, every run, one way, along the brass, east. He tells Karis, Lira and Brom (Vastin is at the fire). Karis enters it as his, as one unchecked glance, and will not ask for a second: "whoever cut them cut each one to go on to the next … I have only his word for it."
110:- **The corner (52:105–157).** The two signs in the corner of the two-bar sheets are two of the twenty-three letters: the first letters of a year's number-words, as the old town's clerks headed a page when short of room; an office's clerk has copied them forward "as he would copy a blot". They are the same two letters as the year at the head of the skin; "the same hundred"; the figure is to be said once, at night, when the reduction is done. **The thumbnail ring** goes round the prick; "I shall ink that when I have had a cord round it." Vastin reads the oldest row standing ("I have never read a paper standing that I did not afterward wish I had read sitting").
111:- **The lamps finding's ground (52:161–221).** A ruled table kept since the Fallow: who crossed, what each carries, of what rank, and the stride; three ranks of Iron, a Copper, a Bronze, and since the 304th the boy and "a man who signed for other people's for forty years"; she has asked six people beside herself. The same stride for every one, whole, no dimming, back whole. After Vastin's stopped sentence and her rule she tears the ruled leaf out and carries it the length of the blanket to the corner farthest from the folded paper: "Two pages … Whoever wants a line from the one to the other will have to find something to rule it with." **Cael's third page** (52:213–221): the technician's sheet lies at the foot of the second row; he does not know to which corner it would lie the nearer, and keeps it off the wool. Nothing is said or written.
112:- **The two slips (52:225–285).** Folded small and pinned under the knot of the oldest row's tape; a wafer of red wax on the directive and a broader, nearly black one on the objection, with different impressions. Vastin knows both seals at a glance and will not name them ("I should have drafted nine. I can feel all of them."). Karis pins them back where they were and enters the day, the place, and "read aloud and understood in part".
113:- **One fire (53:3–71).** Wood for one, carried by the boy from the watch's stack; seven bowls and five. Vastin moves the foot of his stick a hand to the left: "I am informed that the ground falls away on that side." The two sticks stand in one crack. **The healers' third exchange:** the older woman holds two turns of her own webbing apart and Oryn's hands go in under them. Teague to Cael: "You kept my left … I never had the place behind my shoulder stay so full."
114:- **Force, dry (53:75–151).** Behind the step's corner: the right heel on a rib of rock, the right palm on a fallen slab, a body's length apart; the line found by moving the hip a finger's breadth; the thing "standing, as a prop stands when the weight has come onto it"; the ribs give "one clear line of warning … such as a cracked board gives". Three reasons for not letting it go: never fired, and a camp forty yards off; the ribs unread for this; its owner round the corner, not told. Lira: "You looked like him." Brom has the read and the thirteenth said at last: "you'll pay it when it can be paid, and not a day sooner."
115:- **Karis's night (53:155–229).** The reduction leaves eleven years in one hundred, of which the skin's year is the fourth; "*not shown to be the same year; shown to be one of eleven, of which that year is another.*" Being believed is heavier than not being believed. The ruled leaf and her copy of the skin lie on the lid with a hand's breadth of bare wood between; she knows that a sentence exists and that it is six words long, as she once knew the length of a word under a glove; she covers the wood with her bound hand, does not say it, and writes: "*Second page: nothing entered. Not for want of a pencil.*"
116:- **Seln on the gap (53:233–261).** A gap in a ridge, square because both walls are rock; a dry bed going in; the only way through for five miles on either hand; he lay a mile off until dark and saw no fire, no person and no heel-mark. "A watch that I can count is a watch that I can plan for." Teague will not move a stick until he has looked again from nearer at first light.
117:- **Roles (all unnamed):** the keeper of the hold south of the cairn; the man on a bay; the people of the two barred holds; the seven of the watch (the empty-handed man; the two bowmen; the four staves, one with a cough); Teague's tall man, bow (the broad man), boy (the young one) and old woman; the miller's man (spoken of).
119:## State at movement end (for the ledger)
149:## Owner flags
151:1. **The cairn against Book 7 (47:197–201; 48:109–117).** The plan puts Oryn's north-turning cairn at noon on the 300th, before the hall's marsh; the edition's Book 7 has one north-going fork on this line, the heap of chock-stones, a day PAST the marsh. I kept the ruling and minted a second place (a built cairn and a mule-path at the bend where the carters' road swings south to a hold), and she passes the chock-stones on the 302nd without looking up. Is a second north road acceptable, or should the cairn BE the chock-stones (which would move it to the 302nd and change her count)?
152:2. **The leg's intervals (47:253).** Stair to hall in a day and a half, against Book 7's two days and a morning; the page gives the reason (no surveying; a quiet man who knows where the track comes out). Hall to brow is three days, as in Book 7. Does that hold?
153:3. **Ten markers** (table above). Four carry choices that change a scene's sense: 49:169 (whose term "Nobody declares…" is; the scene and the 303rd's log are staged as Cael's); 50:245 (who answers "We've been in three times"); 51:21 (who warns Teague about the arm; staged as Oryn from a distance, since the older healer may not speak before the six); 53:291 (the close's alternation).
154:4. **What "nobody declares" is on the page (50:19–45; 50:99–105).** I have read it by Seln's Book 7 definition ("a crack, a light"): nothing is called by its name, nothing is thrown at a person, nothing answers the watch's light. Brom's Iron (silent, under a coat) and Teague's Force (silent, on a timber) are both used outside the line. Is that the sense the plan means, or should even those be withheld?
155:5. **Deployment thirty-three (48:247–251; 48:327).** I added one use in earnest, the read at the seep, on the precedent of the calf at the fence (deployment twenty-eight). If the count must stay at thirty-two through this movement, the seam of water can be found by Brom's hands alone and four log lines change (48:327, 49:271, 51:349, 53:273).
156:6. **The purse reaches 123 on the 300th, not the 304th (47:227–229).** Four marks at a hold's gate for skins and victual; nothing is bought at the Span. The plan's calendar shows 123 against the 304th. Is the earlier day acceptable?
157:7. **The S19 log's "Tomorrow" (51:339; 51:343).** The fixed text says "Tomorrow I find out what standing in the middle of the first one feels like", written on the 304th; this edition's office distance is three days. I printed it whole and had Cael notice the word and let it stand as "the truth about how near the thing had come". Is that patch wanted, or should the two sentences after the log go?
158:8. **Whose the watch is (49:95–111; 49:213–217; 51:339).** Cael's fixed log says that the Compact fenced the place "and left men on it". On the page nobody but that log says whose the seven are; Seln thinks of the sheets such a posting needs and of "that house's hands". Too much, or too little, for a faction that must stay unnamed and unlocated?
159:9. **A rider on a bay east of the river (47:231).** Movement 7's ruling has nothing seen east of the river as of the 299th. The hold's keeper reports, on the 300th, a man on a bay who went through two days earlier and foretold a crew; and two holds are barred on the 301st. No face, no name. Does a report at second hand breach that ruling?
160:10. **The Span's ground (49:85–97; 51:203–207; 51:261–267).** The ravine ("the sink"), the shelf, the pinch, the arch with a road on its back, the hole twenty yards beyond it, and the name from Teague's carters are mine, built to §8's left-and-right. The Book 7 valley is "open toward the south"; I have put the ravine on the north. Anything here that the pass or the court needs otherwise?
161:11. **The watch's man (50:15–27).** His Path is unnamed and is shown only as a hung point of white light; his call ("This ground is posted. Stand where you are, and declare.") is mine. Is a spoken demand to declare right for this edition?
162:12. **Who is inside, and for how long (50:181–211; 50:235–299).** Five, for about three minutes; nothing opened; four of them without what they carry for the whole of it. **Vastin's sigil goes out for the first time** (50:209; 50:315–319: "It is all there … To the grain."). Does that spend something that the court's line (his fixed speech on the 307th) needs?
163:13. **The young one (47:153–181; 48:197–209; 49:15–27; 50:195–203; 50:271–277; 51:123–141).** His water given to a cloth; the hand at the seep; "Four"; holding at the grass by Lira's boots; asleep with the cloth; and Teague's "He would have to think about whose boots a boy watched." Is the last line, or "he would go wherever he was looked at", too plain a finger at the pass?
164:14. **The broad man's leg and his words (50:261; 50:325; 50:337–343; 51:91–119; 53:33; 53:47).** It is the right shin, the good leg. His consent to Oryn is "in a word" that does not carry to Cael; in Teague's window he speaks to Teague at some length. His first word to the six, "Aye", is said to Vastin's remark about the ground, and a second "Aye" to Oryn's "May I?". Does the unheard word on the 304th sit inside the lock?
165:15. **Teague is thanked and not told; Oryn is not told either (51:55–75; 51:195; 53:63–71; 53:139–147).** Lira and Brom know the thirteenth. Oryn refuses the count "across a row of iron" and has not been given it by the movement's end, though she is owed the ribs. Movement 9 has her knowing thirteen; should she be told on the 305th?
166:16. **The tier in the fragment (51:71–75).** Cael marks that the "Bronze" in his own cataloguing is the thing he once declined to read off Teague's wrist (Book 7 chapter 56). Is that connection wanted?
167:17. **The row that counts short (51:279–309; 51:351).** Fifty-two signs counted as fifty-one, twice, and right with Lira beside him; Karis remembers the inn yard and says nothing. Deniable enough?
168:18. **"Through" as a word on a pier, and *Goes* (51:311–327).** I have staged the plan's quoted word as the one group that Karis can turn on the west pier, and given the twelfth a breath of that course (*Goes*, eastward) just before. If "Through" is somebody's remark about the line, the lead-in at 51:317–321 changes.
169:19. **How "the rows lean along the bearing" is known (52:71–101).** By the twelfth's one unbidden glance over fourteen sheets laid as their walls stood; told to Karis, Lira and Brom; entered by Karis as Cael's and unchecked; not counted as a deployment. Karis's sentence "whoever cut them cut each one to go on to the next" is the furthest that anybody goes. Too far?
170:20. **The corner signs and the figure (52:117–145; 53:159–173).** I have made the two unread signs the first letters of a year in the old town's count (a clerk's short form), the same two letters as the year at the skin's head, with eleven candidate years in one hundred. That is what earns "documented four hundred years back … or near it" in the close. It lays the fifth place's series beside the leaf's date (not beside the lamps). Is that join the plan's, and is the mechanism acceptable?
171:21. **The lamps finding's ground (52:165–181).** A table of crossings kept since the Fallow; seven people; "a man who signed for other people's for forty years". Karis says "sir" to Vastin. No tier is given for him.
172:22. **The two slips (52:225–249).** Pinned under the knot of the oldest row's tape, with a red wafer and a broader dark one. Vastin had carried the order "in my own hand the whole time, under a knot". The rows were counted and re-taped at the spring in Movement 3 without anybody remarking them; the page has Vastin say that he took the folded slips for a clerk's tag. Does finding them inside the flat agree with what Movements 2 and 3 say was looked for and not found?
173:23. **Karis's night (53:189–229).** Her interior says that a sentence exists, that it is six words long, and that she will not say or write it; nothing in it states the sentence or lays one page on the other. Her entry, "Second page: nothing entered. Not for want of a pencil.", is in a notebook that others may read. Inside the limit?
174:24. **Seln's three ages of iron (49:195–205) and the dozen new bars under the hide (51:159–169; 51:333).** The oldest bars are older than he can put a figure to; new ones are stocked at the post; Karis keeps one. Does a visibly maintained fence say more about a present keeper than §11 allows ("every site is old and stable")?
175:25. **The stone at the Stair (47:35–49).** Somebody has removed Karis's marker and laid a turf since the thaw, "to make it agree with his sheet". A yearly visit by the office is implied. Acceptable?
176:26. **Shale-backs at the seep (48:151–183).** Teague's account of their day (licking at first light and dusk; mowing between) and his rule of approach are mine, laid on Book 7's two-part carters' rule and Karis's derived leaf. The mule drinks four skins.
177:27. **Teague's teaching and his thanks (47:83–127; 48:65–97; 53:63–67).** "A piece of ground's a sum"; the three things worth a fist; "I never had the place behind my shoulder stay so full." Cael says "Noted" to him on the 301st (48:93), two days before Teague's own first.
178:28. **Vastin's refusal entered beforehand (49:33–49) and his step over the iron (50:205–209).** He says on the 303rd that he will not stand a wall; on the 304th he crosses on his own motion. Oryn's terms forbid kneeling, running and a wall, and say nothing of standing in a file.
179:29. **Force, dry (53:75–121).** Right heel and right palm, as Teague sets it; the ribs are low on the LEFT (Book 7 chapter 55), and the page says that the road goes "through the cage" and not up one side. Movement 9's "the recoil stops in the ribs" should be checked against that.
180:30. **Seln on the gap (53:233–261).** He sees nothing at all, and that is what he dislikes; the rig, the shelf and the count are left wholly to the 306th.
181:31. **One fire on the 305th (53:3–7).** Two fires on every other night of the leg, by the contract; one here, for want of wood, in two halves.
182:32. **Length.** 36,996 words of mine, at the top of the brief's allowance; the restorations will take it a little past 37,000.
