books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-20.md:13:The carter's sheet, with the four circles and the pencil line through them and the blank sheet pinned to its edge with the line running on across it to the divider's point. The kit's ledger, open at the index. The Compact's records in their rows — five rows now, because the Span's sheets had a row of their own, and the sheets that had never been laid out at a spring had a row too, at the end, that she had not read aloud to anyone. The fifty-two sheets of copied walls from the autumn, rolled, and the Span's piers copied that morning, flat, weighted at the corners with shale. The leaf's copy, in her hand and his, with the gap drawn. The list of parts. The dividers, closed. The two brass edges, side by side along the line.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-20.md:55:"They fail the way a hundred lamps fail when one wire's cut. Not the way a hundred souls fall silent."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-20.md:89:"A directive. Not a survey; an order about the surveys. Six years back, at the head of the file, in the office's ruling hand — the hand that writes the forms and not the hand that fills them." He read it in the register he had once read everything in, which was the register of a man who had been read to all his life by paper and had read back. "*Findings under the fifth column to be held. Not for onward transmission. Not for citation. Not for the seat.* A seal. A rank I'd have saluted, once." He turned the sheet. "And on the back — " He stopped.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-20.md:93:"A second hand." Vastin turned the sheet to the fire so that the light went through it and the two hands lay one over the other in the paper. "Filed under it. Same date, or near. *Objection entered: the holding of a finding is not the finding's disproof. Refer to the seat.* A different seal. A rank I'd have saluted the same." He put the sheet down on the row, face up, the order side, and looked at it as he had looked at a stake on a crest. "Somebody wanted this buried. Somebody else put it in writing that it shouldn't be. That's not a monolith. That's a crack."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-20.md:97:"Six years," Karis said. She had the deep-layer sheet under her hand, the one from the spring with the office's *six years* on it in the office's rounding, and she looked at it beside the directive and beside the index, and did the thing she did, which was to say what the papers said and not one word past it. "Six years back, somebody measured all of it, and graded it, and ordered the grades to be held. Somebody was administering this — the sites, the marks, all of it — recently enough to keep records, and to fight over the records, and to seal the fight. Documented as *stable*. Somebody's still writing that word down." She looked at the fire. "The Compact. That's who keeps records. That's who has a ruling hand and a seat and a seal. I'm not going to say *someone else*, because I've no sheet for someone else, and I've a whole file for them."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-20.md:99:"The Compact," Vastin said. He said it as a man says his own name in a room where it is no longer his. "Yes. Part of it."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:19:"They knew," Karis said, beside him, in the voice for a finding.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:21:"They've always known," Seln said. He had come to the crest's lip on his stomach, as he came to everything, and had the nearest stake in his eye from ninety paces, and read it as he read a lock. "That iron's been in the ground years. Not one winter. The scale's through it." He did not say how many years, because he did not have the number and would not make one. "The hut's a season. The fire's tonight. They've been here since the ice went out and they've been told to expect a crew off this crest, and they've been told how many, and it's the wrong number."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:27:Teague looked at it a while longer. Then he said, to Cael and to nobody: "Drawn?"
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:39:"Somebody does." Cael looked at the barricade, and at the man's wrapped wrist on the turf beside him, and did not look at the wrist a second time, because the man had priced it and it was his. "Nobody declares. Nobody dies. They're seven people on a posting. Noted?"
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:81:Nothing was gained inside. He wanted that entered before he did it, and he entered it, standing on the shelf with a crossbow spanned thirty paces off: the ones who went over would gain nothing over the iron. Lira's speed would be a woman's. Vastin's wall would not come. Every one of them would be, a stride past the iron, exactly what the seven would be a stride past it, which was bodies and steel and the decision to stand there; and the only difference on the whole shelf would be that they had stood there before, three times, and had decided it three times, and the seven had been told about it and had not. What the inside gave was not strength. It was *width*. A shelf was a cart's width, and a line across it was seven; a line that went two strides past the iron was wider than the shelf, and a line wider than the shelf was a thing seven people on a shelf could not face and could not get round.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:83:"By count," he said. Not loud. "Five over the iron, two strides in, level with the four on the shelf. Then we walk. Nobody strikes. Nobody stops walking."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:107:At eleven seconds the hooded man in the middle said, across the ten paces, not loud, "You'll die in there."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:109:"We've been in three times," Cael said.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:113:The crews had removed the watch's ability to hold the approach by making the only safe retreat a line the watch would not cross and the crews would. He wrote that sentence that night as it was, because a fight that can be said in one sentence is a fight that was won by a decision, and the decision had been the seven's as much as his.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:129:Teague's arm was the other price. He had it under his coat by the time Oryn came up the slope, and she said, "Arm," and he said, "Contract," and she said, "I don't read contracts. Arm," and read it — the wrist, the elbow, the shoulder, along the line — and said, "You've done that twice in a fortnight. A third time on that arm and I'll set it in a splint. That's not a healer's opinion. That's a fact about a line." Teague said, "Noted," and she said, "Everybody says that now," and wrapped the elbow properly.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:155:*Twelfth—no, count it again—thirteenth. Wind, Pressure, Iron, Compression, Ember, Shadow, Storm, Anchor, Blade, Tide, Iron Wall, Rune, Force. Thirteen things that aren't a Path, and I got three of them in one city and one bridge. Rate's climbing and I'm writing that down because Hesk taught me to note the trend. The Span's the fourth site and the Compact fenced it, years ago by the scale on the iron, and left men on it. They knew. They've always known. The bearing runs through it and keeps going, oldest-last, and the oldest one's out there in the deep edge, quiet since they built the machine. One more site. I've wanted to stand in the middle of it since Karis said *someone made this.* Tomorrow I find out what standing in the middle of the first one feels like.*

exec
