    67	"I got them." He looked at the fire. "I'll say it once, since I'd sooner you had it from me than guessed at it. I've stood at a thing with men behind me who'd done a deal more than you did yesterday. I never had the place behind my shoulder stay so full."
    68	
    69	He got up before Cael could find an answer, and went back to his own side.
    70	
    71	Cael sat on with his bowl. He had been thanked by a man from whom he had taken something a stride away, at that very timber, in those very breaths; and the man did not know it, and could not be told. He found that the bruise on his collar-bone was a good deal easier to carry.
    72	
    73	---
    74	
    75	He went round the end of the step when the bowls were done, where the fire could not see him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:163:There were eleven left. They lay together, in a row, in the one hundred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:165:She could not choose among the eleven. She had known before she began that she would not be able to; a year that is written by its first letters is a year with a door left open in it. She put down all eleven, and ruled a line beneath them, and laid beside the row the one year that she had in full, from the head of a certain skin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:171:It was not proof that the two were one year. She wrote that down first, so that it should stand above everything else: *not shown to be the same year; shown to be one of eleven, of which that year is another.* A clerk in an office had copied two marks forward, from sheet to sheet, for longer than she could well think about, and had never known that he was copying a date. Whoever drew the first of those sheets had reckoned as the old town reckoned. He had drawn it within a few years of the day when a man ruled a leaf of skin and wrote upon it, in a fair hand, what he had done.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:175:She had wanted for five years to be able to say one such thing with a paper under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:215:A measurement was a fact about a ruler. She had two rulers on that lid. The one had been laid upon every crossing that she had seen, by seven people, and it told her how a thing went out and came back. The other was a man's own account of himself, a long while dead, with his name scraped off it by a hand that she could not identify. Neither had been laid on the other. There was nothing on that lid, or in three boxes, or in a courier's flat, that would serve to lay them so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:221:She wanted to. She let herself know that as well, since it was true, and since a refusal that costs nothing is only a habit. She had stood for five years on the wrong side of shut presses. She had never in her life been so near to a thing, with a pencil in reach.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:227:*Second page: nothing entered. Not for want of a pencil.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:273:*The thirteenth: lined, on a slab, behind the step. Not let go. The ribs would take it. A trial; not counted. Thirteen. Deployment since the Line: thirty-three.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:331:He wrote it by the watch's fire, at dusk, with eleven people about it and one in a hut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:349:*Nothing opened today: not on the shelf, and not over the iron. The read at the seep was the last. Deployment since the Line: thirty-three.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:303:Cael let the count go. It had been eleven. He had been kept waiting that long once before, standing on a plate, and he had never cared for the figure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:5:He did it on the three hundred and fifth day, as soon as there was light, with the hand-axe from a captain's belt, while the rest were lashing loads. He took one leg off the frame that had held two timbers, and trimmed the foot of it square, and bound a pad of rag over the head. Then he carried it into the hut and stood it against the wall by the bracken, and came out again, and nothing was said by anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:145:"I am not going to tell you that it is the same year, since two years may begin alike; I shall work the reduction through tonight, by myself, with the tally-books' pattern, and I shall say the figure once when I have it. I can tell you now that it is the same hundred." Her voice was small and quite steady. "The office wrote down that it is older than two hundred and six, by leaving the figure out. It did not know by how much. I think that I do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:3:The three hundred and third day was the clearest that they had had since the ford, and Cael walked the whole of it toward a thing that he had already seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:9:There were twelve on it now. He could hear them behind him as he had not been able to hear four: the pannier, and the hooves, and two pieces of ash going down on the one beat. He found, somewhat to his surprise, that he liked the sound. It was a good deal harder to feel small in front of a thing with eleven pairs of boots at his back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:37:"Then there is a thing which I should like to have said before we see it, and before anybody is put to the trouble of asking me." He did not turn his head. "I have twenty-four of thirty. A captain has built a count upon what I carry, and I have no doubt that he would find a use for it tomorrow. I shall not stand it. I was told eleven days more, at a mill, by a person who was entitled to tell me; and I broke her word once, under a town, and learned the price."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:113:He turned round, and looked at the eleven of them, and at a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:271:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-three.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:3:They struck the east road an hour from the marsh, on the three hundred and first morning, and it was a different road from the one that Cael remembered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:105:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:115:There were eleven now, and two beasts' worth of load on one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:327:*Thirty-two, and that one. Deployment since the Line: thirty-three.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:11:It had a beat of its own: the ash went down, and the stiff leg came through, and the good one after it. He had listened to that beat down three hundred yards of gallery and up a stair; he had never before heard it keep a road. It was slower than the file. But at each flat hand, when eleven people stood, the stick made up its yards without hurry, and was a stride behind Brom again before two fingers went up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:47:"The next. There is one to a year."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:107:He went at it then as he went at a page. A crossbow had been spanned on that brow eleven times, and a twelfth half done; he had heard the count from a stair. A man who spans puts his boot in an iron and hauls, and he would not do that on a slope, or in soft turf, where the stirrup sank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:117:"The frost showed me their two shapes, and the print of the iron, eleven times in the one place. I'd have found them without. A piece of ground's a sum, the same as a load; you put down what a man needs, and it comes out as a place." He turned his head toward the rim. "You do sums. Do me this one. I've three and myself, and that whole dish to keep. Where?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:191:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:201:Teague's hand came up flat, and eleven people stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:237:"I shut that shoulder on the two hundred and ninety-eighth. A mend that deep wants a week of somebody's eye, and I've said in a yard what I think of a healer who spends herself on a thing and walks off while it's deciding. That's the three hundred and fifth." Her hand had come up, and a thumb went down. "There's a knee behind it that's on my linen and twenty-one days old. And there's an edge at the end of that brass that's twice any I've stood at, with ten of you going at it. I said in a loft where somebody ought to be."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:251:They had walked it hard. A tree as thick as Brom's leg stood in one rut, and it had put on a hand of girth; there were the pools on either side, black and still, and the silver-backed leaves coming. Five people and a healer had taken a day and a morning over that track once, in a strip a mile wide, with a hide held open and a man sent a furlong ahead to be sighted on. Twelve went along it now between noon and dusk, since eleven of them had only to follow a quiet man who knew where it came out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:301:*The cairn at noon. Her road went north there. She looked up it once, and walked on east. Brom's shoulder has until the three hundred and fifth; that is hers to count, and she has counted it. I did not ask.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:307:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*

