+
+The apparent Book 7 pace discrepancy is not a calendar contradiction. Closed Book 7 took a surveying party a day and a morning over the Stair-to-Hall track; this return takes noon-to-dusk because Seln knows the outlet and nobody surveys (`chapter-47.md:249–253`; locked Book 7 `chapter-53.md`, route account). BOOK_MAP §6 and §12 C3/C14 independently fix the cairn and Hall on the 300th. Hall-to-crest remains three days, matching the earlier traversal.
+
+No printed day count is wrong. The hold report of a bay two days earlier (`chapter-47.md:225–231`) is second-hand and does not move any party's calendar. The protected S19 “Tomorrow” is knowingly irreconcilable with the office distance, but protected text governs; the draft must not gloss it (`chapter-51.md:339–343`; fix 19).
+
+## 4. Standing defaults, names and Reader Standard
+
+- **Calendar language:** no month name, English weekday or named season appears. “Spring” is used only for water. “Cold term,” “thaw,” “frost” and “ice” are season-blind weather or duration language (`chapter-47.md:43,59`; `chapter-49.md:101`). The reconciled Seln speech correctly replaces the source's “winter” (`source chapter-19.md:21`; draft `chapter-49.md:101`).
+- **Measures:** feet, yards, miles, paces, strides, cords, furlongs and body measures are used; no metric unit appears in the draft. The source's metres are not to be imported.
+- **Kindling/year count:** Cael is nineteen and no birthday or year count since the Kindling is stated. Karis's two “five years” lines (`chapter-53.md:175,221`) are unsupported academy-era spans and permit a reader to count backward; fixes 29 and 33 remove the numerals. Closed Books 5–8 give only a long effort/closed presses, not a fixed five-year duration.
+- **Path phrasing:** no narrative “his Path” is used for Cael. Spoken “my Path” in the protected S20 close is OWNER-pending #57 and must remain.
+- **Vastin:** no age is supplied. “Longer than any of the four … had been alive” (`chapter-50.md:209`) is relational, not an age.
+- **Reader Standard:** the major fight has real costs—bolts, bruises, a broken shin and a disabled arm—but no gore, no death, no cruelty admired, and medical consent is explicit (`chapter-50.md:325–367`; `chapter-51.md:15–37`). It is suitable for the stated thirteen-year-old reader standard.
+
+The draft creates no unplanned proper name for a person, place or animal. New common-role references remain unnamed: the south-hold keeper; the bay rider; people behind the two barred holds; the seven-person watch (empty-handed practitioner, two bowmen, four staves, one with a cough); Teague's tall man, broad man/bow, young one/boy and older healer; the miller's man; the brown mule. “The Sunken Span” is the planned title/place-name (`chapter-49.md:77–83`).
